      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>2 AVOI DB INDIGESTION MGDOUGALLS SELF-RAISING FLOUR FOR PASTRY.</head>
        <p>2 AVOI DB INDIGESTION MGDOUGALLS SELF-RAISING FLOUR FOR PASTRY.</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL M POULTRY FARMING. _</p>
        <p>Ei 111</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP].</p>
        <p>MINORCAS.</p>
        <p>Of all the Mediterranean breeds of p,-,ultty, the Minorcas are the best known, especially blacks White Minorcas are now rarely seen. These latter are one of the finest egg-producers extant. About 11 years ago they were great favourites with me, I then kept a grand strain, and what layers! They laid eggs quite as large as duck eggs and plenty of them. It does seem strange how these grand bleeds deteriorate in popularity. I am, however, glad to note that they are still bred by a few. Let 1.14 hope that one day we shall have them in our midst again as popular as ever.</p>
        <p>From the " Feat here. Troyta."</p>
        <p>BLANC MINORCA COCA. This week's picture represents a single-combed Black Minorca cock. There is another variety still, and are known as Rosecombed Black Alinorcas. In every respect—with the exception of comb—they are j almost an exact prototype of the above illustration. Minercas of whatever colour or tyre are renowned j as layers of large white-shelled eggs. And when the • conditions are compatible they are prolific egg pro- ducers. Pe:haps chiefly because of their colour, they have been adopted as the ideal town-dwellers' fowl. They stand confinement well, and if kept on a fairly dry and well-sheltered run, they give very satisfactory , results. When chosen for utility purposes, too much notice should not be taken of exhibttion type. Like many another breed of fowls, the black Minorca has suffered greatly at the bands of the showman. They have inbred to excess for head points, with the result that utility points have keen sacrificed. And those who intend adding Minorcas to their list, shculd avoid • purchasing stock from exhibition yards. It is claimed fir the rose-combed variety that they are better winter- layers than the single-combed variety. One reason is that the large single comb is subject to frostbite, more so than the roso-comb ; That being so, they stand the winter better and thus produce snore eggs when eggs are scarce. Another point in favour el the Minorcas—as a light breed—is their table qualities. They carry more flesh than the other Mediterranean varieties, and still further, the flesh is of a gcod quality. I have dressed Minorca cockerels that would have done justice for some of the more popular all-round breeds. They are very useful fowls, and very rightly they still maintain a well-earned reputation. Backyarders, here's the breed for you. FEEDING POULTRY. This is the most important branch in the work of poultry farming. Science has not yet proved the correct amount a fowl should eat per day. And if we are to be guided by nature, and if we take the wild birds as a criterion, what shall we say ? Because, from observation they seem like some children. They partake of one meal per day, and that invariably lasts all day. However, the great secret of success in feeding is not to be overfed. Fowls like human beings won't do much work for food, if they can obtain it withcut. Consequently they soon become too fat, and then they will not lay at all. Under natural conditions they would have to 'work—and work bard—for every scrap of fooa they got. Does this not prove that good health and tip-top condition must follow as a natural result, Hard work IS good for tha body, providing a bounteous supply of food is forthcoming from the labour involved. And still farther, a fowl under natural conditions, never takes more into the system than it requires. Artificially fed they often gorse, not knowing when the next meal may come, They are fed in batches and so they must needs scramble for their share. Under natural conditions, each one must search for its own and can take only what it finds. Now it is very necessary to observe all these points. And the two main issues that al ise from the foregoing argu- ments are : A plentiful supply, consistent with the requisite amount of exercise needed. A PLENTIFUL SUPPLY. This does not mean, that you must give large quantities of food, irrespective of the quality, or simply because it is the food which you are in the habit of using. Even now a gocd many people feed their fowls as though it didn't matter a bit so long as they got a meal of some sort, plenty of it perhaps, but often enough a mixture of two Tarts potatoe pairings and maize meal, with a feed of maize at night, for a change. Now this kind of feeding will never do, you cannot reasonably expect fowls to make profit when fed thus. Clive your fowls a stiff meal and what happens ? cff they go into the scratching shed, and stand huddled in a corner, quite content, never attempting to get about and work.' On the other hand, just give the stock sufficient to appease hunger and then see what happens? Into the field if there is one, if not, into the scratching shed and vtorking away to see what else they can find. The evening meal should be the most substantial meal of the day, because you Fee the fowls must rest at night and consequently they have plenty of time to digest their food. Throughout the day the stock should be kept active, at night they should always have a full meal of the best grains. In the matter of feeding we should do well to copy the Ch;nese. Pro- fessor Holloway tells us, "That the Chinese are perhaps the most successful poultry raisers in the world. They do not feed their fowls, but make them p'ck up their own food, each flock being kept on the move as sheep are on a range." I quite agree that this system would pay here, if adopted by farmers. But then we must cater for a system, which suits the suburban dweller and the backyarder as well, and where space is at a premium the question of right feeding is a difficult one. This now brings us to the question of SOFT FOOD Y. HARD FOOD.</p>
        <p>Where fowie—h-ave a free and unfettered grass run, -very little soft food is requited. On the other band, fowls in confinement need more, because they get less exercise, and thus bard fcod is not so easily digested as with the former. Fowls on a free range are easily catered for, there they get a plentiful supply of insect food and natural herbage, so that when they are fed with a good brand of sound grain,little else is needed. It is, therefore, important that soft food for fowls in confined runs,should contain some substitute for insect food. Cut green-bone and lean minced meat form a splendid substitute and should be given,at least, every other day. A good soft food mixture can he made from the following meal a : Two parts biscuit meal, one and a half parts middlings, and one part pea- meal. Providing you get the best biscuit very little meat is needed, but when you do add the meat always use the water the meat has been boiled in, for mixing the meals, thus the fowls will get all the nutriment from the meat. When mixing the soft food see that it is given in a crumbly state, and not too wet. A good handful per bird per meal is sufficient.</p>
        <p>GRAINS. The m-st wholesome grains for poultry are, wheat, oats, barley, white Canadian peas and maize, the latter only being given when the weather is severe, and when the fowls are going through moult. Buckwheat and hempseed are two other most useful grains, which should ha added in smaller quantities and given occasionally. Buckwheat is a great appetiser and Lempseed a wonderful and natural heat producer. And when given to fowls in ny ult. it is of. great assistance, in supplying oil for the glands of the new feathers.</p>
