      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>MIXED CORN.</head>
        <p>MIXED CORN.</p>
        <p>A good many people habitually purchase mixed corn thinking of course, that by so doing, they are follow- ing a proper method. That is so, providing that the mixture is composed of the right kind of grains, and in a good many instances I am sorry to say this is not the case. I am a firm believer in mixed corn, but I also believe in mixing one's own. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BEAUTIFUL WARRINGTON SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S BULB SHOW.</p>
        <p>DIRECTOR OF EDUCATION AND RECENT CRITICISMS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Drill Hall was bright with spring blooms on Saturday when the school children's section of the Beautiful Warrington Society held its annual show of bulbs. It is evident that, under the cheerful guidance of the teachers, the work of encouraging a love of the beautiful is making good progress amongst the juveniles and Saturday's show—the sixth—was the most creditable yet promoted by the section. About 650 bulbs bad been distributed at the schools and 304 were tended with such good effsct as to be presentable for show purposes. Taste- fully wranged in their respective classes, they made E. bright array of colour, and the attractiveness of the-display was heightened by a fine group of plants and flowers lent by the Corporaticn. A particularly interesting feature of the show was a collection of 310 paintings and drawings (from nature) of spring flowers and foliage. Without exception, they were artistic pieces of work, and showed that the children are being taught to use their faculties of observation as well as to draw and paint carefully and neatly. It is unfortunate that the parents do not take a greater interest in the show. While there was a gratifyingly large attendance of scholars, the adult visitors were comparatively few in number—which is somewhat surprising when one considers the wholly commendable nature of the work in which the society is engaged. The cponing ceremony was performed by Mrs. C. Jackson Holmes, a a member of the Education Committee. Mr J Moore Murray (Directcr ef Elucation) presided, and there were also on the platform :—Mrs A J Willett, Mr J. Longland, and Mr F II Longshaw (secretary). The SECRF.TARY read the following report from the judge (Mr .1 Ryan) :—" I cannot f.il to compliment the children cn their improved exhibits as compared with previous years. Some of the specimens would do credit to their elder. I hope visitors will respond to the call of the officers of tie Society for funds and to msist one of the bravest efforts of the pr. moters to improve the up-bringing of our youngsters." Mr</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
