      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>KING AND TIIE LONDON COUNTY COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>THE KING AND TIIE LONDON COUNTY COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday last his Majesty, accom- ing Westminster Bridge, on the 'south side panied by the Queen, the Prince of Wales, of the Thames. The building will be 750 ft. and Princess Mary, laid the foundation- long, with an average width of 310 ft., and stone of the new Hall for the London County consist of nine stories, the lowest two Council. The new Hall wil; occupy a site of which 'will be below the level of the road. nearly five and a-half acres in extent, adjcin- The estimated present cost is £1,412,000.</p>
        <p>Longshaw expressed the thanks of the society to the following :—Lieutenant-Colonel J B Fairclough for the free use of the ball ; Lady Greenell, for a donation ; Fulton-Smith, for providing the platform ; Mr Ray and members of the Stockton Heath Garden Society for musical programme ; Mr C Mawson (headmaster of the School of Art) for judging the paintings and draw- ings ; Warrington Corporation, for an exhibit of plants and flowers ' • teachers and friends who had subscrib- and given'their services The CHAIRMAN said this was a time when schools— and by schools be meant teachers, scholars, education committees and all connected with education—were passing through very severe criticism—criticism which he ventured to think at times was not based on a thorough knowledge of what was heing done or ' attempted to be done in the schools. That criticism largely partook of a destructive character and was of little help to thE se who were in one way or another trying to do their best. None of those connected with the schools objected to criticism. They welcomed it, if it was honest, and they would try to do their best to act upon it. But he would like to make an appeal for another kind of criticism—a criticism which, was constructive, which appreciated some of the many things which were done in the schools, and which realized the work done by the teachers and scholars— evidence of which they had around them at that moment. He considered that the show reflected great credit upon those teachers and scholars who had taken part in growing flowers for it. Personally, he knew that the members of the Education Committee who did know something of what was being done,appreciated the work of the teachers in that direction. They lad about 300 plants which had been returned out of 650 bought by the children. Scme might say that that was a poor result; but there were bulbs and bulbs. Some would grow and others would not; some arrived at the flower- ing stage one week and some the next. He had the authority of one of the best known horticulturists in Warrington for saying that that show was the best they had had. (Applause). In introduc- ing Mrs. Holmes, the Director said she was actively engaged not only in the work of the Education Committee but of other organizations with the object of helping those who lived in our town to improve their conditions and surroundings and to male Warrington a little brighter. (Applause). Mrs. JACKSON HOLIES said it was a simple yet wonderful way of making the home beautiful for the children to grow bulbs, and she hoped those who belonged to the society would persuade others to join. If they took more interest in making homes attractive, they would not c'esire to seek so much pleasure out- side. People went in too much for outside. amusements in ttr se days, instead of taking life a lit tie more seriously. Warrington was a very go-ahead place and she was proud of the town of her adoption in many respects. She wished the society every success and after praising the drawings from nature, she said she hoped those who had not got prizes this year, but whose work was commended, would still persevere and try to be successful next time. (Applause). Miss May \VEston then presented Mrs. Holmes with a handsome bouquet. Mr. :I. LONOL AND, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs Holmes, referred to her great interest in educational affairs. With regard to the exhibition, immediately he entered the hall he was convinced it was the finest display the school children's section had ever held. Some of the flowers would do credit to anyone and the daffodils were particularly choice. The show reflected great credit not only upon the boys and girls but upon the teachers who had doubtless helped them to bring -their plants to such perfection. (Applause). Mr. I. BOWEN seconded and the vote was warmly accorded. Mrs. HotmEs then presented silver medals with gold centres to the three girls whose exhibits were con- sidered the best in the show—Marie Lloyd, 'Millicent Hollingsworth and Marian Clare. The DIRECTOR of EDUCATION VCRS thanked for pre- siding on the proposition of Mr. T. WESTON seconded by Mr R LUNT. During the afterncon and evening the Stockton Heath Garden Society playedtenjoyable selections. The committee which had charge of the arrangements consisted of Misses Broomhead, Jewkee, Joynson,Jones, Gandy, Bennion and Plinaton, and Messrs Aforwin :and George, with Mr F H Longshaw as bon secretary. THE PRIZE LIST. BULBS. Medals for Three Best Exhibits in Show. Marie Lloyd, Millie Hollingworth and Marion Clare. Hyacinth (in soil) Second prize, Dora Holbrook ;</p>
        <p>ibedi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <p>Mr. John Frederick Price, the Manchester Ceunty Coroner, died very suddenly cn Sunday morning at his home, St. Aubyn, Ashton-on-Mersey. He was quitn well when he went to bed on Saturday night. Mr. Price *as the eldest son of the late Mr. John b rederick Price, whom he succeeded as Coroner about sixteen years ago, after acting 3s his father's deputy for a long time. Mr , Price was fifty years of age.</p>
