      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>PRESCRIPTION THAT IS ABSOLUTELY FREE. SOUNDS STRANGE BUT ITS TRUE.</head>
        <p>A PRESCRIPTION THAT IS ABSOLUTELY FREE. SOUNDS STRANGE BUT ITS TRUE.</p>
        <p>How interesting are the conversations one often hears in public conveyances. The following discussion is ?elated as between two workmen in a tramcar in a Midland town: -</p>
        <p>"I see Henry's back to work." "That can't be, as I went to see him only on Satur- day last, and his pains were dreadful. The doctor says he's got Sciatica or Neuritis or something like that, in its worst form, and may be in bed for months."</p>
        <p>"Then the doctor is wrong, as I saw him as I came from the works, and he told me he never felt better." "seeing is believing, but I can't'believe it till I see him. What did he do ? "</p>
        <p>" He told me that it was a free prescription that he cut from a paper, and as my missus is bad with Rheumatism I asked him for it. Here it is: Go to the nearest chemist's shop and get 60 grains of liephaldol in tablet form. Take two tablets at once, and then one every hour till pain is gone. He says that's what cured him, and if it don't cure the miscue he'll stand me a drink on Saturday night."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN METHODISM. --- QUARTERLY MEETING.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WESLEYAN METHODISM. --- QUARTERLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting was held at Bewsey-road on Thursday,tea was provided by the ladies of the Bewsey- road Church and the tastefulness and floral decorations of the tables won much admiration. Aftea tea the Rev W A Beckett presided over a large and representative meeting, including all the Circuit ministers, the Circuit stewards and representatives from almost every place in the Circuit. The CHAIRMAN presented the annual return of membership which showed an increase of twenty-two and a record number—eighty-eight—on trial. The Stewards financial statement was very favourable show- I lug that income more than net expenditure. All the I ministers were thanked for their services and re-invited I Ito remain another year. All accepted with the exception of Mr Hutchinson, who had been summoned by the President of the Conference to take up a position at Swinton, a sudden and uunexpected vacancy there call- ing for immediate tilling. Mr Hutchinson, who, during the short time he has teen in the Circuit, has made many friends, was heartily thanked for his strenuous work, and in replying bade farewell to the meeting, he expecting to leave next week. Mrs Weaver, Mr Holland and Mr Lawton,were elected as representatives with the circuit stewards to the synod. The Chairman reported that the various Chapel Trusts were in a healthy condition several of them having been quite recently renewed. The different Circuit and Connexional funds all showed an increase upon the previous year this being very marked in the cane of Foreign Missions. A large increase in members was reported in the Bands of Hope and the Temperance Committee was instructed to make arrangements for the visit of the Rev. H. Carter, the Connexional Temperance Secretary. Land having been secured in Norris-stret permiseion was sought from the meeting to erect a School Chapel. The subject was introduced I y Mr Upson, and the scheme was heartily supported by Messrs. Withenshaw, Dr. Bennett, Meldrum, Harris, Corradine, and the Chairman. Perrnission to build was unanimously given and the meeting Enthusiastically took upon itself the responsibility of raising by means of a Circuit Bazaar £l,OOO at least. A large and influential committee was appointed to make all necessary arrangements without delay. The next quarterly meeting will be held on June 1.1 at Penketh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>WIDNES BUDGET.</head>
        <p>WIDNES BUDGET.</p>
        <p>RATES INCREASED TWOPENCE. At the Widnes Town Council, on Tuesday, Alderman Owens submitted the municipal budget for the ensuing year. The amount of the borough rate last year was £15,127 ; the total estimate this year was £15,549, which, with a deficiency brought forward of £155, made the increase equivalent to Id. on the rates. There was alsa an increase on the general district fund of £747, which would be covered with the other penny increase Last sear the rates were 6s. 10d., this year is. The Health Committee had overspent to the extent of £6OO due to the increased amount of sickness.</p>
      </div>
