        <p>Church of England were presented for scrutiny by t, Commission, but the returns of membership of ti Nonconformist bodies were so unreliable that tilt own witnesses threw them over and the Commis: could not accept them. Taking the Nonconforall figures at their highest,and those of the Church at the lowest, the Church came out on top It was the fare and most progressive religious Indy in the Principal' —in fact it was so large that their opponents dare face a census. The Prime Minister let the cat the bag when he said the Welsh Disestablishment should be pushed forward so as to enable it to over.rlt the Veto of tbe House of Lords in this Parlittr.aeli.</p>
        <p>The GOV-er-r;meict were hast3ning- -ITec-a-Je. they that if they did not, there would not be any 3.2' conformists left in Wales ; they would all be ChtO men. (Government Laughter). Even in Wales tIJ could not get crowded meetings in favour Disestablishment•</p>
        <p>Mr C DUKES (Soc., West Ham) said the Cbl evidently rested upon property qualifications. t majority of the workers in Wales did not want „ Anglican Church any more than they wanted the D"r conformist churches. Some of the self-styled shenhe of the people, he contended, put profits and divide° before human life. Mr C PERRIN (C, Rutland), said the measure was t, most vindictive that had emanated from the Liba' party for a considerable time. It was a deliber°l attempt by a political party to break the of the Chcrch in England and \Vales Ii"" was the result of long and gradual growth not of - mere State connection. The None'</p>
        <p>forraists were not Willing to separate their for010;1 Wales from those in England and whenever an sPtod for separation had been made by Welsh NonconforrOnh it had been defeated. Mr Asquith stated We,oo Disestablishment was fully discussed and decided 01'p at the last election. Of 210 Liberal candidateeeo England, however, only four mentiored Slrel/, Disestablishment in their addresses and six 13317,0,0; referred to religious liberty and equality. In Werpte 21 members issuing addresses only eight motto Disestablishment and 2 religious equality. „ ) h° Mr W KILDUFF` (Nationalist, Dublin Ilerolllfr strongly protested against the advocacy of a etbero, solution of the education question. He asked meoolo 'carefully to consider whether Disestablishment mean the deterioration of Christianity, and to t" twice before they gave a vote for it. 1013; Mr. J. MODERMID (C., Paisley), said the Diaestahlio ment Bill bore out the remark of the "Scotch .Aos„l3.totf er in his speech at Warrington that " the Pr.gell instincts of the British people were still very '3' 1118 developed." (Laughter.) He complimented tbe Government on the support they had received fr 013400 secularists. It was characteristic of the support to, which they depended in the country. As to the °ltfri ment that Church property was national properUool' Asquith declined to accept an amendment which the allow the Church to retain any endowments whi.6/0. Courts of law might hold to be private benefactif ne9) The PRIME MINISTER (Mr E Wynne .°031)0,f repudiated very strongly the suggestion of the lu'reeP' for Paisley that the Government ought not to ii"" te, the support of the secularists. He was prepsre(4olo' accept the support of anybody if it would ros.l..etter advancement and improvement. It would be far, ~" ot the if the members opposite would remember tuf ot. great Founder of Christianity had very little It tr, about creeds and dogmas but a great deal 000 s' brotherly relations that should exist amongst E