      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT RESOLUTION DEFEATED.</p>
        <p>Considerable beat was engendered in the course of the adjourned debate on Welsh Disestablishment and Disendowment at the weekly meeting of the Warring- ton Parliamentary Debating Society on Tuesday evening and "strangers " at the back of the House became so demonstrative that the Speaker had to request them to "curb their natural feelings." Considering that a division was to be taken, the attend- ance on the Government benches was meagre and when the resolution welcoming the Premier's promise to Disestablish the Welsh Church was put to the House, it was defeated by 19 votes to 15. The debate was re-opened by The Rev E C E CARLETON (C.. Truro), who said some of the speeches in support of Welsh Disestablishment consisted of a stringing together of election leaflets which were no more reliable than some of the utter- atlCo3 of the Liberal loaders. From Mr Asqnith down- wards through Mr Lloyd George and Mr Ellis Griffith we bad had utterances on the subject that were not I very creditable to their knowledge, either as lawyers or historians. They either invented history or some one invented it for them, and as for the law, sometimes the most obvious and elementary principles were overlooked and violated. Mr Ellis Griffith talked a little while ago of the Church's Parliamentary title to her property for the purpose of decrying it. That was rather funny, considering that the one object of the Bills of Mr Asquith had been to make a new Parliamentary title to the property for other people to whom it never belonged. If a Parliamentary title was good, in the case of the new claimants, why was it not good in the case of the old possessors? But to show that it was not a Parliamentary title, Mr Lloyd George went back more than 1,000 years, even to the days of the British Church, hundreds of years before Parliament existed, in order to make out a flaw in the Church's title. Fancy a man, and a lawyer too, going back over I,COO years to find a flaw in title! There was not a landlord in the Kingdom, not even the Sovereign who could go back as far, and the attempt was of itself sufficient to stamp the whole thing as dishonesty. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Ellis Griffith sought to support his argument for a State origin of the Church's property by giving as a reason the fact that the payment of the tithe was enforced in the Ecclesiastical Courts, quite overlooking how far he gave his case away by alleging such a reason. If it had been a State charge or a State grant or secular State property in any sense, why should the payment of it be made recoverable in the Ecclesiastical Courts, which I were the private courts of the Church, instead of the ordinary civil courts of the country ? It was dealt with in the Ecclesiastical Courts it was Church property and voluntary in its origin. The Speaker nest described as utterly devoid of founda- tion the attempt of Mr. Lloyd George, in his preface to Mr. P. W. Wilson's recent book, to convey the impres- sion that Parliament had been accustomed to deal with Church property and had exercised the right many Limes. He had written to the Prime Minister (who said the serve thing when introducing his first Welsh Church Bill 11 or 15 years ago) and to Mr Lloyd George asking for the authority upon which the statement was made. Mr. Asquith sent no reply ; Mr Lloyd George sent by his secretary a formal acknownsent. Mr. Lloyd George stated that the property held by the Church was given to the whole of the people in Wales. That was not true, for no money given for religious purposes was given to any except those who would use it religiously and for religious purposes. Further, when men made a conscience of detaching themselves from the cause which the money was given to maintain, the responsibility rested with themselves. (Hear, hear.) It must not be thought, as Mr Lloyd George seemed to think, that any minister of God had any responsibility for those who would not receive his ministrations. What was the command of the Saviour to His Apostles when sending them out to preach ? "If they will not receive you or bear you, shake off the very dust of your feet as a testimony against them." Mr Lloyd George took upon himself to contradict the spirit of that message, and to encourage men in the rejection of the Church's message by leading them to expect that if they would only stick out they would have a chance of plundering those who went to them with the Church's message. (Applause). Mr It D OWEN (L, Chelmsford) said the member for Chatham (Mr B S Townroe) bad stated that the Liberals did not touch the question of Disestablishment until they had come to their difficulties with Mr Redmond. (A VOICE : " Quite right.") Then why the Royal Commission in 1906? (Government applause.) It could not be asserted that at that time, at any rate, the Government were in any difficulties with Mr Redmond: (A VOICE : " They are now.") It was contended that Nonconformists were out for plunder, with the object of strengthening themselves at the hands of Anglicanism. His experience, however, was that the men who were not touched by Christianity usually lumped all Christians together and did not make any distinctions of creed and denomination. It was constantly stated that Christianity was played out. The argument used to back up that assertion was that the Church could not stand on its own bottom, that if it were not for State aid it would die a natural death. Those who were in the churches did not believe that, but the onus rested upon them to disprove it. The Bill offered a unique opportunity for the Anglican Church to prove that she was capable of holding her own and was not, as was often asserted, a mere parasite. Mr HARTLEY (C., Skipton) said he was a Free Churchman but, unlike many Nonconformists, he was not willhog to risk a set back to religious activity in order to wound another Church whose only crime was that her ministers occupied somewhat higher social positions and their stipends excited the cupidity of some people. If the Government would bring in a Bill to reform and re-organize the Church of England, be would be with them heart and soul and, if necessary, would vote against bia own party. There were numerous glaring defects in the Church of England. The money which the Church had at her command,if properly distributed, might do a great deal more good than it did at the present time. The abolition of sinecure positions andthe giving to the parishioners of more voice in the manage. ment of the churches were reforms which be would be willing to take up. But reform was quite different from demolition, which the Liberal party wished. The great bulk of the people of Wales did not want Die- establishment ; they did not care two straws about it. It was simply a political move, like Home Rule. Of course, Nonconformist ministers and Radical M.P.'s wanted it. The sl.P.'s knew that they had good electioneering agents in the Welsh Nonconformist ministers, and the ministers must be paid for their labours ; therefore the Radical M.P.'s would reward them by attempting to abolish what they regarded as an opponent. He did not think the self-styled party of righteousness had any right to try to preclude religion from the State. The tendencies to evil were strong enough already and they ought to maintain the national recognition of religion. Mr CHAMBERLAIN (SOC, Gorton) expressed regret that the House had to waste time discussing such a subject in view of the industrial crisis. However, he supported the Bill as a step in the direction of the freedom of the subjects of the United Kingdom. It was high time that the Church was Disestablished. She had begun to lose her hold upon democracy. We were marching OD, and out of the present state of society would evolve a more democratic institution than the Church of to-day. If Christianity was what its followers claimed it to be, it would stand without being buttressed by the State. He supported the 11111 with an ulterior motive because he believed that if they Disestablished the Church they would also get secular education le the schools ; whereby the child of the worker could be taught educa- tion that would fit him for life without having dogmas and creeds drummed into him. The Church was not supported by the workinclasses but was endowed by the parasites of industry. Mr W H STA.NSPIELD (Leader of the Oppcsition) said he would respect Ors advocates of the Dis- establishment and robbery of the Church more if they would only act straighly and with an absence of cant. The Prime Minister (Mr E Wynne Jones) was notori- ously inaccurate in his statements but never in the comae of his experience had he (Mr St .nsfleld) come across a speech E 0 full of gross inaccurankes as the Premier's speech in support of the Bill. He had declared that the Government had no quarrel with the Established Church and had expressed the hope that the question would be raised above party strife and religious animosity. The Home Secretary (Mr W Howarth) bad also stated that Disestablishment would be to the spiritual and financial advantage of the Church. If that were true,why did not Nonconformists practice what they preached ? If it would be good for the</p>
      </div>
