      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Examiner, Saturd y, March, 16, 1912.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturd y, March, 16, 1912.</p>
        <p>resolute game, and rushes caused the game to take a more even turn. Eventually Mclntyre placed his side in a more advantageous position, and quick transfers being witnessed on the left wing, Waywell went over for a try a few minutes from the close. Result : Broughton Rangers, two goals, two tries (ten points) ; Warrington, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WIDNES WEAK AT SALFORD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES WEAK AT SALFORD.</p>
        <p>This match was played at the Willows, Weaste, before only a poor attendance. The game cannot be described as a great one, the defence of Widnes being extremely weak. In the first half the " Reds" ran up a score of twelve points, Pugsley and Luvelnck scoring tries, Mosley placing two goals and W Thomas one. In the concluding half the home side were once more superior both Pugsley and W Thomas running over, Mosley gosling on each occasion. It was only in the closing stages that the visitors showed any semblance of their form, Jones, the Widnes back, sc )ring through a bad miss by Launce. Result :—S ',Hord 5 goals 4 tries (22 points), Widnes I try (3 points). —„—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>'ST. HELENS IN THE FIRST FOUR.</head>
        <p>'ST. HELENS IN THE FIRST FOUR.</p>
        <p>Despite a deluge of rain, there was a fair crowd present for this return League game with Swinton at St. llelens. The home team were early penalised for an informality, and Preston landed a goal. Play fluctu- ated, and eventually Swinton were penalised for not playing the ball, and Turtill made the scores equal. Swinton kicked to the home end, where Briers threw to Turtill, who mulled, and Anderson rushing up scored a try vi hich Preston failed to augment. From loose play Greenwood handed to Flanagan, will easily scored, Shalicross failing. For " feet up" Swinton were penalised, and Turtill put the ball over the bar, and in the closing stages Swinton fell away ; Greenwood and Molyneux adding further tries for St. Helens, which Turtill improved upon. Result :—St Helens 4 goals 4 tries (20 points), Swinton 1 goal I try (5 points).</p>
        <p>MATCHES ON WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>OLDHAM WIN AND WIGAN LOSE.</p>
        <p>Oldham 12, Widnes (h) 11 4,000 Ebbw Vale (h) 6, Wigan 5 Huddersfield (h) 44, Bramley 3 1 eeds (h) 26, Brpughton Rangers 3 3,000 Barrow 13, Bradford (h) 6 2,C00</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION AFFAIRS. Cup Final for Leeds.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION AFFAIRS. Cup Final for Leeds.</p>
        <p>--- At a meeting of the Northern Union General Com- mittee. held at Huddersfield, on Tuesday night, it was decided that the final tie in the Northern Union Cup Competition shall be played at Headingley, Leeds. The international match, Wales v England, wita fixed to be played at Ebbw Vale on the 30th inst. The Leeds Moorland Rugby Union Club asked the permission of the Union for their players, suspended by the Rugby Union, to be allowed to play in Northern Union football. Their request was granted. The Hull Amateurs Club was elected to honorary membership of the Union. Among matches re-arranged were : Leigh v Runcorn, March 13th to March 28th. Salford v Hull Kingston Rovers, March 13th to March 25th. Wakefield Trinity v Rochdale Hornets, March 13th to March 27th. Warrington v Oldham, March 23rd to April let. St. Helens v Dewsbury, March 23rd to March 28th. Barrow v Rochdale Hornets, April 1811 to April 20th. The referee in the Ebbw Vale v Leeds match reported that seventy minutes instead of eighty minutes were played in consequence of bad weather. The committee decided the match should stand. The Batley Club were ordered to pay the Birstal Club the amount of compensation due for the signing on of T. Hudson on or before the 20th inst. The committee made a similar order on Normanton St. John's Club in respect of compensation due to Outwood Church for the signing on of J. Stringer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>REFEREE SERIOUSLY INJURED.</head>
        <p>REFEREE SERIOUSLY INJURED.</p>
        <p>At Porth on Monday, Edward George Hansford, a youth, of Wattstowi, was remanded on bail charged with doing grievous bodily harm to W. E. Williams, a football referee, at a recent junior cup-tie at Wattatown betwein Wattstown and Aberaman. It is alleged that Hansford struck Williams as he was leav- ing the bath at the club's headquarters, and as a result' his condition is now stated to be serious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
