      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION. RECORD SEASON RE-VIEWED.</head>
        <p>WORKS BOWLING ASSOCIATION. RECORD SEASON RE-VIEWED.</p>
        <p>A record season was reviewed at the annual meeting of the Works Bowling Association which was held on Monday evening in the Club room at the Horse and Jockey Hotel, Winwick-road There was a very encouraging attendance of representatives of many works teams. Mr J Willett presiding. SECRETARY'S REPORT. • - - • •</p>
        <p>Mr W PATRICK, honorary secretary, submitted his annual report, which stated that there was nothing but praise to be bestowed upon the officials of the Associa- tion and the bowlers of %Varrington in general for the successful manner in which the Association was.carried on during the season 1911. la concluding his report last season, he stated that the prospects of the Associa- tion were bright, but, seeing that they were coming to a new house and new surroundings, it was just possible that the interest would drop somewhat, but he was pleased to be able to report that such was not the case, it was rather the reverse. (Applause.) They had</p>
        <p>A RECORD ENTRY in their competition, 50 teams entering from 35 works, registering 2,200 players, whereas last season they had 39 teams from 27 works, registering 1,247 players. It tcok 14 Saturdays to complete the competition' 49 matches being played altogether, 70 start being the limit, and, although a limit team (Corporation No. 2) won the competition, he did not think it could be said they were over handicapped ; in fact, the whole of the handicapping was very fair, as out of the 49 games played, 25 were won by less than 20 point s,the winners of the competition themselves being lucky to win on two occasions by the narrow margin of 14 points. The attendance at the matches was very good, almost twice as many paying to go on the green compared with the previous year. The competition over, they commenced their annual handicap. They had an entry of 483,being 158 more than the previous year. That found another six weeks' work for the committee. Mr T Jackson (Crosfield's) was the winner. THANKS to the generosity of Lady Greenall, who gave the first prize, value £5 55., the generosity of the President (Captain G. Crosfield), who gave the second prize, value £3 35., and the kindness of the Vice- Presidents, the Association were able to give 121 prizes to the value of .£53 IGs. 6d. (Applause) Thanks were due to Mr. and Mrs. Rushworth for the able manner in which they catered for the bowlers both on and off the green. The green had been kept in splendid condition through the season at no small cost. (Hear, hear and applause.) Praise was due to both players and spectators, the loser congratulating the winner, the winner wishing the loser better luck next time" in the good old English way. At no time was it found nececessary for Mr. Rushworth to complain to the committee. Last, but not least, the committee and officials were deserving of a word of praise for the unselfish way in which they bad carried on the Associa- tion, sacrificing their own pleasure week after week for the benefit of others. (Applause.) HONORARY TREASURER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The balance-sheet presented by the Honorary Treasurer (Mr. Rushworth) showed a total income of £ll9 12s. 5d., and after paying expenses there was a balance of £2 10s. 4d. Both reports were adopted. The CHAIRMAN complimented the secretary uprn his admirable report end said the committee sacrificed a great deal of time in the interests of the association. The past season had been an excellent one and be I believed that of 1912 would be even hotter. (Applause)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Captain George R Crosfield was re-elected president the Chairman remarking that in the captain they had the right man in the right place. (Applause). Mr W Patrick, honorary secretary, Mr Rushworth, honorary treasurer, and Messrs R Straw and A Burdett, auditors, were re-appointed. The following were elected the Committee :—Messrs J Willett (Crosfield's), T Dean ON hi tecross), Bandies (Fletcher's), W Simister (Longford Wire), H Belford (Wire Ropery), T O'Brien (Castle Rubber), J Bandies (Corporation), J Stewart (Monks Hall), J Smith (Richmond's), F Dale (C L C.), and J Howarth (Naylor's). It, was decided to form a general Council, consisting of a representative of each works, and this body will have control of the handicapping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SOME INTERESTING POINTS.</head>
        <p>SOME INTERESTING POINTS.</p>
        <p>The question of handicapping gave rise to consider- able discussion*, a proposition being brought forward by ;/Ir F Dean that the teams te handicapped on the same basis as the competitors in the annual handicap— that is individually instead of in teams. The feeling of the meeting was in favour of the con- tinuance of the present system which the secretary said had worked successfully from begioning to end. It was further proposed that each player should ba required to pay one penny for admission to the green, the money thus collected being placed to a separate fund for the purchase of a better quality of medal than was at present given. It was stated that such a levy would have realized £5 last season. Several delegates expressed the view that the pro- posal was not in the best interests of sportsmanship and eventually it was defeated. After a discussion it was decided to join the British Crown Green Association, which it was mentioned would debar professionals from taking part in the competitions. A proposal that a consolation competition be held for all clubs knocked out in the first round was rejected.</p>
        <p>George Matbery, a labourer, of Sunderland yard, Wakefield, was fined 15s and 17s 6d costs at Wakefield on Monday, with the option of a month's imprisonment, for taking his fox-terrier by the collar and bashing" it twice against a wall.</p>
        <p>TENASITINE: a New Liquid Cement for Mending and Making. In tubes id., 2d., 3d.9 6d.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL INSURANCE.</p>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE'S ACTIVITIES.</p>
        <p>"It's as simple as A.8.C." is a very old saying, but applicable to many ,things modern. At first the National Insurance Act could not come within the scope of this phrase, but since its inception many societies have organised public meetings for the put. pose of enlightening the people on matters which appeared complex to the lay mind. One of the most prominent of such societies in Warrington is the Progressive Labour League which has conducted a series of weekly meetings with an encouraging degree of success. (There was a good attendance in the Wycliffe Congregational Schoolroom on Monday even- ing, tholchief speaker being Councillor Middleton of Manchester. Mr. M Llewellyn presided and be was supported on the platform by the speaket,Mr J Morton and Mr A Buchanan.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN in opening the proceedings said it was remarkable how the Insurance Act had been misrepresented on every hand. Every little argument that could be raised was being put forward at the present moment. (Hear, hear.) They saw big placards on which were the words "Ninepence for four- pence. At the Dumping Exhibition it said that the only way to get ninepence for fourpance was by voting for Tariff Reform. (Laughter.) He was sure they knew eout the markets that had been closed and how much the consumer had to pay for things if a tax were imposed upon them. (Hear, hear.) As workingmen they ought to take a greater interest in things which had such vital meanings for them. They had almost looked upon the Insurance Act as something which would not he true. Ihsy had not been used to such . . . .</p>
        <p>legislature on their behalf. As time went on the Insurance Act would advocate itself, for it would speak for itself on every hand and tt ey as workingmen would only be surprised they had stood aloof from it, at all. In regard to the sanatoria benefits under the Act, the workingmen would have the same chance of life as those who had wealth and affluence. (Applause). Councillor MIDDLETON, in the course of a very inter- esting address, said it was for the roason that the Act was quite a new thing in politics that they were going about explaining its provisions. Some Acts of Parlia- ment had not been worth explaining, but an Act that was going to affect 15,000,030 people in this country Was one the people wanted to know something about. It was absolutely necessary to take some steps to cover the working population with some form of insurance. To cover everyone they must have some form of legisla- tion and so t ey got the Insurance Act. At the beginning the Opposition had said they agreed with the principle of the Bill but we had since fooni that their principle was entirely against the measure. (Hear, hear). If it, the Act were not compulsory it would not be national. (Hear, bear.) Then again instead of setting up in competition with the friendly societies the Government had decided that the scheme would be better worked through the societies now in existence. (Hear, hear.) The workman was not grumbling very much about his payments because he realised that inas- much as the benefits would to come to him it was only reasonable to ask him to pay to the fond from whic'o the benefits would be derived. The employer had not been very unmanageable. He had no doubt realized that it was not unfair to ask him to do somthing in the matter, as it was to his interest and the interest of every industry that the workers should be kept as fit as possible for the work they had had to do. (Hear, hear.) He was of the opinion that when the Act was in operation the employer would forget be wls paying because the payment would get merged with the general tun of things. _ . . . . . . .</p>
        <p>The SPEAKER dealt at some length with the various payments and benefits under the Act and said he thought the greatest benefit was the free medical service and he hoped that would be secured. (Hear, hear.) Also in the maternity benefits they got one of the many human touches which were in the Act and he thought those benefits appealed to people most. As soon as a child was born it begari earnins 7s. 61 per week for four weeks. (Applause.) Thoso benefits would have the effect of greatly diminishing the death rate amongst very young children. It was scandalous in this country that so many children died before they had learned how to live properly. The Act meant real social reform because it went into the homes and lives of the people and did something to uplift them. It would eventually he as popular as the Old Age Pensions Act. (Applause). Numerous questions were put to the Councillor who answered them in a lu3id manner. Mr NEW, in proposing a vote of thanks to the speaker, said they were, by the Insurance Act, going to do a great deal to wipe out sickness by the abolition of slum property and better sanitary conditions. (Hear, hear.) They were not only going to cure consumptives but they were going to try and stop that terrible scourge by making the landlords responsible for proper sanitation. (Applause). Mr MORTON seconded and C )uncillor Middleton after a formal response proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman which was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>LIBERAL MEETING IN ST. JAMES' SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETING IN ST. JAMES' SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>If the activities of the above society have been sur- passed at all it has been by the Warrington Liberal Association who have also held a large number of meetings for the purpose of explaining the workings of this great measure. Arranged by this association a meeting, which was well attended,was held in St James' school, on Wednesday evening. An excellent address , on the Act was given by Mr 'l' Ternent, of Rochdale. Mr 11 New, who presided in the absence of Mr John Dodds, in opening the meeting said they should , all bear in mind that the Act had not been introduced simply to provide against sickness and unemployment but it was going to strike at the root of some of the greatest evils they had to contend with. It was going to strike at the root of poverty, it was going to try and alleviate the burdens of the ' women in times of maternity,the time when she felt the burdens heaviest. It was going to strike a blow at the slum property owners and make them provide better dwellings. Then there were the glorious sanatoria benefits. The time had come when the nation must grapple determinedly with this terrible scourge. They were proud the Liberal Party had had tho privilege and the courage to bring that Act into being and get it through the House of Lords. Despite the gloomy fore- bodings of the Tory Press and the Tory Party generally who were trying to get the people to believe that the Act would not come into operation until January 1913, they were assured by Mr Masterman that it would come into operation in July of this year. (Applause.) They all deplored the great industrial upheaval in their midst but they could confidently state, despite the placards of the Tory party, that they had found the man,who was temperate, sane and broad-minded enough to bring the employes and masters together on one common ground and issue. (Applause.) He hoped that a settlement would be arrived at before the week-end. (Hear, hear.) He was sure there was a good time coming. In regard to the Minimum Wage Rill, the man in the street seemed to think that the Prime Minister had only to frame a Bill and put it on the table and it was passed. He, however could not:compel the employers or owners to accept it, but he was doing a saner piece of work in asking them If they were prepared to accept it. (Hear, hear and , applause). Mr. TERNENT, at the outset, wanted to associate him- self with the remarks of the Chairman in connection with the present deplorable strike. He was certain that if they had had any other man than Mr. Asquith at the head of affairs at the present time, the probability is that the country would have been in a state bordering on revolution. There was a party in the country to-day, who wished to use force and compel the working men to accept conditions they had no right to accept. They had got away once and for all from those days of coercion, and they were never going back to them. (Hear, hear.) He was sure Mr Asquith was using the right means, and eventually he hoped good would be the outcome. .</p>
        <p>The remarkable thing about the Insurance Act was that for the first time in our British House of Commons the principle of the Act was accepted by every political party in the country, when it was first introduced. On the second leading of the Act there eras not a single member of Parliament prepared to go into the lobby and vote against the principle. What, then, was the principle ? It was the principle of a contributory and compulsory scheme. Everyone accepted that in May last, but since that time they had had 'certain classes of Tory demagogue who had been going about the country saying that a contributory scheme was not the scheme the people wanted. If Lloyd-George had wanted to win the popular feeling of the country it would have been an easy matter for him to have brought in a non-contributory •gcheme and to have got the money from sources Which would no doubt have roused the ConservAtive Party. (Hear, hear.) Lloyd George wished to have a scheme that would be universally accepted and he wished to bring in an Act that would be beneficial to the great masses of working people. (Hear, hear). The Tory party had placed themselves in a very peculiar position and they, the Liberal Party, would not forget when the time for amendments of the Act came, that the Tories asked for a non-contributory scheme. At the South Manchester election they were met with the cry "Why is not this scheme a voluntary scheme ?—lt seemed as if the old Manchester school of politics had been resurrected, because everyone was standing up for individual liberty. (Hear, hear.) He, the speaker, thought they had got past that day in the political life of this country. They must have compulsion became the condition of. life in this country had been a failure, They had tried voluntary, systems, and all honour to the men who had formed the friendly societies in the past and have tried to gather in the masses of the country in such societies. These societies, however were com- posed of the cream of the working classes. They were failing to get in the masses of the population so that they were forced to go back and make insurance cornpulsp7. (Hear, hear.) In every organized community of men it ought to be that every man in that community should be able to reach a certain state of comfort in his existence. (Hear, hear.) After he</p>
        <p>had worked hard and done his duty be ought to have an existence which brought independence and lifted them up from beggary and poverty. (Hear, hear.) That was only a modest ideal for every working man to have but they had never yet reached. it. If a man bad an accident he was reduced from a state of independence to absolute beggary. Not only he himself but his family who were dependent on him. What the community wanted was healthy men and women ; it did not want men to lose their independence and fall in a state of poverty and drag their wives and children with them. (Hear, hear.) lathe National Insurance Act was a scheme which had for its chief aim the alleviation of sickness and desti- tution. (Applause.? It brought within its scope all manual workers who earned less than £3 is Gcl per week. There were certain types of workers—those getting less than 93 per week—that the State contri- buted one penny for directly. The worker in such cases would not pay one farthing. The employer would have to pay sixpence and the State's penny would make it into the sevenpence. That was the penalty for the employer who paid low wages—(hear, kear)—and those people would be brought into prominenoe in the future (Hear, hear) In certain industries the State prevented the wages dropping to such a low margin. There were some firms who were going to do the generous thing as a firm in Essex had recently promised to do and that was to pay the whole sevenpence. (Applause.) Mr Ternent then gave a lengthy and admirable explanation of all the workings of the Act and at the close many difficult questions were answered by him to the satisfaction of the company present. Votes of thanks were heartily accorded the speaker and the Chairman and suitable responses were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>"THE BOSS OF THE SHOW."</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday,lllr G lather presiding. There were also present :—Mrs Simpson, Mrs Collingwood, Miss Broadbent, Rev A M Mitchell, Dr Murray, MeEsra E L Bent, A J Jolley, IV Gill, C Brooks. R Lee, NV Furness, W Houghton, J Ball, J A Hughes, J A Rushworth, A Brooks, A Bottoruley (clerk), and R %V Peacock (deputy clerk). "BOSS OF THE SHOW."</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the last meeting were submitted the Rev A M Mitchell inquired whether a letter had been received from the Liverpool Vestry with reference to the expunging of the word " pauper " from cfficial records, &amp;c. The CFIAIRMAN : You are out of order. The Rev A M Mr;citELL : I submit lam not. The CHAIRMAN : I submit you are and lam boss of this show. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>REV. A. M. MITCHELL AND THE ESTIMATES. The Finance Committee considered the Clerk's estimate of expenditure for the half-year ending September 30th, 1912, and recommended that the same be approved add that calls be made upon the respective overseers of the Union for the bum of £14,000 based upon the assessable value of each parish. Mr. JOLLEY, in moving the adoption of the minutes,l I said the call recommended was £1,200 less than the anticipated deficit, bat the Committee were satisflad that the balances justified that course. The poundage call on each parish would remain the same as in the previous half-year. Mr. FURNESS seconded. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL moved that the estima' e 3 he referred back for a more detailed report. " I hardly know how to address you," he said to the Chairman " whether as Chairman or as boss of the show." The CHAIRMAN : BOES " will do. (Laughter). The Rev A M MITCHELL : Perhaps if I say "Mr Chairman and boss of the show" I shall be sure to be right. (Laughter.) Continuing he reiterated his com- plaint made at the last meeting that there was nothing in the estimates to indhate the outlay on aoirits or to differentiate in the salaries of the various officials. Was the cost of spirits included in the item of " in-maintenance or did it come under the head where the objectionable word " pauper" occurred namely, " paupers in hospitals and schools" ? There ought to be tabulated statements and be suggested that it would be a good thing if they followed the example of some other Boards and gave the cost per head of the maintenance of the inmates. He would also like to know how the cost per head of officers' rations compared with the amount devoted to the maintenandd of their poor brothers and sisters in the House. ".1 am very sorry, Mr Chairman and boss of the show" concluded Mr Mitchell "that I should have to draw attention again to the matter. I looked forward te this meeting with a certain amount of satisfaction, feeling that some light would be thrown on the subject by the Finance Committee, but I have came here, it seems,l more or less on a fool's errand and I don't like it." The amendment was not seconded. The CLERK was asked if be wished to answer Mr. Mitchell but replied "I don't see that there is anything to answer." Continuing, he said Mr. Mitchell must know that the estimate was the considered esti nate of the Union expenditure for the half-year and therefore must include everything. Mr. Mitchell had hit him (the Clerk) twice with regard to the use of the word "pauper." He (the Clerk) had told the Guardians on more than one occasion that he had never used the name except when he was absolutely forced to do so, in relation to statutory heads. The estimate was based on the headings prescribed by the Local Government Board, and every member of the Board must know that the item "paupers in hospitals and schools " related to those persons who were chargeable to the Board in institutions over which they had no control. When Mr Mitchell asked whether that item included the expenditure on whisky or whether it was included in the sum for in-maintenance, he (the Clerk) said the rev. getleman wanted to get more experience and knowledge of Poor Law matters, and then he would not ask such questions of him. The Rev. A. M. MITCHELL I don't get much infor- mation from you, anyway. The CLERK said the Chairman of the Finance Com- mittee by whom the estimates had been considered, would have been prepared to answer any question from Mr. Mitchell or any other member. The question of an analysis of expenditure was a different thing altogether from an estimate. The Rev A M MITCHELL: We have not had the least information. The CHAIRMAN : Order, order. The Rev A M MITCHELL : It is not fair. The minutes were then approved. THE PROPOSED NEW BUILDINGS. The Building Committee having ascertained that the number of male imbecile end epileptic patients in the Workhouse is greater than the number of female imbecile and epileptic patients, recommended that accommodation be provided for 100 male patients and 60 female patients. The following architects were asked to submit plans and approximate estimates : Messrs W and S Owen, Messrs Wright and Hamlin, Mr Barnish, Messrs Curran and Son, Mr P Silcock, Mr A Warburton, Mr W Walker, and Mr Garnett, all of Warrington. The minutes were approved. ROMAN CATHOLIC REQUEST. .</p>
        <p>The Rev Father Wa'matey, of St. Peter's, Woolston, wrote stating that it had been resolved to restore the church at a cost of £3OO or £4OO. He asked the Board, as pewholders on behalf of the Roman Catholic children at Padgate Cottage Homes, to grant a subscription. The letter was referred to the Cottage Homes Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON WORKINGMEN'S ASSOCIATION (N.A. CENTRE). The Northern Anglers' Executive Council met for the first quarterly meeting on Saturday last at the Church Hotel, Bolton. Crewe Centre proposed that all dates for E.C. meetings be fixed so that they will not inter- fere with club matches.</p>
        <p>The important matters dealt with poisoning of Trent and Mersey Canal ; tar-spraying of roads ; Is. licences to fish river Brock •, E.C. dates June Bth, October sth, November 30th ; the right to fish free rivers ; appoint- ing emergency committee; representatives for National Federation, Messrs. Redford, Bolton, and Brown, Crewe ; extension of anglers railway .tickets for 5 days, and date for grand fishing match Saturday, September 28th, the match to be fished on similar lines to last year ; one out of every live in club matches goes to centre match, and one out of every five in centre match to go to the final, lowest prize to be 7s 6d. For the benefit of new members and those who missed the list of set and special prizes we published some weeks ago we will again put them in print Set Board, Ist and 2nd bream. 6s and 2s ; Ist and 2nd chub, Gs and 2a ; Ist and 2nd roach, 6s and 2s ; Ist and 2nd perch, Gs and 2s ; Ist and 2nd trout, 6s and 2s ; Ist and 2nd dace, Gs and 2s ; Ist and 2nd gudgeon, Gs and 2s. Special prizes, Ist, 2nd and 3rd roach from Norton, Bs, 4s and 3s; Ist 2nd, and 3rd gudgeon from Norton, 8s Is and 3s • Ist and 2nd eel from Acton 'Bridge, Gs and 2s ' • let and 2nd daddy ruffe, open waters, 5s and 2s Gd ; Ist pike, close season, 5s ; Ist flounders, close season, 5s ; Ist roach from Norton, juveniles, 53 ; Ist gudgeon from Norton, juveniles, 5s ; Ist six roach, open waters, must be caught one week-end, 5s ; Ist six roach in July, 23 6d ; Ist roach in August, 23 ; Ist roach in September, 2s ;from Norton,lst novice opening match, 3s; Ist novice annual match, 3s ; total of set board and srecial prizes .£7 la. Members please keep this list by them for future reference. Angling for the last week end of the open season was not very favourable but anglers turned out in fair numbers and if the quantity was not great it was made up for by the quality. We anticipated a busy week end for the Set 130ard and were not disappointed. Four roach were marked up, the lowest weight turning the scale at Isoz 4 drams. There was also a catch of six that weighed 3lbs, not at all a bad week's weighing 3onsideriog the unfavourable conditions. It cannot be a great while before the eels are in full swing as one angler took nearly a dozen at the Govvy on Sunday last, and with a weeks warm weather they should be in strength. Mr. John Orgill, of the Shrewsbury Arms, has promised to give a prize for the largest Roach caught in the Gowy, from opening season till November 31st, conditions, to be weighed in at Shrewsbury Arms, any evening before G o'clock. ) Next club night Monday March 25th at S o'clock. A. S.</p>
        <p>3</p>
