        <p>rtflE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>- -------. - SATURDAY MARCH 16 1912.</p>
        <p>The advocates of joliE NAVY retrenchment are much -4,3TImATEs. more certain to be dis- appointed with r. thal:lC*T CHURCHILL'S first Navy estimates Ct those Who think in battleships, as Mr. ErhCI,L RI-10DES was said to "think in last.lsl.:es." There has been talk during the £N„ f lew rom days of a saving of anything 'OOO t0£2,000,000.--;- These expectations 1)11-.111(11'1; have been justified if only the k' 7ng Ship- Vote had been matter of concern. h °I what is saved in one direction 13:1'ever, is swallowed up by other •141.1ds on the nation. On the Ship- lag Vote there is a saving of more a' Million and a quarter, but the actual to tleti°ll in the Estimates does not amount Triore than £300,000. The expenei- ' .zeT for the , financial year that has not yet q)t. 111cleu was £44,392,500 and the estimated threilditure for 1912-13 £44,085,400. Even qnlay fall below what will actually be itlilted• In the explanatory statement i)f ii;" Was issued last night, the First Lord this e Admiralty is thoroughly candid on 13eeli Pc; int. The Estimates, he says, have Bistc.rained on the assumption that the Progr of other Powers will increasedec:• Should this expectation Ileee, -lit tO be ill-founded, it will be solc,sarY, and it is intended to bring forward . 'ones,. Y Estimates, both for men and The broil saving on the Shipbuilding Vote is the gilt about through the cutting down of ilattile,ar:s Programme to the extent of one for isulP• The proposals now made are artrlo°l-Irllr large armoured ships, eight light 13rope,e, d shiPs, 20 destroyers, and the ,r.'ionate number of submarines and Nniar 131, Y Craft. The total cost of this I 1111140 ailnie is to be in round figures four the and a-half. The proportion of new Programme to be undertaken in</p>
        <p>the new financial year, says Mr. CHURCHILL' is larger than usual. Several reasons are given why the reductions on the Shipbuilding Vote are to only a small extent a reduction of expenditure. There are several considerable increases. One of these in regard to men. The Navy cannot be increased in extent and efficiency without increasing also the number of the men who stand behind the guns. The Estimates pro- vide- for an addition of 2,003. Then, of course, as prices have been rising all round, there is a large addition to the cost Of material. New construction means new armament, and there is therefore an increase in that direction. Also, finally, the works at Rosyth and at Portsmouth call for a large additional expenditure, as the contractors cannot keep terms with the Government unless they put in a great deal of extra work during the coming year. Thus it comes about that the anticipated saving is almost all distributed on one hand or the other.</p>
        <p>Though the Estimates will be regarded unfavourably by many supporters of the Government, who apparently do not realise the full implications of its policy and the full needs of the nation, they are not likely to stay the clamour for increased naval expendi- ture on the part of the Government's political antagonists. It may be useful, therefore, to call attention to some hard facts. The statement is being constantly made, without a shadow of foundation, that his Majesty's Government is neglecting the Navy. The simple truth is that a sum of between thirteen and fourteen millions a year is being spent on the Navy in excess of what was being expended upon it by the late Government in the finan- cial year 1901-2. The Naval expenditure of the current financial year has been much the largest on record, and the expenditure now , proposed is only a second best. The reduc- tionf as we have shown, is exceedingly small. The present Government may be said to have built the existing Navy. We had not a single completed Dreadnought when its predecessor left office. Stating the fact broadly, the pre- sent Government is spending more on new construction by ten millions a year than did the Government of Mr. BALFOUR when it was doing its utmost. What is it that its Naval critics want? Many of them, it is obvious, do not want the truth to be known. But people who regard the great work of Naval defence from a national standpoint, and not as it may be made to serve the petty interests of party, will agree that the policy of the Government is sound, adequate, and patriotic. _</p>
        <p>On the Opposition GRAPPLING WITH benches there is no very PAUPERISM. strong inclination to</p>
        <p>criticise unfavourably the Way in which Mr. Burns administers his Department. But the plea is raised that, while the administration is all right, the Government has omitted to legislate for the problem of pauperism, because any measures of that kind would not be "vote-catching." Mr. Burns pointed out on Monday that the work of grappling with pauperism was not confined to a single Department. How could it be said that the Government was doing nothing to grapple with pauperism when in pensions, grants to the Development Com- mission and the. Road Board, feeding and medical inspection of the children, the Government was spending £15,000,000 more per year than three or four years ago in dealing with pauperism and its cognate problems?</p>
        <p>Drat Mr. Asquith's "UNDER NO colleagues and followers ILLUSION." have an unwavering confidence in the cour- age and wisdom of his leadership was strikingly manifested at the banquet at the Covent Garden Theatre. There was no mistaking the note of that great gathering. Mr. Asquith's speech was just as strongly marked by one dominant note—the note of calm determination. Recent elections, Mr. Asquith admitted, had been fought almost exclusively on the Insurance Act. And that measure had been represented as a " vote-catching" piece of legislation! The Government was never under any illusion on that. But the facility with which misrepresentation has stirred up a fleeting unpopularity against the Act, has never, Mr. Asquith declared, for a single moment affected the satisfaction of the Government that they had been able to secure what in the domain of social reform would be found in the long run to be the greatest boon ever conferred on the working classes of this country.</p>
        <p>Sir G. Croydon Marks's AN IMPORTANT Bill to remedy Non-</p>
        <p>_ ANNOUNCEMENT. conformist grievances in single-school areas passed its second reading without a division on the last " Parliamentary " day of last week. It is a very moderate measure, enabl- ing non-provided schools to be transferred in these areas, with reasonable facilities for denominational teaching. Lord Hugh Cecil, however, denounced it furiously because it proposed" to make in Church schools undenominational teaching—which Church- men detested—the rule and denominational teaching the exception." Ihe most interest- ing feature of the debate was the announce- ment made by Mr. Pease, as President of the Board of Education, that the Government intend to deal with the subject next year in a comprehensive spirit and on a national basis.</p>
        <p>N(YIEM</p>
        <p>In reply to questions in the House of Commons, on Monday, Mr. Masterman stated that he knew no reason why the Insurance Act should not come into operation in July, and the Prime Minister said he iintended to introduce the Home Rule Bill early in the week in which the House resumes after Easter—the date always contemplated by the Government since before :the opening of the session.</p>
        <p>* - One of the greatest sufferers from the Suff- ragette persecution is Mrs. Asquith, and her extraordinary experiences threaten to affect her health. Women have actually tried to throw themselves in the way of her motor car, and she has received letters threatening to poison her and her children. She never knows when she may find her home in a state of siege. She, like her husband, must always go under police protection, and her younger children cannot leave home except I under guard. • a</p>
        <p>Warrington women Liberals will look an inch, at least, taller for the next few days. At the spring conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Women's Liberal Associations in Blackburn on Wednesday, the President announced that the Warrington Association was now the largest branch in the country. For some time Blackburn has led the way, but the Warrington women have forged ahead in fine style since the last election, and now hold the proud record with over 2,000 paying members. We almost wish they all had votes ! • . .</p>
        <p>* * Miss Crosfield took a prominent part in the Conference meetings by seconding Mrs. F. D Acland's resolution in support of the Con- ciliation Bill,and Women's Suffrage generally. Her clear, logical advocacy of the cause created a very favourable impression. One of Miss Crosfield's most telling illustrations was that there are to-day more women in trade unions than there were men in trade unions when the men got the vote. Together with most serious politicians, Miss Crosfield deplored the latest outbreak on the part of the militants.</p>
        <p>* * • The travelling public of Warrington and district—although not by any means so badly served as Manchester suburban dwellers— are ohafing under the inconvenience caused by the dislocation of the railway services. Mr. Alfred Brooks, of Rixton, was so annoyed at the sudden and unannounced change in one company's arrangements that at the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday he facetiously, suggested that they " might invite some of the oil ladies in the Workhouse to accept positions as railway directors. . The Chairman (Mr. G. Mather) added insult to injury by remarking that the §uggestion was a libel on the old dames. * *</p>
        <p>From Hobart come; the first news of the conquest of the South Pole by Amundsen, the Swede. And that city has other associa- tions with the explorers of renown, especially with the most famous of early Arctic ven- turers. From 1837 to 1843 Sir John Franklin was Governor of Tasmania, and did much to stimulate the growth of the baby city. But the lure of the Arctic called him from his official duties, and he came home to sail for the North Pole on that expedition from which no one returned. Hobart comm- emorates his name in Franklin Park, the central space which has been cleared of the old Government house in which Sir John and Lady Franklin lived.</p>
        <p>* - In the course of a powerful argument in favour of women's suffrage, addressed to a public meeting in Oxford, on Saturday night, Lord Haldane said that because a few mis- guided women had been carried away by an excess of zeal for their cause, there was no reason why those who held deep convictions on this question should refrain from express- ing those convictions or from seeking to bring them home. If at the moment the popularity of the cause had been affected, suffragists should neither lose courage nor abate their efforts one jot. A great responsibility rested with this generation—to take advantage of the first practical chance which Parliament had ever given for the extension of the franchise to women._</p>
        <p>o * Is the fisherman's worm -an "animal" within the purview of the Protection of Animals Act, passed last year? This is a question which has of late grievously agitated fishing circles. We are interested to see that Mr. George Greenwood, M.P., in a letter to the "Times," allays the fears by quoting the legal opinion recently published in the "Fishing Gazette: We are of opinion that live-bait fishing in the ordinary and usual manner, where the mode of attaching the live-bait to the trace is such as not to occasion more pain than is necessary for the successful use of the lure, is not cruelty, and does not constitute an offence under the protec- lion of Animals Act, 1911, nor render the angler liable to penalties. We are also of opinion that the use of worms or maggots for bait is not an offence under the Act.</p>
        <p>So that after all, Parliament has not inadvertently dealt a blow at the fisherman, and the worm may go on turning—on the * *</p>
        <p>hook 1</p>
        <p>Mr. Bonar Law is not a success as a leader, and, away altogether from the House of Commons, he is regarded as a disappointment (says the London correspondent of the "Yorkshire Observer.") Those of us who knew. Mr. Bonar Law fairly well dre,not surprised. He is an earnest man, but shallow, and has fallen into the mistake of imagining that a piling up of adjectives represents force of argument. He has had a bad fall over the question of the Army rifle. Lord Haldane laid a neat trap for him, but the Unionist leader has only himself to blame for tumbling into it. If he had made any inquiry at all of any one who. is in the army—and his party assumes that all officers belong to them—he would have learnt that it is a Conservative Government which is to blame for the present defective rifle .4„ He could also have learnt from the High School of Musketry of the experiments which Lord Haldane had ordered to be carried out with a new weapon. Even when he spoke this rifle was in the hands of a battalion at Aldershot to undergo a triaL</p>
        <p>In the case of Mr. J. F. Awbery, the genial master of the Workhouse, Nature seems to have given wealth with one hand and taken away health with the other. The news that he is about to resign his position in order tolive abroad will be received with genuine regret by his many Warrington friends. It seems the irony of fate that only a few months after the stroke of luck which brought him a fortune of £lB,OOO, Mr. Awbery should be stricken with an illness which has permanently incapacitated him from dis- charging the duties to which he was so con- scientiously devoted. " Humaneness " has been the keynote of his regime at the home of poverty in Guardian-street, and the inmates have come to look upon him as a kindly friend and adviser who felt for them in their misfortunes. Outside his official life, Mr. Awbery has had many • interests. He was one of the first to associate himself with the Parliamentary Debating Society on its resuscitation a few years ago and,as Chief Secretary for Ireland, was responsible for a Home Rule Bill. No-one will join more cordially in wishing him a long and happy retirement than his fellow members of the local Parliament.</p>
        <p>S LOCAL PARS</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>We are please] to learn th it Mr. Hero y S. llorrahin, who ig a native of Warrington, Iris obtained the Associate Diploma . the Institute of Commerce, and the Association of Bookkeepi7g Teachers. Liberal Women's Ward Teas.</p>
        <p>We are informed that the Ward Teas for White- cross, Latchford, and Stockton Heath, will not be held until the end of April, when Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, is expected to preside.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLES.—Agent for " Douglas" &amp; "Premier." New Models now in stock. Trials and all information free.-J.S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge St., Warrington.-Advt Welsh Disestablishment. A mass meeting of protest against the proposed Welsh Disestablishment and Disendowment will be held in the Parr Hall on Monday at 7.45 p.m., when the speakers will be the Bishops of Liverpool and Llandaff, and Mr. E. W. Johnsoa (a Welsh Layman) and others</p>
        <p>Next Week at the Hippodrome. Another good show is promised for next week at the Hippodrome. Amongst other turns which will appear will be The Martilloni family of acrobats, and Phil Rees stable lade, who will take part in a novel racing act. Turner Parr will present the Comedy Meistere, who are, we understand, comedians and vocalists of a high standard.</p>
        <p>Warrington Liberal Association. Under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association a mass meeting will be held in the Wycliffe Schools, Becvsey-street, on Monday next, to commence at 8 o'clock. Mr. C. E. Parker will preside and the speaker will be Mr. T. W. Dobson, ex-M.P. for Plymouth. An invitation is cordially extended to ladies, and questions are invited.</p>
        <p>" PREMIER " CYCLES.—Special lines at £5 10s. or with 3 speed gear, £6 153.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51-55, Bridge at., Warriogton.—Advt. " What Every Woman Kn3ws." A notable engagement that will be welcomed by all playgoers is that of next week at the Shakespeare, Liverpool, when J. M. Barrie's masterpiece " What every woman knows," will be presented by Miss H.lll Trevelyan ' and Mr. Edward Gweun's Co., including Miss Hilda Trevelyan, who will appear in her original character of " Maggie Wylie" a part she has played in London for over 500 nights.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Band of Hope. In connection with the above society, on Monday last there was a limelight lecture entitled " A Trip from Manchester to Oban, by the Caledonian Rail- way." The slide, which were kindly lent by the Railway Company, were of a very interesting character. The lecture portion was well delivered by Mr. Mancock before a fairly good audience.</p>
        <p>LAWN MOWERS ground by special machinery. Send your machines now before the rush.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. Education.—The Dental Clinic.</p>
        <p>The Education Committee will meet on Monday evening next. The Board of Education has sanctioned the arrangements for a dental clinic, and the Medieal Inspection Committee has appointed Mr H H Taylor and Mr H Frankish as school dental officers at a salary of £42 each per annum. klis3 K Grant has been appoin'ed attendant at the c!inic at a salary of :c_r3o.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street P.S.A. Society. The half-yearly distribution of prizes took place on Sunday last before a fair meeting. There were numer- ous prizes which were distributed by Mrs Simpson The chair was ably occupied by Mr Thomas Hewitt. Miss Adams was the soloist and her sweet voice was heard to advantage. The pianist was Mr G Hughes. • Prudential Staff Entertained.</p>
        <p>On the invit ttion of the Superintendent, Mr. S. Brocklehurt•t, and the Assistant Superintendents, the members of the staff of the Warrington District, num- bering 45, met at dinner on Thursday evening at the Wiumarleigh Cafe. After dinner the Superintendent reviewed the work of the district for the past year, and dealt with the prospect for the present year. He also took the opportunity to go into the new Insurance Act —in connection with which the Company intended to become an approved Society— and after making a lucid explanation of the Bill as it affected their interests, it was deemed advisable to give an oppor- tunity for its discussion at a subsequent meeting, and also then to deal with other matters concerning the welfare of tha district. Each assistant superintendent and several agents spoke, and although the national coal strike which darkened the horizon was detrimental to their interests, yet the settlement of this, combined with the increased benefits offered .in both branches to the public, could not fail to make the assembly optimistic for the future. The proceedings were enlivened by a capital musical programme, arranged by Mr. Jack, the local cashier, several mem- bers of the staff creditably contributing. Miss Brocklehurst ably presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>- VAC'trUM CLEANERS, thoroughly reliable machines from 375. 6cl.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmongers, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Fletcher, Russell &amp; Co. Ltd. After writing .£2,650 off sundry investments and charging depreciation, debenture interest, and the balance of the cost of removal from Pendleton to Warrington, theie is a profit for the year of 6,152; while £1,611 was brought forward. The directors recommend a 5 per cent, dividend on the ordinary shares and carry £363 forward. The balance of goodwill, amounting to £21,250, has been written off out of the reserve fund. The last report, which was for ten months only, showed a profit of £3,611, after charging depreciation, debenture interest, proportion of removal expenses, &amp;c,, and £5OO was brought forward. There was no ordinary dividend for either that period or for 1909-10, but 5 per cent. was paid for 190 S-9, after taking £5,000 from the reserve fund. Union of Clerks.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National Union of Clerks was held at the Labour Exchange on Tuesday, evening. The chief busi- ness being making final arrangements for the Mass Meeting of Clerks, to be held in the Co-operative Hall, Warrington, this Saturday, evening, at 7.30. At a special conference held in London on March 2nd, it was decided that steps should be taken to become an approved Society under the Insurance Act. In January 243 new members were enrolled and four new branches formed Leytonstone, Small Heath, South Leeds, and P.L.A. No. 2. The Hackney Borough Council have adopted our minimum wage of 355. for all clerks in their employ at the age of 21. Will of the late Mr. David Garnett. The will of the late Mr. David Garnett, of West- .clifferroad, Birkdale, a member of the firm of Robert Garnett and Sons, catinet makers, Sankey-street, Warrington, has been proved at £85,187. Mr. Garnett was a prominent local Wesleyan Methodist, and was closely identified with Warrington and Penketh for many years, but had resided latterly at Birkdale, Mr. Garnett bequeathed £5O to the Wesleyan Mission carried on at the Charles Garnett Memorial Hall, Liverpool ; £5O to the Wesleyan Methodist Mission carried on at the Central Hall, Manchestsr ' • £5O to the Leysian Mission, City-road, London ; and £5O to Dr. Stephenson's Children's Homes, Bonner-road, London, N.B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Boy's Foot Severed.</head>
        <p>Boy's Foot Severed.</p>
        <p>Richlrd Harley (16), of Back Green-street, War- rington, met with an accident at the Whitecross Wireworks, on Wednesday afternoon. It appears he slipped in front of a works locomotive which passed over his leg, cutting off the foot near the ankle. Picture Pleasures. The chief attractions at the popular Picturedrome next week are" Vanity Fair," a dramatic representa- tion of Thackeray's noveLifor the first three days, and "The Red Cross Martyr" for the rest of the week, Madame Sarah Bernhardt, in "La Dame Aux Camellias "—the picture of the age—is coming! Bohemian Concert. The Warrington branch of the rail aay clerks association, held a Bohemian concert at the Lion Hotel on Saturday, the spacious ballroom having been kindly lent by Mr. Lee for the occasion. An excel- lent programme was submitted. Songs were given by Mr J E Hannan, Mr H Nurse, Mr J T Stout, Mr 11 Lee (Liverpool), Mr S Aukland (humorist), and the Excelsior quartette. Mr H A Roberts was the accompanist. The company, numbering upwards ej 200 members and lady friends, separated about 10 30 p m., after spending a most enjoyable evening Warrington P.S.A. The Warrington P.S.A. Society have transferred their headquarters from the Co-operative Hall to the Park Picture Palace where the first meeting was held on Sunday afternoon. Mr. Starkey presided over a large attendance and gave an address on "Selfishness.' Mr. F. Walker read the lesson, Mr . Gleave offered prayer and Mr. Nelson gave an interesting address taking for his text " There was a sharp rock on one side and a sharp rock on the other side." •He made reference to the present cri,is in England and said there could be no system of true progress without Christianity. Vocal items were ably rendered by Miss FlenunB, and Mr. McArthur, and the P.S.A. band •were responsible for an enjoyable seleotion. Shunter's Death. Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inqiiiry at Warring- ton on Monday int) the circumstances surrounding the death of Ernest Ashurst, shunter, 26, years of age, 15, Oakwood Avenue, who died as the result of an accident at the works of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co. Ltd. Mr. H. Greenall represented the firm, and Mr. W. J. McCaghey, H.M. Inspector of Factories, Was also present. John Greaves, shunter, 5, Cyril-street said that about 420 on February 16th he was engaged with eceased in tail-chaining. After unlooping a waggon from the engine, they noticed that it was going at a great speed. Deceased went round the front of the waggon, whilst witness went round the back. As he was crossing, deceased slipped on the metals and the waggon passed over his legs.—ln answer to Mr. IfcCaghey, witness sail it would have been impracticable to have shunted the waggon with the engine.--The Coroner said Ashurst had lost his life in his zeal for.hisemployers.—A. verdict of "Accidental death" was returned, the jury expressing their sympathy with the widow and children of the deceased.--Mr. Greenall and the Coroner concurred in the expression of sympathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>GLADSTONE JUNIOR! THE G 0.M.;-s--GRANDSON AT WARRINGTON. SPEECH TO LIBERALS LAST NIGHT.</head>
        <p>GLADSTONE JUNIOR! THE G 0.M.;-s--GRANDSON AT WARRINGTON. SPEECH TO LIBERALS LAST NIGHT.</p>
        <p>The visit to Warrington last evening of Mr. W. G. ,C. Gladstone, M.P., grandson of the revered " Grand old man" of Liberalism, was the occasion of a great meeting in the Cc-operative Hall last evening. It was the first public demonstration held by the Junior Liberal Association, since its recent re-organization and the members had entered into the arrangements with enthusiasm. The only regret was that a prior booking of the Parr Hall pre- vented that building being the scene of the gathering A crowded attendance was presided over by Mr ELloyd Jones (chairman of the association), who was sup- ported by Mr P AI Oliver (prospective Liberal candi- date for the Knutsford Division), Mr C E Parker (president of the Liberal Association), A 'dermal) James Evans, Councillors H Roberts and J T Locker, Messrs P Peacock, II G Roberts, G Toulmin, P H Brown, P Delaney, It Delaney, H New, E A Sanders and others.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRMANT announced the receipt of a telegram from the Chief Whip, announcing that the Conference with reference to the coal strike as still sitting They had hope and faith, continued Mr Jones, in the wise and</p>
        <p>PATIENT STATESMANSHIP Or Tan PRIME 3IINInTER in this national difficulty - a difficulty which no previous Prime Minister had ever experienced. They hoped his efforts would soon bring about a settlement by consent. (Applause). Mr. GEORGE Tout/vim moved a resolution express- ing approval of the work of the Government in passing the Parliament Act and the National Insurance Act, and its unabated confidence in the Prime Minister and the Cabinet, in their determination to pass into law measures granting self-government to Ireland, Dia- establishment of the Church in Wales, and Adult Suffrage. Mr. RICHARD DELANEY seconded.</p>
