      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE, M.P.</head>
        <p>MR. W. G. C. GLADSTONE, M.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. C. GLADSTONE, M.P.. who was welcomed with loud and prolonged cheering, began with a personal note by recalling that Mr. P. M. Oliver and he were fellow students at Oxford. Referring to the prolongation of the coal strike, he said we might turn</p>
        <p>THESE GREAT INDUSTRIAL CRISES to some account if we set our winds to the task of reflecting that our community in its present stage was not furnished or provided, as it should be, with machinery for settling industrial disputes of such magnitude as this. "I do not think" continued Mr Gladstone. "it is a hopeless task that such machinery should be estab- lished. It has been established in other countries, in those great democratic communities—our dominions over-seas. All of the legislation, I daresay, is not of a type which would be successful in this country but there is machinery for settling industrial disputes in Canada which recommends itself to all those who desire to see the likelihood of the possibility of the stoppage of the nation's industries being abated if not removed." Mr. Gladstone then detailed the leading features of the Canadian Industrial Disputes Investi- gation Act which is limited in its scope to those industries or occupations which are absolutely vital to the commercial life of the nation. The indus- tries. included steamships, telephones and telegraphs, gas, electric light, water and power works, and coal mines. The principle upon which the Canadian system was based was that the resources of arbitration and conciliation must first be exhausted before recourse was had to a strike or lock-out. (Applause). In 83 cases out of 04 the Board of Arbitration had been successful in bringing about an agreement, although the acceptance of its findings was not com- pulsory. The Board was a very simple affair, indeed, it was not at all like the cumbrous and rather ' elephantine Industrial Ci uncil which the Govern- ment had set up in this country. It was composed of three persons—one chosen by the employees, one by the employers, and a third by the two already selected. If the Board failed to thing the parties together it submitted its recommendations to the Minister of Labour who published them fyr the information of the public. Before arbitration had been tried, or during the arbitration proceedings, a strike or lock-out was unlawful, and if either the employees struck or the employers enforcea a lock-out, they rendered themselves liable to a fine.</p>
        <p>THE CANADIAN ACT continued Mr. Gladstone, was a very happy illustration of the efficiency of leaving the people of a country to settle their own affairs. (Applause) If Canada had never had self-government she would probably have been ruled by the same ideas as we had with regard to industrial disputes, and she would never have worked out for herself legislation which, be submitted was a very distinct advance upon what we had got in this country. Wherever we had tried it, the experi- ment of permitting the people of a country to manage their own affairs had been successful. And yet England still denied the right t) manage their own. affairs to</p>
        <p>TILE HUSH rEort.r. Neither Scotland nor Wales had ever withheld their consent to Ireland's demand. The reason lay in the fact that Scotland and Wales were themselves dis- tinctive parts of Great Britain and were better able to understand what it was like to be gwerned by a Parliament that wa., not always successful in under- standing their peculiar characteristics, feelings and conditions.</p>
        <p>" Why is it" asked Mr. Gladstone, "that we still find opponents to Home Rule for Ireland? It always Etrikes me that underlying the whole point of view of our opponents, there is current a misconception of the part that has been played by the Home Rule or Nationalist party in the politics of Ireland and England respectively and relatively. There is a tendency to regard the Irish party as the party that keeps Ireland disturbed, agitated and discontented, as the party that in the past has been implicated in incidents not wholly complimentary to it, as the party dosed with orisinal and continual sin to a double extent, as a party, in fact, but for which British nile in Ireland would have been successful. I should like to submit an estimate of the part played by the Nationalist party in Irish politics, an estimate which does them more justice and is far more accurate. I submit that</p>
        <p>THU HOME RULE PARTY DAS SAVED miAsT, from she worst consequences of the crime and till, blunder—to gD tho words of that great historian who arse flied a Unionist member of Parliament— which was perpetrated by the Act of Union. During During the time when Ireland was seething with violence, disorder, crime and discontent, in the days of ' the Fenian movement —the physical force movement— the Nationalist party had steadily held out to the people of Ireland both the means and the hope of remedying the grievances of Ireland not by force, not by the use of firearms, assassination, or crime, but by means of the Imperial Parliament at Westminster. Their central object has alwaya been and is to-Jay to remedy the grievances of Ireland by Parliamentaryraction." (Applause.) The Nationalist Party, Mr. Gladstone proceeded had taken the constitutional course. They said that if they sustained the demand, the British people in the end would be sure to see that justice was done to them. (Applause.) The Sinn Fein movement in Ireland subsided for the reason that the Home Rule party stood in the way and killed it. It was a calumny to call the Irish party a party of self- seekers, when they had imposed upon themselves a. self-denying ordinance which he had never heard of any other piriy imposing upon themselves. If t -morrow at were thought expedient by either of the great parties in the State to secure the support of the Irish members in Westminster by putting into office one or more of the leaders of the Irish party, those leiaders would never 'ask what the office was. They would refuse it at once, because they were under a self-denying ordinance to accept no office of profit or Per until the grievances of their country were remedied. The Nationalists as a party had been the one efficient barrier against Fenianism and the physical force movement and yet all the gratitude they got was, 15) far as the object for which they went to Westminster was concerned, to be ignored and trampled upon. There were some people whom it never seemed to strike that the 'rush party had a clear claim on their consideration and sympathy. For nearly half a century the Irish party had repre- sented to us the sustained demand of the overwhelming majority of the Irish people for self-government. (Applause.) The pernicious antagonism between our people and Ireland did great hirm to the industrial and economic welfare of the country. Sufficient stress was not laid upon the aspect of</p>
        <p>HOME RULE AS AN INDUSTRIAL QUESTTON. There was no doubt that Home Rule would bring to Ireland a great extension of industries and would considerably develop her prosperity in all directions. How could they have a country approaching any degree of prosperity when the railways were in such an unsatisfactory condition as the various Commissions had reported them to be in Ireland? An Irish Parliament could not sit for a fortnight in Dubin without taking measures to improve that system. (Applause.) The pernicious antagonism of the Irish people towards a system of government did great harm to the moral character of the people as a people, because such antagonism taught a people habits of lawlessness and discontent. No country would ever attain its maximum of industrial prosperity or economic development so long as there existed in that country a system of government to which the people of that country were antagonistic. (Hear, hear.) He appealed to their political opponents to agree with the Government to seize the first possible moment to end that antagonism. —(applause)— and extend to the Irish people sympathy and consideration for their legitimate aspira- tions and sensitive feelings. He suggested the time had come when they should set themselves to work , for the ambitions of the Irish people for self-govern- ment by which they would secure a greater moral and material strengthening of the uni...n between the two countries. (Loud applause). The Opposition to Home Rule was not inspired by democracy. It was estimated when the movement was first set on foot in practical legislative form that five-sixths of the Libe al Peers of that time were opposed to it, as were also four- fifths of the big employers of labour and men of social position. It was further estimated by his grandfather— (loud applause)—that with regard to independent workingmen, only. about one twentieth of them wet e opposed to it. That showed that the oppos- ition to the movement had been what might be called an aristocratic opposition. (Hear, hear.) Had the democracy been opposed to the cause it would have been wiped out and the party which espoused the policy would have been absolutely destroyed. " Happily for Ireland" concluded Mr Gladstone, "the democracy of this country has shown a superior judgment to that of the aristocracy, and the other sections of the community, and when the history of the whole movement comes to be written the credit for carrying through the settlement of the Irish question, in accordance with the wishes of the Irish people, will go, not to the men of rank nor to the men of money,. but to the democracy of this country. (Loud and prolonged cheers.) MESSAGE room MR. CROSFIELD. The following telegram from Mr. A. Ff. Crosqeld, who is staying at Cannes, was then read : "Only absence at this great distance prevents me joining in enthusiastic welcome to grandson of great and famous 7 statesman who honours us with visit to Workington to-night. All go:,d wishes. Croefleld." (Loud cheers). Mr P M OLIVER in the course of an interesting , address said the Liberals of Warrington seemed to have survived the lures of the damping exhibition— (laughter)—the Shakespearian recitals of Mr Harold Smith—(laughter)—and the effects of a little place called South Manchester. (Laughter.) Notwithstanding an accident like that in South Man- chester the Government continued to bold the fort-, and it would take the enemy a lot of time to get them out of it. (Applause.) The ammu- nition the Tories were using at present was rot very good—tle bullets were dum-dums and, according to the laws of war, ought not to be used —but fortunately, their powder was damp. (Laughter.) While there was so much mi,conception and misrepre- sentation with regard to the Insurance Act, they knew well that they would have to face, no doubt one or two little accidents, but they were perfectly willing to face them. (Applause.) Mr. F. E. Smith went to South Manchester and delivered a scathing attack on the measure although be had previously declared that it would be a great misfortune to the country if it did not become law. (Applause), Councillor IL ROBERTS said Conservatives had told him that the time was coming when he would be on their side, that Liberals and Con- servatives would have to join forces—against what? Against the people? He thought he could say for a few Liberals whom he knew that that day had not begun to dawn or even to be thought of. (Applause.) The great difference between the Liberal and Tory parties was this : The Tory party was the party of the classes and sought to retain priviliges for the classes. The Liberal party was the party that recog- nised a commos right on the part of evezy man born in this land to have at least an equal oppor- tunity so far as the laws of England were concerned. (Applause.) Referring to the Dumping Exhibition Councillor Roberts said its promoters tried to, persuade people to believe that everything the foreigner made was bad and that everything good was English. They might therefore have offered a better inducement to psople to go to the show than foreign clocks. (Laughter cud applause). The resolution was carried unanimously. A vote of thanks was passed to the Chairman and speakers on the proposition of Mr P H BRovv:sr, seconded by Mr P DELANEY, jun.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>CA. _...IRITED HORSE. CHAUFFEUR FINED FOR NEGLECT OF SIGNAL.</p>
        <p>John Gordon flibbert, chauffeur, Little Saughatl, Chester,was summoned at the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday, for failing stop when signalled to do so by a person in charge of a horse and van and, on February 16th, at Daresbury.—Defendant, who was represented by Mr Churton, of Chest, r, pleaded not guilty. William Green, a vanman in the employ of Messrs W Monks and Sons, Bridge-street, Warrington, said that at about 5.20 p.m. on the date in question. he was going in the direction of Warrington from Daresbury, when be saw a motor car coming at a good speed from the direction of Warriniton. He signalled to the driver, but the car did not stop, and as it passed, his horse swerved a little and the car caught the cart throwing it over. The car pulled up in fifty-nine yards The cart was damaged and the horse unnerved, and he was shaken. Cross-examined: The horse was a spirited One, but was not restive that day. If it had not swerved there might have been a collision. A boy named Fairclough, who corroborated, admitted that the horse was "a bit frightened of motors." The defendant, in evidence, said he sounded the horn and never saw the driver make any signal. After the accident Green told him no 61 +me was attached to either of them. The car was going at about sixteen miles an hour.</p>
        <p>Defendant was fined 105 including costs. YOUTHS' BAD RECORD.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on -Thursday Albert Antrobus and Percy Antrobus, 70, Oldham-street, were charged with breaking in and stealing from the house of Mr. Ai thur Rogers, postman, DG, Oldham-street, on Saturday, March sth, a quantity of cakes and sweets.—The Chief Constable stated that the house was left secure about 7-20 on Saturday last, but at nine o'clock it was found that someone had teen in the house as several doors were open. On a further examination it was found that a window at the back had teen broken open and the glass carefully taken out and 14id on one side. The prisoner Percy h.d admitted that he and his brother had been to the shop, whilst the elder prisoner denied the statements and said his brother had concocted the story. They had been on remand since Monday and in the meantime other inquiries bad been made and it was found that the two prisoners had broken into the shop. Since Monday the elder prisoner had admitted that he had taken the goods. Couilauteg, the Chief Constable said that was a case where something ought to be done with the elder boy who had been placed under Mr Firth not long ago. They ought to send him for trial with a view to core- reit tat fr R term of Borstall treatment. He wool' ask thatthe other boy be sent to a reformatory. The case was ognal iernanded for seven days. RECKLESS MOTORING. TAXI DRIVER HEAVILY FINED. At the Warrington Police Court on Thursday, Samuel Myles, Park Villas, Edison-road, Patricroft, aas sum- moned for having driven a motor car at excessive speed on February 23rd.—Defendant did not appear in Court. The Chief Constable said be had received a telegram from hire =eying that be could not get a train but defen- dant had his own taxi-cab and he,(the Chief) did not sea why he should not have put in an appearance. P.O Kenwright stated that about four o'clock on the day in question he was in Knutsford-road when the motor car rushed past him at a terrific rate. Evidence was also given to the effect that the defendant was under the influence of drink and further evidence was given as to the speed of the vehicle which was estimatedat between 50 and-40 miles an hour. The read aces crowded at the time. A fine of £5 and costs was imposed.—Defendant was also summoned for baying committed a similiar offence in Liverpool-road on the same day.—Mr W Williams stated that he was coming along the Liver- pool-road in the direction of Warrington when he saw the motor car coming at the rate of 40 miles per hour. The defendant was laughing and talking with someone else in the car and it crashed into the oft wheel of the cart he (witness) CM in charge of. The impact threw ore of the occupants out of his seat in the car. He was sure the defendant was drunk. The axle of the cart was broken. Further evidence was given as to defsndriat's condition Pnd also the excessive rate he was travelling at.—A floe of £lO and costs was imposed, making £l5 and costs for both cases.</p>
        <p>, • ( MI 11 ill 0 RTS 04 MOTO ',ll AND SPT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in tho chair), Aldermen Tinnion and Bolton, Councillor Wilkinson and Messrs H Thornton, S Wright, W Roberts, T Locker and W</p>
        <p>Furness. ALLEGED THEFT OF RHUBARB.—Martin Rowan, Jockey-street, was charged with stealing 29 bundles of rhubarb valued at 16s, the property of Mr F Bailey, of 201, Winwick road, a stallholder in the Market. —Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—The Chief Constable said that the prisoner, who had been employed by the prosecutor, purchased two boxes of oranges for 18s and paid lOs on account. He went back to the Market in the afternoon and said half of the oranges were rotten and prosecutor told him to sell what he could. Prisoner then asked to be allowed to take 16s worth of rhubarb out to sell it. Prosecutor agreed and gave him the fruit,telling prisoner he would pay him :3s to 9s for selling it. Ile had, how- ever, never seen the prisoner since, and he had not returned the rhubarb or the money.—Prisoner, who said he had been ill and could not go to prosecutor, was ordered to pay the costs of the else, or in default 14 days' imprisonment. "RUM AND BLACK BEER."—Thomas Daley, Flag- lane, appeared in answer to charges of assaulting Detective Reddington and Alice James, a barmaid; also with having been drunk and disorderly and refusing to quit the Vine Tavern.—The Chief Constable stated that between 7 and 8 o'clock on Saturday night the prisoner along with three other men knocked glasses off the counter of the Midland Hotel, and struck the barmaid across the face. He afterwards went to the Vine Tavern. lie refused to leave and when arrested struck the officer and kicked him on the legs.—Mr. Steel, who defended, said the prisoner was a horse dealer in a big way of business and had been in Warrington five or six weeks. On Saturday about 12 o'clock at noon he met one or two friends who told him that rum and black beer would cure the cold which he had. He admitted the offences and said that the prisoner bad been atsolntely teetotal or two years previous to that occurrence.—Dsley was fined £5 including costs.</p>
        <p>WFDICFSDAY.—Bef ore Mr Charles White (in the chair) Councillor C Jackson Holmes and Mr W T A FAMILY THEFT.—Charles and Elizabeth Bate (man rnd wife) and Alice Domville, mother of the female prisoner, all of 38, Cockhedge-lane, pleaded guilty on remand to a charge of stealing a large quantity of wearing apparel to the value of £6 13s 3d, the property of Mrs Peers, 68, Buttermarket-street.—The Chief Constable stated that between the beginning of January and the end of February, Mrs Peers, who carried on business as a draper at the above address, began to miss articles of clothing. Soon after Christmas the woman Bate rented the rooms at the back of the shop and it was from that time that theclothing began to disappear The pollee were informed and it was found that the window of the shop was easily accessible and insecure. 'The prisoners had disposed of a large quantity of the goods at pawnbrokers. It would appear the man had been acting under the influence of the we man.— Previous convictions were proved against the woman. Domville and she was sent to rrison ifor six months ; the female prisoner Bate, for three months,and Charles Bate for two months.</p>
        <p>lIIRSDAY —Before Mr J Rymer Young (in the chair,) Me=ers W Roberts, W T Williams,J Upson, A J Jolley W Richardson arid Dr Naden. BOY'S 111EFTS FROM METERS.—Joseph Lawton (15), Lythgoes-lane was charged with stealing on Monday March 4th 2s. 9d. from a gas meter situAted in the residence of Elizabeth Beckett, 34, Lythgoes-lane and he was further charged with having committed a similar offence and stealing 4s, 64. from a meter fixed In the house of Alex Lawton, 21, Lythgoes-lace on January 11th. The Chief Constable said that Mr Lee, the meter inspector, had reported the matter to the police and on Saturday last the boy had admitted the offences.— The case was dismilsed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS-COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>RACES AT ST. HELENS TO-DAY. For the novice race starting at 3.30 p.m. seven teams have entered. Rylands W.R.C. Harriers will be repre- sented by E A Burton, Harold Holbrook, Thorniley, J VVarburton, A Whitmore and H Woods who have done well in recent inter-club contests and, if the wiremen have not erred in their calculations, should give the winners serious trouble. Warrington A.G. have selected NV J Bradbury, S Siddall, G Summerfield, G Wakefield, W Walters and T Wilson, all of whom are moving well for novice?,and very hopeful of maintaining the reputa- tion of the club. For the so-cilled Senior Race starting at 4.30 p.m., live teams have entered. Warrington A.C. will he represented by A Gregson, J Hands, T Rowlinson, E Scott, F Shadwell and W Thorniley, and even though the opposition inclndes the winning teams for the last two Northern Junior Championships, and other runners of note, the local lads expect to provide the best of ,hem with astern struggle, and are very earnest on bringing the honours to Warrington. Train leaves Bank Quay, low level, at 1 51 p.m. and vs the races take place from the Sutton Commercial Football Ground there will be little time to spare. Local runners are advised to assure themselves this morning that the train is expected to run and be prepared to leave earlierat one o'clock by a bus from the Park Gates.</p>
        <p>‘CoAarrisE,' Transparent Cement for Glass, China, ao. ed. and Is., post free.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN.</p>
        <p>EARNEST DEMAND FOR THE FRANCHISE.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON THE LARGEST BRANCH.</p>
        <p>The spring conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations was held on Wednesday in the Burton School, Blackburn. The morning session was given up to a discussion of organisation and propaganda work. The opinion was expressed that the views of the North of England need fuller expression on the National Women's Liberal Federation, and it was decided to recommend the Lancashire and Cheshire Associations to support the nominations of Lady Haworth and Mrs Walter Rea for places on the Executive Committee of the Federation. The delegates from Warrington were Mrs. Osborne (hon. secretary). Mrs. Lindley and Miss Booth. Mrs Stewart Brown (president) announced that the Warrington Association, of which Mrs. A. H. Crosileld is the president, is now the largest branch in the country. Up to a fortnight ago that honour was held by Blackburn.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Stewart Brown at the afternoon meeting, made a short speech, in which she spoke of the deplorable tactics of an extreme but small section of women suffragists. She hoped that Liberal women, while 1 recording their emphatic condemnation of the unwise tactics of a misguided few, would not cease to advocate the enfranchisement of women. They ought to be the more active in furthering the cause by constitutional means. She feared very much that the militant suffragists had made lukewarm supporters of the move- ment cold and had driven others into the ranks of their opponents. Mrs. Francis D. Acland moved a resolution expressing profound gratification that the Conciliation Bill is to be introduced in Parliament on March 22nd," and urging all Liberal members for Lancashire and Cheshire to support it, and also to vote for the amendment to the Electoral Reform Bill advoca'ed by Mr. Lloyd George, Sir E. Grey, Lord Haldane and other Cabinet Ministers. The resolution concluded : "This confer- ence fears if Liberal members fail to support these measures a large number of their most active workers will refuse any longer to support the Liberal party, to which they look to do justice to the claim for their Parliamentary enfranchisement." Miss Crosfield (Warrington), in seconding, urged that if women obtained the vote their wages would eventu- ally go up. Corroboration of that contention was to be found in Norway and Australia, where women were enfranchised. While they desired women to combine more and more, it might not be generally known that there was now a larger number of women in trade unions than there were men in trade unions when the men got the vote. In such organisations there were 201,000 women in 1908. but as a memberof the deputa- tion told the Premier, trade unions without the vote were like guns without bullets. The latest outbreak on the part of the militants had undoubtedly affected the cause, but she heped that those who had the welfare of women at heart would not relax their efforts on behalf of wornan's suffrage. She would like to remind critics that the late Lord Salisbury said :" I know of no reason at all against woman's suffrage." The character and intellect of women were an enormous rational asset, and ought to be brought into full play. Some of the muddles that distressed the country at present, such as the deplorable condition of slums and slum dwellers and the want of practical education might be due to the fact that there bad been only one, point of view represented. It was certain that the awful starvation and degradation of thcusands of their sister women was solely due to the circumstance that there had been no vote to protect women on the economic side. They were citizens, and partners with men in the Sts.te, consequently the welfare of the country concerned them every bit as much as it did men. It was not rightthat they should leave the concerns of the country wholly in the hands of men, who, not wishing to run them down in the least, were not archangels. It was the business of women to help in the running of the rountry and take their share in the responsibility of the State. As Mrs. Acland had said, the last part of the resolution not in the least in the nature of a threat. It was not intended as a threat. It was only a state- ment of what would happen. They were most loyal Liberals, and they held Liberalism very high, but they regarded their duty to the cause of woman suffrage higher still.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>The Night Side of London.</p>
        <p>A drama of the type of "The night side of London" has its own particular class of patrons and one cannot expect such a prcduction to be .popular with all theatre-goers .A play of this kind might open to severe criticism if it were not for the fact that the players portray the various characters in a more than ordinarily clever manner. Mr. C Watson Mills Company. which is appearing at the Royal Court Theatre this week, is a very powerful one and the artistes show much talent in parts which are not very palatable. The aim of the author has aeen to depict life in some of the shadiest quarters of the metropolis, as well as in the west end, and with the aid of such actors as Mr. Aidan Lovett, as the new evangelist and Miss Mary Mallalieu, as Esther Dorincourt, the girl with the odds against her, the Effort is successful. The other members of the company show an equal amount of skill in adapting themselves to the aim of the pro- duction which is to show the temptations and trials of young girls amongst the seething masses of un- sympathetic citizens which only London holds. It would probably be as well, if, instead of going into the details of the dra,ma, we confined cur space to dealing with the actors and actresses who are appearing in this sensational object play. The events of the story are dominated by the rapacious and unscrupulous Jew, Herod Steinburg, and Mr. Alfred Maurice makes the part as repellent as it must be to achieve the author's intention. Mr. Aldan Lovett puts consider able earnestness into his acting as the evangelist, though occassionally his treatment of the role seems somewhat perfunctory. Miss Vera Grafton as Naomi Merrick is a typical adventuress and accomplice of the Jew. Miss Madge Heyton as Magdale Fawcett gives a striking portrayal of the woman in peril. Skill in characterisation is shown by Mr. F. Thorpe Tracey and Mr. J. G. Maine as the blind aud dumb men, victims of the hideous Jew. Mr. Em Carlton as Curly Dabbs, Cockney chauffeur and Miss Irene T.Tracy as Slithers, are mirth provokers and actors of no mean order whilst Miss M.-Kitts, as Lelia La Brunne, a woman of pleasure, shares equally the applause for her imper- sonation of a woman of the streets. There is also. the jocular man about town—a character which is ably undertakk n by Mr. Frederick Arthur.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>NE-XT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NE-XT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>Next week the sensational drama "The Execu tioner's daughter " will be presented by Mr. Fred Little and Mr. George Slater's Co.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The verdict of all who have had the privilege of visiting the Hippodrome this week is "An excellent all-round show." With such an abundance of clever artis.es it is hard to decide which turn should have the premier position but after careful criticism no doubt the turn provided by Silbon's cats is the one which pleases and interests the audience most. A number of cleverly trained cats and dogs perform numerous difficult and amusing tricks and the one word to cover their antics is—marvellous. Indeed it is nothing short of a marvel that animals can be trained to such a pitch and perform feats in so easy and natural a manner. A feature if the performance is the looping of the human loop by one the felines which does not fail to elicit voluminous applause from the appreciative audience. Verno and Voyce score a decided success in their satirical scena " John Bull's Cash Box." In an interesting and humorous manner these artistes endeavour, and are successful, in show- ing to the audience how John Bull is interested in anything foreign but neglects his own "family. " Mr. Vern() makes up cleverly as an ever generous "John Bull " whilst his colleague disposes of the three other characters in the sketch with equal skill. Their rendi- tion of the song " Bread and meat " tnet with a large amount of applause. The four Casinos provide a terpsichorion act much above the ordinary, their steps being well chosen and neatly displayed. Gladys Mavius, the Caudian girl, sings two popular songs in such a breezy and pleasing manner that the audience cannot fail to appreciate. She also shows herself to be a no mean exponent of the art of step-dancing. As a siflleur mimic and Hebrew comedian Dave Bryant earns his quota of applause in a very clever manner. The llargreaves trio combine pathos and humour with a large amount of success in a very clever and original act. Harriett Rose, as a comedienne is a decided favourite but she would do well to confine her efforts to things humorous, as her rendition of a sentimental song did not call forth as much applause as did her effort in lighter vein. Last but by no means least comes the humorous cinamatograph production "Have it out " a story which tells of the sufferings and the dream of a man afflicted with that chronic complaint, toothache. If that is the way teeth are extracted we would much rather prefer to " Have them in."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>THE MUSIC OF NEXT WEDNESDAY'S CONCERT. Dvorak's " Stabat Mater."</p>
        <p>Antonin Dvorak, the composer of "The Stabat Mater,"—the principal work to be performed at the Musical Society's concert next week—was born in September, 1841, at MiThlhausen in Bohemia, and died suddenly in May, 1904. He was destined by his father for a butcher, a trade in which he was him- self engaged along with the business of an innkeeper, but a kindly fate willed it otherwise, with the result that to posterity has been bequeathed some music the extraordinary beauty and strong individuality of which are themes of world-wide admiration. Brought into contact, while quite ycung, with the bands of itinerant musicians who roamed his rative country, an ambition was aroused in Dvorak to devote himself to music, the freedom from conventionality and the leaning towards the romantic school, shown in much cf his work, attesting the influence of these early associations. A considerable portion of Dvorak's early life was spent at Prague where he enjoyed the close friendship of one Karel Bendl who assisted him greatly in his studies, the while he earned a livlihood as an organist. It was not until his 32nd year that Dvorak came before the public as a composer. The spontaneous and national character of the music of an early cantata, "The Heirs of the White Mountain, "ensured hi, success, yet curiously, the wildly unconventitnal music of a later work—an opera entitled " The King and the Collier "—caused it to be abandoned by reason of its impracticability, an untoward event which prompted Dvorak ultimately to re-write it, after which it met with a cordial recep- tion at Vienna. A friendship with Johannes Brahms, about this time, marks the appearance of Dvorak's series of Slav Dances, composed originally for the pianoforte though afterwards scored for the orchestra,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>in which later form they were played at the Crystal Palace London in February 1879. In these he had almost as great a success as had Bratarns with the Hungarian Dances,which had preceded them by several years. Dvorak's pcsition as a new and fully developed composer was now recognised and assured, and his career was henceforth watched with eager interest. The writer of a lucid article in Grove's Dictionary of Music says "the composer of the Slav Dances has no history—his history is to be read in his works, not in any biography." From this time forward Dvorak's lame became gradually more and more prominent. His "Stabat Mater "—composed as early as 1876, though not published until 1881—was introduced to the English metropolis in 1883 by an amateur association called the London Musical Society, and from 1881 Dvorak became a familiar personage in this country, he himsolf having conducted a successful performance of "The Stabat Mater" in that year. Later in the same year, after conducting at the Worcester Musical Festival, Dvorak received a commission to write a cantata for the next Birmingham Festival, with the result that "The Spectre's Bride" was added to the growing list of the composer's works Speaking of the characteristics of Dvorak's music genctally, and of "The Stabat Mater" in particular, the before-mentioned wi iter in Grove's Dictionary says : "An inexhaustible wealth of melodic invention and a tich variety of colouring are the qualities which most attract us, together with a certain unexpectedness from which none of his works are wholly free. The imaginative faculty is very strongly developed so that be is at his best vhen treating subjects in which the romantic element is prominent. His works in the regular classical forms are the least favourable specimens of his powers. When we con- sider the bent of his nature and the circumstances of his early life this is not to be wondered at ; the only wonder is that his c-ncerted compositions should be as numerous and successful as they are. None of Dvorak's earlier works for chorus gave promise cf what was to cane in "The Stabat Mater." Even musicians who knew his previous compositions can scarcely have expected his setting of the Latin Hymn to be full of the highest qualities which can be brought into requisition. Perhaps the most striking feature of his work is the perfect symyathy of his character with the spirit of the words. The Bohemian composer not only threw off all trace of his own nationality but adopted a style which makes it difficult to believe him not to have studied the best Italian models for a lifetime before setting pen to paper. We do neat mean for a moment to hint at any want of originality, for here as elsewhere the composer is indebted to no one for any part of his ideas. But in such numbers as the " Inflammatus " and others the Italian influence is unmistakable. It has been well remarked that he treats tr.() hymn from the point of view of "absolute music" ; that ia to say, that he dwells, not so much upon the meaning or dramatic force of a verse or idea, as upon the general emotion of the whole. It is this no doubt Iv hich leads him into an apparent disregard of the order and connection of the words of the hymn, though a more commonplace reason must, we fear, be assigned for the not infrequent false quantities in the Eetting of the Latin verse."</p>
        <p>. It should be noted that " The Stabat Mater" not, on Wednesday, be heard in conjunction with the Latin text In the irterests of a wider public to whom the miginal words do not appeal an English e valent or adaptation—for it can hardly be called, even a free translation—has been written to Dvorak's music by Mr. Fred J. W. Crowe under the title "At the foot of the Cross," and it is with this word basis that the Musical Scciety will present the work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DR. C. T. REYNOLDS'S " FOR ALL THE SAINTS."</head>
        <p>DR. C. T. REYNOLDS'S " FOR ALL THE SAINTS."</p>
        <p>Among the several items compiising the second I part of Wednesday's programme is an anthem, or I rather a chcrus with orchestra, " For all the Saints" which calls for special mention. It was first performed so recently as Dvcernber 7 last yelr, at the Eleventh Festival of the Liverpool Church Choir Association in St. George's Hall Liverpool, 11 a choir of some 500 voices with organ accompaniment and parts for brass instruments and di ums. Upon the adjudication of Dr. Cr J. Bennett, the eminent organist of I incoln Cathadral, " For all the Saints,"— a setting of Bishop Walsh= How's familiar hymn—was selected from nineteen compositions sent in anonymously for this Festival by local composers, and it proved to be the work of Dr. C. T. Beynolds, the organist of Christ Church, Claughton, Birkenhead, who personally con- ducted the initial performance. For the Warrington concert full orchestral parts have been specially scored by the composer, who will again conduct the work— one in which deep interest is, we believe, taken by all the Musical Society's members.</p>
        <p>ATTACK ON TRAMWAY CONDUCTOR. ST. HELENS COLLIERS FINED. The story of a savage attack on a tramway conductor by a number of colliers was related in the St. Helens Police Court on Monday, when William and James Ratcliffe, father and son, were charged with baying assaulted Thomas Iloghes,a conductor employed by the St. Helens Tramway Company. The evidence showed that on Friday night,March Ist, Hughes was conducting a Rainhill car. As it was crowded he would not allow the defendants to stand at the back, but ordered them upstairs. It was raining at the time. They came down again shortly afterwarde, and threatened him. When they got off the car at Thatto Heath they struck him, knocking four teeth out of his head and leaving him dazed and bleeding. while another man was picking Hughes up the younger man kicked him in the face. For the defence it was con- tended that the conductor struck the men, and that another man had assaulted him.</p>
        <p>The-deTe-n--din—ts;;;;wfiled-'27; and costs or 28 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <p>/0</p>
        <p>Sunday Observance. The Lord's Day Observance Society has sent to all the clergy and Nonconformist ministers their annual appeal for sermons, suggesting that such should be preached on the first Sunday after Easter (April 14th). Special reference is made to the increasing provision for pleasure-seekers and for military training on Sundays, and also to the unsatisfied state of the country in spite of increased benefits bestowed on the many— this being an only too sure consequence of disregarding the will of God. Preachers are urged to proclaim the pre-eminence of the Lord's Day. St. Barnabas' Bible Class. At the conclusion of the above class on Sunday a pleasing ceremony took place, the occasion being a presentation to Mr Sam Cburm, previous to his departure for Saskatchewan, Canada. The Vicar (Rev J lf 0 ifollingworth) spoke in feeling terms of Mr Churm's connection with the choir, Bible Class and Social Club, and wished him every happiness and prosperity in his new home. Mr B Dimelow, secretary made the presentation which took the form of a travelling rug, together with a brush and comb in case. lie thanked the recipient for his valuable assistance at all times, and,iu the name of the Bible Class and Social Club Mr Chnrm suitably responded. On Tuesday evening the members of the Social Club met to bid farewell to Mr Churrn previous to his departure the following day Gramaphone selections and songs were rendered at intervals. The Rev 0 A Coupe and Mr B Dimelow (secretary) spoke of Mr Churm's good work in connec- tion with the club, and Mr Churm feelingly thanked all present for their good vishes. Light refreslments were provided by the secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>English Church Union. The Discouragements of Officialism In _ _ Church Life.</head>
        <p>English Church Union. The Discouragements of Officialism In _ _ Church Life.</p>
        <p>The Warrington I r-anC-I; of the English Church Union held a public meeting in the Church House, Bridge- street, on Monday evening, when the Rev. C. E. Douglas, of Manchester, one of the originators of the Society of Faith, gave an interesting addms on "The Old Mother of England." The Rev. J. H. G. Hailing. worth (hon. secretary of the branch) presided over a good attendance which i.ncluded the Rector (Rev Canon Willis), the Revs. J. Holden, J. Collins, G. A. CJupe and Buckmaster.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN pointed out that the English Church Union stood for definiteness which was, above all things, necessary at the present time. It was by definite teaching that they would produce strong and robust members of the Church and be able to present a bolder front to the enemy. The attitude of the Church of England was often too full of compromise and he would urge those who believed in the Church taking a definite and determined stand to seriously consider joining the English Church Union. The Rev. C. E. DOUGLAS dealt with his subject in a very comprehensive way. He said the Caurch of England was by far the oldest institution in thia country—not simply becswe it dated from the days of our Saviour but because it was the oldest existing organization of men and women that was known in the realm of England. Having dealt with the early history of the Church in this lind, the speaker touched briefly upon the Establishment which, he said, was a treaty made between two independent powers—the Church of Christ and the realm of England. The last time the relations between the Church and the State were varied was in the reign of Henry \TM. Then the Church, acting under the instigation of a King of blessed memory and many wives—(laughter)—decided that the time had come for all appeals to go to the King and an Act was passed, called the Submission of the Clergy. It was then laid down that the clergy, instead of having the right to tax themselves should pay the same taxes as the laity, that all appeals should be heard by the King and that the Church of England should enjoy its full liberties. That was passed by Convocation and since then there had been no alteration. Ile (Mr. Douglas) spoke as a strong Radical and with him Dis- establishment was not a question of party politics but of national honour. As Churchmen they had entered into a treaty with the State awl one party to an agreement had no right to repudiate; It witbout the consent of the other, as long ns the other side was reasonable. To introduce into the House of Commons a Bill which professed to revise the Establishment by abolishing it, without introducing it into Convocation, was the greatest possible breach of honour that any nation could be guilty of. (Applause.) It was reported that the Government had been very much affected by the protests of Literal Churchmen—so much so that instructions were given that the Bill to be presented to Parliament should be varied to meet their objections, but after a fortnight's work (so the gossip ran), the draughtsmen reported that it, could not be done. The principal difficulty in the way was that the Government had no right to dismember the province of Can erbury without the consent of the Church. (Applause.) Having elaborated this point, Mr. Douglas went on to emphasise that the Church of England was an institution that stood quite distinct from the State—in fact the constitution of the State was modelled on that of the Church. The government of the Church as a co-operate body was committed to the bishops as the successors of the Apostles, but every member of the Church, by the Spirit that was in him, must guide his own devotions and service in the ranks of Christ's church and live according to his conscience. There was no place in the world where a man had so much freedom of thought, expression and action as in the Church of England. There was no single cause of any importance within the last 300 or 400 years that had not begun in the Church of England, that had not been persecated at the start and then finally the officials had moved forward and taken possession of it. There always settled on any human organisation in the course of time a sort of mould or fungus of officialism and those officials were dead against anything that was going to upset the nice, comfortable, settled order of affairs. From the bottom of his heart he believed no Church could ever be happy if it stood still ;it must be moving forward. Within the last 150 years we had had a great Protestant revival in England,headed by John Wesley,a priest of the Church What did they do to him? They fired him out. Now they would give all the endowments not only of the Church of Wales but of the Church of England if they could get back the enthusiana for Christianity that was lost by driving John Wesley out of the Church. The lecturer also instanced the official attitude of suspicion and censure towards the leaders of the Oxford move- ment, the result being that Newman and Manning were turned out. Was it not ridiculous, he asked, that people were not to be allowed to think and act without officials trying to squash the life that was within them? It was for such liberty that the English Church Union existed. One of the great drawbacks they had suffered from in the past had been the perversity of officials— whether they were political officials given high office in the Church or religious cfficials—in attempting to interpret the law according to their own individual wishes. The government of the Church was a free democracy of the people of God. The Spirit of God was in every confirmed man and woman, and eaca of them could move a reform in the Church, just as much as if they bad a seat in Convocation. Anybody could second it, and steadily theething would move through the living Church, from soul to soul, heart to heart, and brain to brain, until it became a custom and slaw of the Church of England. That was the Church they looked forward to and that was the reason why every man and woman should join some society that represented the views he or she held. No Churchman was doing his duty if he stood between two stools. It was by p:ople who had princinlex and stood up for them, who wanted to see things altered and set straight, that the Church was reformed. (Applause). The Rev. J. HOLDEN, in moving a vote of thanks to the speaker, said the vagaries of bishops constituted their great difficulty. The members of the English Church Union were often regarded as extreme and dangerous people, but they stood for the beautiful moderation of the Prayer Book. Mr. E. H. COATES seconded. He expressed regret that more men were not present and said too many were only nominal Churchmen and did not take the trouble to study the history of the Church to which they belonged. The vote was heartily passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>Preaching on Sunday evenine from the word "Son thy sins are forgiven" (St. Mark, ii. 5), the (Rev. W. Reid claimed for man a tripartite life as mysterious as that of the Triune God, and denied the special creation of the soul or spirit, man's life being one, and Jesus the Saviour not of men's souls or bodies, but of men. He asserted that if not correct to say that all suffering in life is consequent on sin—not necessarily the sin of the individual afflicted—at least all suffering that is not the outcome of imperfection is the result of sin, and that sin in the life must inevitably communicate itself to body, that sin is always lose, and a loss which mere forgiveness cannot replace, that sickness besides being a judgment and warning, is one of God's greatest gifts as an index, and a protest against the violence offered to life, that Christs power, or In other words, the aspect of His forgiveness which is more visible, is needed to break the paralysis of the will, that power is infused into the life will communi- cate itself to the body, and become the nucleus of a new life, and that in recovery is seen a sacrament of God's forgiveness. He claimed the ministry of healing to be one side of God's work of Redemption, and as shown in Christ's mission to be inseparable from all other aspects of it. On Thurday evening last week, Dr. Ferguson presided over a large gathering, when the Rev A Farquhar, of Longsight, Machener, delivered a most interesting lecture entitled " Bonnie Prince Charlie,' reminiscent of the Jacobite rising of 1745. Most graphically he told the story, pointed with racy joke and quotation, from the landing of Charles Edward ou the coast of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WAR ftfIGTOPI. 41( ' hurch Pir inio9 r-</head>
        <p>WAR ftfIGTOPI. 41( ' hurch Pir inio9 r-</p>
        <p>.-e</p>
        <p>a</p>
        <p>la</p>
        <p>(A,</p>
        <p>Inverness up to his escape, and death in Rome. The views, and the Jacobite songs excellently rendered by Mrs Miller and Mr Farquhar, greAtly added to the interest and insttuction of the lecture. Mrs Snailham accompanied, and Mr B S Owen operated the lantern Brick-street Mission.</p>
        <p>The married people in connection with the above mission held their sectional effort on Thursday last week, which took the form of a tea party and concert, when about 150 sat down to tea. After tea a capital programme was gone through, presided over by 111 r Jamts Harding, who informed those present that the proceeds of their effort were toward renovating the church at a very early date. A moss enjoyable evening was spent. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. The members of this society celebrated its 19th anniversary on Sunday. The Mayor, who presided, spoke of the pleasure it gave him to be present at their 19th anniversary of the society. Speaking upon the question of ignorance he said it was one of the greltest Causes of evil, and he hoped that all right-thinking men, especially in a brotherhood like this, would put forth every effort to reach and improve the lot and conditions of slum life. He deeply regretted the absence of the Rev Herbert Dunnien, of Liverpool, who was unable to be present as he could find no suitable train. Mr E Wynne Jones kindly consented to 1111 the gap and spoke upon "Prayer," dealing with Mr Slatchford's objection to it because it was unnatural, unscientific, unworthy and unavailable. Taking each objection in turn, Mr Jones asked what was more natural than to pray, especially on the part of the man who was in trouble and great dancer. The close communion of the soul with God availed much. Prayer was the mighty lever for the soul, life, prosperity and safety of the nation. Miss Sarah Silcock rendered a solo" Hear ye, 0 Isreel,"and a duet by Miss Silcock and Mr Dutton, " Watchman, what of the night ? " was much enjoyed. Mr McEachern and Mr A Healey took part in the meeting and Mr W II Payton presided at the organ.</p>
        <p>• .111.4.9,</p>
        <p>417.* 0 ollt, • a. • Alb 0. IMO/ Vf/I , I 0 ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Rev. H. Dunnico's Attack on the Free Church Council.</head>
        <p>Rev. H. Dunnico's Attack on the Free Church Council.</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Dunnico (formerly pastor of Golborne- street Baptist Church, Warrington), preaching at Kensington Baptist Church, Liverpool, on Sunday even- ing,referred to the recent meetings of the National Free Church Council, and said that a more grotesque and absurd utterance than that of the Rev. Dr. Dixon, of Spurgeon's Tabernacle, had surely never been made on a representative platform. The attack that Dr. Dixon made upon the late Charles Darwin was void of sense. Charles Darwin was just as much a prophet of God as Dr. Dixon, and a far more reliable one. If history proved one thing more clearly than another, it was that herever theology had come into conflict with gener- ally accepted opinions of scientific and learned men, theology had been proved wrong. Surely in days like the present, when the challenge for a restatement of faith in terms acceptable to the modern mind was never so urgent and the work of keeping the most intelligent section of the community in tourth with churches was never more difficult, it was a colossal blunder to allow such arrant nonsense to be proclaimed upon a representative platform. Referring to the discussion by the Council on social questions, Mr Dunnico said that futile discussions and pious resolutions would not blind the eyes of demo- cracy to the fact that the National Free Church Council was a party caucus,controlled and subsidised by wealthy capitalists who exploited it for party political purposes, aed in return were singled out for party hormrs. The very men selected to discuss social problems were for the most part ministers of wealthy churches, without any really practical first-hand knowledge of the subject they dismssed. As a matter of fact they were chosen to deal with such questions because they were known in official circles as " safe " men. For a free church minister to identify himselfwith the working-class move ment meant ostracism and boycott of a most despicable kind. It was true that an occasional Labour M.P. was invited to address the conference as a sort of decoy duck for the democracy, but to all intents and purposes the Free Church Council was a mere wing of the Liberal party—and a retrogressive one at that—lacking almost entirely the brave spirit that had once made Nonconformity a power in the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>New Church.</head>
        <p>The New Church.</p>
        <p>"The Second Coming: How Accomplished and When."</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the West Lancashire Mission of the New Church, the Rev. W. H. Claxton, Vice- president of the New Church Conference, commenced a second series of Lectures in the Masonic Hall, on Wednesday evening. There was a small but interested audience. There was no subject, he said, which showed in so striking a manner the line of demarcation between the old and the new, as this of the Second Coming. The various sections of the Christian Church agreed in looking forward to it as a future event. The New Church regarded it as one that was already accom- plished, and these two positions were irreconcileable. The Second Coming, the Last Judgment, and the End of the World were declared in Matthew xxiv. and xxv. to synchronize. From the establishment of the Christian Church these events had been looked forward to with a mingled feeling of eager anticipation and dread. The Apostles of our Lord had regarded this event as likely to occur within their own lives. The Second Coming and the end of the world however did not occur in their day. About the year 1,000 the world, and particularly the English rot igious world, was thrown into a state of great excitement by the belief that the end of the world was near at hand, and the ordinary duties of life were neglected by many, Still later the prophecies of the Bev. Mr. Baxter and others had aroused fresh excitement and anticipation. But all these human prophecies had been destined to be falsified by events, and the Christian world had sunk into—he would not say a state of unbelief,—but a state of indifference front which it was very difficult to rouse it. This state of indifference was very irrational if Christians really did believe that the prophecy of the Second Coming was still unfulfilled. In that case the Lord had yet to comer and as he declared he would Come like a thief in the night, and that when ho came he would not find faith on the earth, and there would be a danger that he would be unrecognized, it behoved Christian believers to be constantly on the watch lest He should come without recognition. And this was the claim of the New Church, that He had so come,and that just as at the firat Advent, He was in the world and the world knew Him not, He had come unto His own and I His own had received Him not, the light was shining in the darkness and the darkness comprehended it not. Dealing with the orthodox conception of the Second Coming, the lecturer said the belief in the resurrection of the material body was now generally discountenanced, having been seen to be scientifically untenable, and the idea of a last judgment as a kind of legal assize at which ledgers of men's deeds, kept by a recording angel, to aid the Divine remembrance, would be opened, was really too childish a conception altogether of this great event. The New Church presented a different view which relieved the mind of contradictions, and harmonized Scripture with itself, with reason, science and comm,,n sensP. This claim was that the Second Coming was coming in Spirit, and not in person, by the unfolding cf the spiritual or Divine Wisdom of the Word, the Lord having appeared to Emanuel Swedenborg about the riddle of the 18th century, that "bankrupt century " as Carlyle the great writer had termed it, when Intl lelity was rampant in England and on the Continent and coin. missioned him to make known this spiritual sense and the doctrines of the New Dispensation unto the world. According to this view the Second Coming was not in the clouds of the physical heavens, but in the clouds of the literal sense of the Word, which ot soured the glory of the Divine sun, and yet was the essential vehicle of Divine revelation, drawn by Divine power from the minds of men. In the Word the Lord could thus be scen by the " eye "of the mind—the understanding—of every man, and this the British and Foreign Bible Society was rendering possible by the dissemination of the Bible at the rate of over: 0,000,000 copies a year in more than 400 different languages. The lecturer concluded with a brief explanation of the nature of the Last Judgment as a general judgment similar to that accomplished by the Lord at His first coming, in the interior spiritual world, unseen by man but removing the pressure ma d the force and power of ths evil of that world from human life, working the redemption of all from the powers of darkness, and restoring to man liberty and power to live in accord- ance with the Divine Will, and thus to obtain salvation. The extent of the topic caused the lecturer to leave little time for questions, though several were asked and briefly answered. The lecturer promised to deal with these further next week and to leave more time for discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <p>R</p>
        <p>'S</p>
        <p>£7</p>
        <p>'6,</p>
        <p>PHILONIATHIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>CANON STEVENSON ON "IDYLLS OF THE KING."</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warringtoo Philomathic Society, which• was held at the o.ld, Academy on Wednesday evening, was of spec' interest, a large attendance of members and friends being favoured with a paper by the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson on "Idylls of the King." The lecturer dealt in a very interesting and lucid manner with the inception, decline and dissolution of the Order of the Round Table, giving special prominenog to ''Gareth and Lynette" and "The passing of Arthur. He also emphasised the spiritual teaching of Tennynol' Of all Tennyson's works, said Canon Stevenson, none had endeared themselves more to his readers than the "Idylls of the King." The charm of their story, the beauty of thought, the nobility of language: the perfection of description and of metaphor, had ail combine Ito make up poems unsurpassed of their kind. They set forth the ancient story of King Arthur, a story at least a thousand years old. How IS arose none could tell. Whether Arthur was to he regarded as a historical or legendary person bad been long debated and never decided. Tennyson hinosel; seemed to have believed in a real Arthur who liveu about 500 AD, and defeated his enemies in Wales. There was, he (the lecturer) thought, no doubt that the story was allegorical. Tennyson said so himself, though he deprecated the way in which some inter' preted its meaning. He did not, as was the ease io some of the old romances, invent adventures for the sake of the allegory, but took an existing story ani breathed his own conception into it. " Gareth anu Lynette" showed us the Order at its best. It was the golden age. The King sat in the great hall 14 Camelot, with his knights ranged about his throne. Clear honour, shining like a dewy star of dawn, And faith in their great king with pure Affection, and the light of victory And glory gain'd and evermore to gain. To him came the oppressed, the wronged,the weak, 03 as each tale was told, a knight rode forth to deliver the oppressed and to punish the wrong-doer. The "Idyll of the Holy Grail" occupied a central position, acting as a test cf the knights and showing how some, noble members of the Order were still to be fonou while others were sinking lower and lower into bass' ness. Referring to the passing of the King whose work was done, the lecturer said Aithur went ward to to his last great battle. His mind wts heng with the thoughts of erring wife, false friend anu ruined Order. We could not wonder that for a moment his _ faith was shaken. It was bard to understand wh .</p>
        <p>all these things should be, but if for a moment ti cloud was dark it soon rolled away, and he felt th though he might pass away, yet life, true life, rat" than death, awaited him in the future. Was his wo and his life a failure ? It seemed s to him at ti time, but no pure and noble life was ever lived vain, and the story of King Arthur, ba it fact fiction or somewhat of both, had been an inspiration many and would be to many more, as long as the ts was told. (Applause). Mr. JAMES B kLI, (president of the society), in pr . •</p>
        <p>, . posing a vote of tha'ziks to the lecturer, said the socie bad reason to be indebted to Canon Stevenson f' considerable help and encouragement, and he fe sure the members and friends present were happy I have had the opportunity of listening to so expert g interpreter of Tennyson. Mr. Ball spire of the )51 use that Tennyson had made of a style of EngW blank verse which he had practically re-discovere He wrought his ideas to a fine pitch of perfectio There was perhaps too much workmanship ani polit about some of the lines, but the resulting bean' was something which we could highly appreciat (A pplause). Mr. CECIL WILLSON, headmaster of the Lyrg Grammar School, seconded and gave his estimate Tennyson's genius. He considered that the " of the King" were the most likely of Tennyson works to be permanent,because they dealt with matte of real human interest.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. PEattis supported and the vote was heart° pa,sed. .</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON, in responding, expressed opinion that Tennyson was not only a very fine but a beautiful character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>CLEVER WOMAN'S PRIVATE BOOK.</head>
        <p>A CLEVER WOMAN'S PRIVATE BOOK.</p>
