      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March, 16, 1912.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WORKINGMEN AND THE INFIRMARY. £1.,397 Handed over to the Institution.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN AND THE INFIRMARY. £1.,397 Handed over to the Institution.</p>
        <p>The workingmen of Warrington are no doubt taking a deeper and more active interest in the Infirmary and Dispensary as each year passes by. This conclusion arises out of tho fact that the annual contribution from this body to the Infirmary shows a steady increase each year. The total contribution for the year just closed amounted to £1,413 103 lid, as against £1,249 Os Id for the previous year. After deducting expenses amounting to RIG Is 11,1, the handsome sum of £1,397 9s 10d as against £1,231 33 id for 1910, was handed over to the infirmary, through Mr. John Fairclough, at the annual meeting of the Workingmen's Committee which was held in the Town Hall on Satur- day afternoon. The Mayor (Councillor Joseph) pre- sided over a large number of representatives of the various workers and was supported by Mr W Peter Rylands (chairman of the Board of Management), Alderman T Burton (chairman of the Workingmen's Committee), and Mr J H J Hempen (bon secretary). The MAYOR said he was glad to find his interest in municipal affairs led him to the feelirg that the employees and employers in Warrington were on much better terms with each other than in many other places. That Workingmens Committee was founded in 1870 and he had the pleasure and honour of being present at the inangural gathering. He must congratulate them on the service they had rendered and the manner in which they had worked for such a worthy institution. The Mayor then referred at some length to the increase in cases which had to be dealt with by the Infirmary staff and said that in 1886 there were 162 in-patients and 20 out-patients whereas in 1906 Have were 607 in-patients—four titres as many—and 307 out- patients. That was a very great advance in the number of important. Cases nearly in the proportion of one per day. lie had introduced those figures to remind them of the great and increasing work of the institution and at the same time thanking them for the service they had rendered to that worthy institution. (Applause). Mr. J. 11. J. HamesoN, in submitting the hon. secretary's statement of accounts, said they had hoped to get .£15,000 from the various works but, they had been somewhat disappointed. They had made an increase of £3 on the infirmary concert and an increased contribution from the open-air festival of nearly £lO. On the expenditure side they had only involved the sum of £l6 Is. Id. which was a very low amount to bring in such a large sum. They had lost about two weeks contributions through the railway strike. The committee had been considering a systematic collection from from shopkeepers and others es they were anxious to find new sources of income. (Hear, hear). Alderman BURTON in moving the adoption of the accounts, said the result of the past. year's work was most gratifying to the Working Men's Committee, coupled with the knowledge that their subscriptions were gradually on the increase. During the first year of the Committee's existence they handed over to the infirmary .C 165 les 4d. They thought that was good and were very proud of it, but since then, through the activities of the committee, their annual subscriptions had gradually increased until they had reached the handsome sum of £1,397 9s 10d. (Applause.) He was sure the Board of Management would be pleased with that contribution and that everyone would be satisfied who was in any way connected with the movement. (Hear, I ear.) It was always a pleasure to him to attend the meetings of that committee because its business was conducted on the. most pleasant and satisfactory lines and the members all worked harmoniously together. The result was that since the formation of the committee they had realised the total sum of £21,241 lfis 2d. (Applause.) That was a very largo sum and must have gone a long way in helping to centred the noor and suffering people in the town and district of Warrington. (Hear, hear.) In regard to the balance sheet they would notice that some of the firms in the town had allowed their subscriptions to go down a little. On the other hand there were firms who had sensibly increased their subscriptions. Messrs Armitage and Rigby generously increased their subscription and Messrs. Rylende, Bros., Ltd., doubled theirs—an act which he common ded to all the other works. Other notable increases were the St. Helens' Cable Company, Ltd., the Union Tanneries, Ltd., the United Velvet Cutters' Association, Ltd., and Messrs. Peter Walker and SODS, Ltd. The increases from the six firms named amounted to £lOl 18s 21d. The committee were very anxious in regard to the future, not only to keep up their present subscriptions, but they had deter- ruined to increase them in order to meet the growing financial needs of the Infirmary, (Hear, hear.) The Iwork of the Infirmary was expanding very rapidly. Nearly 12,000 patients had been treated during the year. There had been 5,507 eccieents,an increase of 1072 It was almost incredible to think so many accident in Warrington during the year. The home patients totalled 1,155, an increase of 170. If the funds were dried up to any extent the Board of Management would have to cut down expenses and the first thing they would do would be to dispense with the doctors who bad been engaged in outside work. The result was that the patients affected would have to provide medical aid for themselves. There were only a few who could do that, and the others would have to call in a union doctor, which would not only be pauperising them but would be lowering them to the public mind. Alderman Burton then handed over a cheque for £1397 12e. 10d. to Mr.JohnFairclough who,on behalf of the Board of Management, thanked the committee for their handsome contribution. Mr. W. P. RYLANDS, in seconding, congratulated the committee most heartily on the work they had accomplished, It was a very important thing to bear in mind that such a large percentage—one in seven—of those who lived round the district got some benefit from the institution they were there to support. At the time of the extension it was generally considered that they were embarking on a sea of financial difficulty. However, they embarked upon it because the old buildings could not supply the needs of the town and it was necessary if the work had to be done that they should have the improved buildings and if the town were not prepared to pay for the maintenance of the work of the Institution then tbe buildings must stand idle. He was optimistic enough to believe they would be able to make both ends meet in a comparatively short time. At that time he told them that if they doubled their ordinary sources of income the difficulty would be practically overcome. They bad doubled those subscriptions but the workers were perfectly entitled to scrutinise the subscription list and see if those who could afford, were sup- porting that worthy cause as they should do. It was not from a sense of charity they were supporting it, but from a sense duty. (Hear, hear.) The work of the institution had greatly increased, lie apprehended there was no reason why the steady increases and sub- scriptions they had maintained should not continue in the future. Since the extensions there had been increases in every direction, both in the number of cases treated and in expenditure. In regard to the possible effect of the National Insurance Act, one did not wish to meet trouble half-way but the Board of Management had watched the progress of this Act with.a great deal of apprehension. Whether the Act would do them harm or not he could not say. He would urge upon them the fact that of those who were attended at the Infirmary,eeclud- ing accidents, not 25 per cent would be insured persons. The Act did not carry with it any securities for the well-being of the wives and children ; it was only for the workers who contributed. Those who could not</p>
        <p>contribute were bound to have something of an infirmary character and he urged upon them to keep up their subscriptions to that worthy institution. (Applause). Mr. GEORGE WALKER, in proposing the re-election of Alderman Burton as chairman, said he had taken a very deep interest in the institution. - Mr. HATTON seconded. The Alderman had expressed his intention of retiring but was persuaded to accept office again. He expressed his gratitude for the cordiality shown to him by the members The other officials re-elected were, Deputy-chairman Mr 0 Walker auditor, Councillor C Jackson-Holmes,. secretary, Mr J H J Hampson.After testimony had been; paid to the valuable services rendered by each of the officials votes of thanks were heartily accorded to the Chairman, Vice-chairman, Secretary, Hon Auditor, Hon Surgeons, Press, Warrington Football Club, Mr Down's Band, Warrington Charities' Committee, War- rington Apollo and Warrington Male Voice Choirs and to His Worship the Mayor for presiding, and also for the use of the Council Chamber. Mr. GEORGE WALKER, in presenting his report of the work of the Education Committee, said that after nine years' membership of that authority be felt convinced of one thing—and that was that the committee ought to consist of representatives solely appointed to deal with education. The Town Council had plenty to do without bothering with that special subject. Ile was a member of the Sites and Buildings Committee, who got more kicks than halfpence, and were open to very severe criticism. It was a queer thing but it generally took twelve months after an idea had been expressed that a school was wanted in a certain locality, to get the first brick put down. If they wanted to see the seamy side of civilisation they ought to be on the School Attend- ance Committee. Continuing,Mr Walker said if a child was to take full advantage of the education given, it ought to have gcod health and that was [why he was in favour of school clinics. Be liked the education to be adapted to the particular needs of the child and unless that were done, he thought the whole thing was a miserable failure. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>VETERANS AT BIRTHDAY PARTY.</head>
        <p>VETERANS AT BIRTHDAY PARTY.</p>
        <p>An unusual birthday party was held at Long Sutton near Wiese* on Saturday. The total of the ages of the host and his six visitors was 530 years. Individually the guests' area were 82, 78, 73, 73, 72, 71. The host was 80 years of age. When the renewal of a beerhouse licence (The London) came up before the Windsor magistrates, the Chief Constable of the borough said that within 200 yards of the home there were 11 alehouses, 5 beer- houses, 5 general licences, and 2 licensed restaurants aud railway refreshment rooms.</p>
        <p>The Sheffield police are making an effort to stop the practice of throwing banana skies in the streets, and in two cases brought before the magistrates the defendants were ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>"A HASS." In thinking of the projected new Union Offices, I was somehow reminded of an incident that came under my observation some :30 years ago. The Guardians in my district re•furnished their Board room, and on the back of each chair was placed a brass plate with the name of the township represented by the occupant. One member was so impressed by this arrangement that he resolved to adopt it at home, and forthwith gave orders for his initials to be in like manner engraved on every seat in his dining- room. Then, like the famous Duke of York, who marched his 10,000 men up the hill and immediately marched them down again, he oidered the removal of the initials. They were " ASS." COL. EDEN.</p>
        <p>The Inspector of Constabulary who was here last week is brother of the Bishop of Wakefield. Both are gentlemen in the true sense of the word. Col. Eden is highly spoken of by the police generally, just as he was by his own men when on active army service. As for his brother, his praise is in all the churches. He succeeded Dr. Walsham How, in whose hymns many Nonconformists delight, and he fills the chair as second Bishop of the diocese right worthily. LADIES' GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>The note as to the suggested halfpenny tram fares reminded me of another thing. When they abolished second class compartments on their system, the L. and Y. Railway Company adapted a proportion of them for ladies exclusively. It was time to make provision of that kind. The writer of " Wcinen's Chat" pointed out last week how the average Britisher loves to commune with himself in a shy and reserved sort of way. This unsociable habit leads him as far</p>
        <p>transformed by patches of greenery, which it was hoped would have the further advantage of providing the district with pit-props, and in other ways yielding revenue to the planters. A considerable area was planted with suitable trees, and the experiment proved that afforestation is thoroughly worth doing where it can, as in this case, be cheaply done. Suitable trees can easily be chosen, and it is surprising how quickly they flourish even on pit-mounds in a disttict where sunshine is only too often blotted out by volumes of black smoke. I am rather surprised that Alderman Bennett should appear to begrudge a trifling expenditure on an attempt to adorn a public place with trees and so minister to the health and enjoyment of those who are condemned to labour amid inartistic surroundings and to live in mean streets. I am quite sure he would not do so if he did not sincerely believe that it was throwing money away. He seems to think that those who favour the tree-planting are—how shall I put it? I will illust-ate by reference to an amusing story told at a dinner of the Authors' Club in London, by Sir Alfred East, 'President of the Royal Society of Britist Artists. Ile was speaking as a landscape painter, of the delights of the country and particularly of the charm of trees. "I remember, he said, " passing along a country road and seeing two men cutting down a tree. I love trees, which, as you know, are associated in many ways not only with art, but with literature and history. I protested against these men removing that tree and thereby spoiling the landscape. I said to one of the men, What harm has the tree done to you that you should cut it down?" The man was surprised by the question, and paused—as so many British workmen do. He carefully wiped his brow, and, addressing his mate, and pointing to me, said, " Dotty." However, we who would have the woodman spare the trees may answer, with St. Paul, "We are not mad, but speak forth the words of truth and soberness." MISQUOTATION. In the Town Council debate respecting the bridge approaches Councillor Hawthorn armed that in given</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>OXFORD UNDERGRADUATES AND THE COAL STRIKE.</head>
        <p>OXFORD UNDERGRADUATES AND THE COAL STRIKE.</p>
        <p>At Oxford. a remarkable movement has been started. A number of undergraduates have organised themselves into corps of volun- teers to help to avert the disaster with which the nation is threatened by the coal strike, Four hundred undergraduates have</p>
        <p>expressed their willingness to work the mines, and negotiations have been opened with some coalowners with a view to engag- ing their services. Our picture shows mem- bers of the Oxford University Strike Emer- gency Committee discussing details.</p>
        <p>- •••,,,,m,':''iM7-., .._ •-,-.4siNfhaki:iisi.., ?..k-,,,."'-',,:i...);-.‘y--:,:.;,..,4:ti1,,A 5...:,..,::-..!iq 7,•••,.':',7..;.......::,...:ij:,...,:...!::;',.:4!..41,;',Ettet.k.iti0teitfirt,ititztipt....,::zp..1.;.;?.0.1titiik:81:0t:,! e 0111:.!.-.......,Aii.,...:,::'''''''''.'''':-.......::::.:1*tgk....:..:.:':':i:4.16",,......'.:!......::ititii5gr:;..E:.:.,...:::',,:i,...:i;:-.:..,..:.::: ;::, ....."....: . '(N..l•.'il#l'...;grtitttiliNt; ~..,...,..,,,....,..,,w,:wg.„......:qr .....r.-.:..i.R''.'.......,.:t....:,r..'..........::.....,:,.";:......''..'....:,',-.;..i5i;';.......,....7...1..."5". ":;:.'...........*''''''. - ...).."'A'"4'l"''''..l.P{4l.);* -* i11,'11.11.111.1.11::',.,.,::,........:UH Iiiii;4,!:.!:::-. ...:4.7i1:114.:::...t..t.k.1:;r:::-i'-'::::...:.:.-": ::'..A.301•11..g ':::!.' ...'..''''''.'!.:.::,.'.i....,.....,*::-..iiiiiiiiiiiiiiiil4ll:l4lll:ll!. ..s..k,',..:....'...'.::!:.::.:Wki',V.r...jinßli:4:NiKtlgg.a.:gtigttnh.;:.::.hAa.mt ~,,.''0.v.....:..,,,f...;.f..: .. ...: :::.:,-,.*,,ai5t....1ikii....:....t..-.::lii:iiiiil:d..o.:gglltßiliiitZi;iiNliAietAß-ii,W.-::64:.'i.gNti.ti Ni,....',..:444Atti...,::;:taki::'lti...:;E;V:KgiiiiN.RiPiiig.:ooolilitklV!....Ail'?..:. :':!:.:.'..'!'''',.."'.‘"•:.':*:41,1611.1:4....;:: ,f..:::: .;.,:....'...-..,;.'" .'110.i'L.:..xt.:,,,,....,-:Niii;fi:::!:..t:::':' ''...:::,::Y•;;;P:rlt .-::::-.1i1!::':::-.:iliii:N4iii::-Ik.' I ~.;1...i.• *N ....:............':'.. .....„.......;;;-....,,7,.. :.. . -,;.4‘, _ .....;.... ..:,......,::i4l-4Q.0- r...-..? ow „,:..„.........::.:-......i.,...:i.:U:'.1:',,:i... ~.,,,.... . . ' ..' . , .7.....,;.,... ...........-44...',4.1.-iiigtiftio..7vtais.N.....N. .- ...,,,,,,::.*ii.,, ; ... ........ ~.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>as possible to travel alone, and whenever possible, he will deliberately select an empty railway carriage. Now imagine a solitary busineis girl setting forth on a railway train journey over a tunnelled route, and finding one min in each of the third-class carriages. Naturally she welcomes the innovation designed to ensuro her of a compartment that is to the male sex "verboten, "as the Germans say. To the present, however, the privilege has been reserved only by means of a printed sheet stuck on to tbo window; and to her chagrin and the annoyance of officials, whenever the man who wants a carriage to himself discovers one of these unoccupied, he just rips off the distinguishing slip and takes possession. EDUCATION.</p>
        <p>In her address at the Sankey-street Co-operative Hall, Miss Madams insisted that we must begin a real system of national education. If, as she thinks, we bay° barely touched the fringe of the matter, what is likely to be the cost when we fairly grab the hem of the garment Surely it were better that specialists should first adjust their own differences and agree on something like a common basis before proceeding further to make ducks and drakes of public money in expensive experiments which one section no sooner enters upon than another rises up to unsparingly condemn. Does Mr. Jarman complain that the boys sent from our schools and setting their feet on the first rung of the commercial ladder are lacking in the very elements of success in the sphere they would enter, Miss A. K. Williams airily retorts from the platform of the Queen's Ball, that education does not now consist of turning out little machines warranted to get all sums right and not have more than three mistakes in dictation. Do parents make sacrifices in order that children ambitious to be trained for the teaching profession may continue a year or two longer at the secondary school, Principal Griffiths discourages all concerned by declaring that one of the most serious mistakes of our existing educational system is the herding together of young people all aiming at the same profession. Moreover, who shall say that he is wrong, or that anyone else who undertakes to lay down the law for us is mistaken? Indeed, who will cross swords with Professor Bonner when he flings down the gauge that 'lf there is any avenue which should be free, it is the avenue of education " ? Nay more, who will deny Mr. W. B. Steer's proposition that knowledge is after all far less important than wisdom ? And then —but all this is too wonderful for me and I must leave it for the alleged experts to quarrel over, as they did in the beginning, do now, and, I fear, ever will do. Let me just add that for Professor Sadler, whose outstanding merits Miss Madams so generously recog- nised, I have genuine regard. I know him when he was University extension lecturer, end, now that he is Vice-chancellor of the University of Leeds, I was very glad indeed to renew my acquaintance with him two or three weeks ago. ALGEBRA. On the occasion above refeired to Mx. Jai man con- ceded that the boys whom he adversely criticised might be able to do algebra, although lamentably ignorant of ordinary arithmetic. The remark sent me hack in thought to a period when I was editing a certain provincial mid-weekly, and a local church dignitary who had compiled for a school publishing firm a text book on algebra dropped in with an advance copy. Inviting me to review this work he directed my attention to two of his problems in particular as being beyond the capacity of any elementary scholar in the district. I had not myself so poor an opinion of the local boys' attainments, and, in saying so, I challenged my clerical visitor to offer two copies of his latest book as prizes for the first two solutions sent to me after an announcement in the paper. He readily agreed, repeating his conviction that there would be no competition. As a matter of fact both prizes were claimed within a few hours of publication of the conditions. Though I did not mention this to the astonished ecclesiastic, it is only fair to add that the winners were like the superior ' apples &amp;c., which the fruiterer uses as "toppers they belonged th the more promising material of the school to which the headmaster devoted extra attention for show purposes at the annual speech day. TREE-PLANTING. So Alderman Bennett fancies that trees will not grow in the neighbourhood of St. Elphin's Park? Properly chosen with due regard to the peculiarities of soil and other local conditions, trees can assuredly be made to flourish in less likely places. Some of the communities among whom my lot has been cast from time to time have trained the young folk to observe Arbor Day each year; and, even in industrial towns which prior to these celebrations had a most depressing character, splendid results have been achieved in the course of a few years. Some time ago I read bow an association had been formed in what is known as the Black Country— that inferno of ironworks and coalpits in the neighbourhood of Birmingham and Wolverhampton— for the purpose of beautifying the district by afforestation. Trees were planted on the ironworks refuse heaps, and after a while dreary wastes were</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>circumstances they would "spoil the ship for a ha'porth of tar." It is so refreshing to hear a literally accurate quotation in such an atmosphere that I venture to commend ths speaker for not saying,as most people do Fay, "a penn'orth of paint "—just as, taking these things from one another instead of going to the source occasionally, they say " Fresh fields (instead of woods) and pastures new," " A little know- ledge (learning) is a dangerous thing," and so on. I have made a big collectioa of these common mis- quotations, and may add half-a-dozen more samples. The even tenour of their way" should be "The noiseless tenour of their way." "Speeding the part- ing guest" should read "Speed the going guest." " Fine by degrees and beautifully less" is mostly, quoted as "Small by degrees and beautifully less.' " When Greeek meets Greek then comes the tug of war," in the original is " When Greeks joined Greeks then was the tug of war." A man convinced cgainst his will, is of the same opinion still " is good rhyme, but utter nonsense ; it is "He that complies agiinst his will is of his own opinion still." Another ridiculous misquotation is "A wet sheet and a flowing sail." It should be" A wet sheet and a flowing sea." The last of these is from " Hudtbras," whose author was of course incapable of such a monstrosity as he is daily credited with by the thoughtless. Just consider one moment. How is anyone to be " convinced " against his will? Unwilling compliance is conceivable; but nvoluntary conviction is of course impossible. ANOTHER ADAGE.</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton says that whether they like it or not Warringtonians make their living by smoke. To be sure, he does not mean that we are all engaged in or dependent on the tobacco trade ; neither would he be understood as arguing that everytate of us habitually uses "the weed" inDome form to sustain life, and that any who decided to abstain from it would die there and then. No such thing ; indeed, as I cast au eye over what I have written, I wonder if I ought not to apologise and hasten to disclaim intention to insult either the worthy councillor or the intelligent reader. Perhaps rather than spend time upon that I may at once proceed to explain that Mr. Petnberton's suggestion simply was that " where there's muck there's money," and that the converse of the proposi • tion was equally tine and unassailable. Certainly 1 recognise that a good deal might be said on the other side; but there I must leave it and all else for the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>GENERAL BETHUNE'S NEW POST. -- Coming Appointment as Director of Territorials.</head>
        <p>GENERAL BETHUNE'S NEW POST. -- Coming Appointment as Director of Territorials.</p>
        <p>In three weeks' time the training of the Territorial Force for the season will commence in earnest by the departure of many detachments for holiday quarters with regular troops, or at Ilialey and other places, says the "Morning Post." Concurrent with the opening of the "outdoor season " a number of important changes in commands take place, most of them occurring on the 31st inst. The chief alteration concerns the post of Director-General of the Territorial Forces. This normally should be effected on April 2, but the present holder, Major-General J. S. C)wans Is unlikely to commence his next duties as Quartermaster- General to the Force at the time originally arranged, and his successor as the Territorials' military chief when chosen will not take up duty before the end of May at the earliest. The choice is likely to fall on Major-General E. C. Bethune, now commanding the West Lancashire Division. There will be several alterations in the commands of the Territorial divisions. A big effort is being made to raise the quality of the Territorial Force's training to a higher level by the appointment of RegulAr officers who have been success- ful in training Regular troops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>HELPLESS FOR FOUR YEARS. Confined to Bed—No Treatment of Good. In Two Months as well as ever. AN AYRSHIRE MIRACLE.</head>
        <p>HELPLESS FOR FOUR YEARS. Confined to Bed—No Treatment of Good. In Two Months as well as ever. AN AYRSHIRE MIRACLE.</p>
        <p>The following case has caused deep and wide- spread interest throughout Ayrshire. Mrs. Strain, Mull-street, Catrine, Ayrshire, N. 8., writes : "Just a line to tell you that Dr. Cassell's Tablets for Weak and Nervous People have entirely cured me of spinal paralysis after lying helpless in bed for four years. I could do nothing for myself, as my arms had lost all power, and were quite useless. No treatment did me any good until I took the Tablets. I then soon found life returning to my fingers and arms, and in two months I was up and about as well as ever. I attri— bate my cure solely to Dr.Cassell's Tablets and nothing else." Writing again in September last, Mrs. Strain says : "I am still keeping perfectly well. I have not needed to take Dr. Cassell's Tablets since being cured by your great remedy." Dr. Cassell's Tablets for Weak and Nervous People are a revelation to those suffering from nerve and organic weakness, digestive and kidney troubles, and all diseases arising from physical debility in children or adults. Price 10d , Is. lA. and 2s. 9d. at all chemists. A free sample box sent on receipt of 2d. for postage, etc., to Dr. Cassell's Co. Ltd., King-street, W., Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE CO.</head>
        <p>PRUDENTIAL ASSURANCE CO.</p>
        <p>CONTINUED PROGRESS.</p>
        <p>RECENT ATTACKS REPLIED TO.</p>
        <p>The ordinary general meeting of the Prudential Assurance Company, Limited, was held on Thursday, March 7th, at Holborn Bars, E.C., Mr. Thomas C. Dewey (chairman of the company) presiding. In the course of his address, the Chairman said the Prudential continues to progress. The total income of the company during 1911 was £15,252,937, being an increase of £370,961 over the previous year. The income from premiums was £12,943,676, being an increase of £211,239 ; and the income from interest, dividends, and rents was £2,809,261, being an increase of £159,722. It will be interesting to notice how the total premium income is made up. There is £7,182,000 from weekly premiums, £949,500 from monthly premiums, £973,500 from quarterly premiums, £983,000 from half-yearly premiums, and £3,356,000 from yearly premiums. Weekly and monthly premiums paid through the industrial branch during the year amounted to £7,631,908. The yearly, half-yearly, and quarterly premiums paid through the ordinary branch amounted to £4,812,268. The new annual premium income for the Tear in this branch was £325,699, assuring the sum of £5,396,721. The mortality in both branches during the past year was well below the expectancy. The claims of the year in both branches, including bonus additions, amounted to £6,399,331, the endowment assurance policies in the ordinary branch being responsible for £2,319,180 of this amount, which is an increase of £lBO,OOO over the previous year. The amount paid in cash for surrenders in each branch shows a reduction over the previous year. During the past year 140,617 policies have been surrendered in the industrial branch in exchange for free policies upon which no further premiums are pay- able. The number of these free policies now in force is 1,705,885, and the number of free policies that became claims last year was 43,668. In addition to these free policies, we have in force over 70,000 policies on which the future premiums have been remitted after age 75 has ben attained, end on which over twenty-five years premiums have been paid. During the sear 1911, in accordance with this practice, 16,528 policy-holders were freed from the payment of future premiums, and we pild claims on 10,996 policies where the payment of premiums had previously been remitted The assets of the company, as shown in the balance sheet for 1911, amount to £81,230,682, as compared with .C 77,529,220 at the end of the previous year. The increase of £3,710,456, however, by n, means represents the actual investment work of the year, since, owing to repayments, the amount invested during the year exceeded 0,000,000. Great care has been devoted to the selection of these investments, and the shareholders may rest assured that there is nothing in our balance sheet of a risky or speculative nature. (Hear, bear). You will have seen from the report that we are in the happy position of being able to declare an increased rate of bonus in both branches of the company. (Hear, hear). In the ordinary branch the rate has been increased from £1 14s per cent on the original sum assured to £1 168 per cent. In the industrial branch, Lam glad to say we are able this year to distribute as bonus the sum of £510,000, being £90,000 more than the amount distributed last year. (Hear, hear.) We propose to give 5 per cent on all policies becoming claims upon which at least five, but less than ten, years premiums have been paid ; 10 per cent on policies of ten years' duration, 15 per cent on policies of fifteen years dura- tion, 20 per cent on policies of twenty years duration, 30 per cent on policies of thirty years duration, 90 per cent on policies of forty years duration, and 50 per cent on policies of fifty years duration and upwards. (Applause ) The rate of expense in the industrial branch is now less than 381 per cent on the premiums received, or just over 33 per cent on the income from premiums and interest. In the ordinary branch the rate of expense hes fallen to under 8 per cent on the premiums received, or less than 6 per cent on the income from premiums and interest. THE ATTACKS UPON THE COMPANY. During the past year public attention has been directed to an attack which has been made upon the company by a certain weekly journal. The directors had no difficulty in deciding at once that the right method of dealing with the attack was to treat it with the contempt which it deserved—(hear,hear)—and their decision was confirmed by some of the most dis- tinguished counsel at the Bar. The directors intended at the proper time and place to make an authoritative statement on the subject,apd the present in is convenient opportunity for so doing. It has been alleged that the directors and managers of the company connive at irregularities on the part of their agents. On behalf of the directors and managers of the company I say emphatically that there is not a particle of truth in the allegation. (Hear, hear.) Let me deal briefly with the principal grounds upon which, as I understand, these allegations are based. For many years the company have allowed a son or daughter to insure a parent whose age was over 60 and under 70 years for a reasonable sum to provide for funeral expenses, the proposal for the assurance being signed by the person whose life was to be gestured. It is said that these policies were void because they did not cosoply with the terms of the Gambling Act of 1774. I believe that I it has never yet been decided that funeral expenses in I such circumstances did not constitute an insurable interest, but I prefer to give what I consider to be a, practical and business answer to the allegation. It isi this : That never yet have the company repudiated one of these policies on such grounds. (Hear, hear.) Had I the person whose life was insured died the day after the policy was affected the company would have paid the full amount due. Another charge is that some agents have taken signal tures for proposals for insurance is an irregular way. Any such action is directly contrary to the instructions under which all our agents work. I will not attempt to excuse any agent who may have done so ; he deserves and will receive punishment for such an offence if any such misconduct is proved. After a close experience extending for upwards of fifty years with the company's agents, I say, without hesitation, that as a body of men they de their work well and honestly, and they will bear favourable comparison with any other equally large body of men, drawn I care not from what source. (Applause.) I need not further allude to this topic more than to say that the incident has afforded us the opportunity of proving the strength and confidence of our policy-holders, our shareholders, and the staff, especially the outdoor staff, of the com- pany. (Hear, hear.) As regards our staff, it was inevitable that many should suffer from the effects of such an attack, but, nothing daunted,each man pursued his way with confidence. The attack upon us has utterly failed—(hear, bear)—the high reputation and honour of the company remain untouched, and the record of the year's work testifies to the honourable success we have achieved. (Hear. hear). THE INSURANCE ACT._ -</p>
        <p>The Insurtnce Act has now been passed and contains provisions under which we are enabled to form an approved society for the purpose of working the National Health portion of the Act. The chief con- ditions laid are (I) that such approved society shall not be carried on for profit ; (2) that it shall be self- governing. We are taking the necessary steps to form n approved society, and are of opinion that it would be wrong of us to allow our policy-holders to be compelled to join any approved society other than a Prudential one. (Hear. hear.) The Chairman concluded by moving "That the report of the directors and accounts certified by the auditors be received and adopted." The resolution was carried unanimously and the formal business of the company carried through without a note of dissent. THE PRUDENTIAL IN WARRINGTON. As showing tho tremendous connection this Company has in Warrington alone it is only necessary to mention that during last year a sum of £19,639 was paid in claims and policies maturing in this town, and even in the first two months of this year £3,600 has been paid through the local District Office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>" GOODALLS " Furnishers and Decorators, EXPERTS IN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>DECORATION.</head>
        <p>DECORATION.</p>
        <p>Designs and estimates free.</p>
        <p>KING STREET, MANCHESTER.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>NATURE'S WE A THER-TELLERS.</p>
        <p>Bees are also notoriously fine weather animals, not venturing out when it is liKely to be rainy, in accordance with the lines :</p>
        <p>" If bees stay at home, Rain will soon come. If they fly away, Fine will be the day."</p>
        <p>Even more sensitive than animals, the plant world is full of weather prophets. We have the well-known pimpernel, or Plough- man's Weatherglass. The opening and closing of the petals of the pimpernel are better understood amongst the Bedfordshire labourers than the indications of any instru- ment. In fine weather it opens in the morn- ing (usually from 7 to 8 a.m.) and closes in the afternoon (2 to 3 p.m) ; if it closes earlier than usual, or fails to open in tbe morning, this may be regarded as a sure sign of rain. I could mention other "weather tellers," but these may be sufficient for the present. I recommend my nephews and nieces to look for them not only that they may learn the weather, but that they may learn to use their eyes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHRYSANTHEMUM.</head>
        <p>THE CHRYSANTHEMUM.</p>
        <p>A writer in " Sunday at Home" says that the Germans have a pretty little legend about the origin of the chrysanthemum.lt says that one day a poor charcoal-burner found a little child in the depths of the Black Forest. In sheer pity he took the poor little thing home, and husband and wife and children did all they could to make the strange child happy in their poor home. Then one day, so the legend says, a sudden brightness shone about the strange child, and he vanished from their midst. Then the poor man and his wife dis- covered that they had, all unwittingly, entertained the Christ-child; and when the charcoal-burner next visited the spot where he had discovered the little stranger, he found there a beautiful yellow and white flower, and that was the origin of the chrysanthemum.</p>
        <p>GOING VISITING. When I go out to spend the day I say when I must come away; So they can tell me, "Time to go " Because I might forget, you know! I wear a napkin like big folks, And I don't ever have to coax For one more cooky; and I spread The jelly thick upon my bread. I never have to eat the crust— They don' tell visitors, " You must " And I have ketchup on my meat, And truly tea that'Ei very sweet. And I have such an appetite I eat until my dress gets tight; And no one says, "Your eyes, I fear, Are bigger than your tummy, dear." And all the time I'm glad I came, But somehow,l oan't quite explain Why, going home's the best of all, And hearing mother softly call: "Is that my Sunbeam back again?" And then she kisses me; and then I know I love her most, if she Does sometimes say "You can't," to me. THIS WEEK'S STORY.</p>
        <p>A Scotsman the other day went to a Lon- don dentist with a toothache. The dentist told him he must have it out. Scot: "Mon, then I must hae gas." While the dentist was getting it ready the Scot began to count his money. The dentist said, somewhat testily, " You need not pay until it's out."</p>
        <p>The Scot replied : "I ken that; but, as ye're aboot to mak' me unconscious, I jist want to see hoo I stan'."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CIIANCELLOR'S NIECE'S DEATEL</head>
        <p>CIIANCELLOR'S NIECE'S DEATEL</p>
        <p>The Chancellor of the Exchequer, with Mrs Lloyd George, and a few personal friends, went to Waterloo on Monday morning to meet the 11.30 train from Haslemere,which brought the remains of their favourite niece, who died at llindhead, where she had been stay- ing for some months for the benefit of her health. The Chancellor placed on the coffin a wreath to which was attached the inscription" From Uncle David." Other wreaths also were deposited by Mrs Lloyd George and Mr and Mrs William Evans, of Birmingham.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE SCHOOLS AND THE COAL SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>It was stated at the meeting of the Cheshire Education Committee on Monday, that the coal supply. In some of the schools was running short, and it was decided to advise school managers that if fuel was obtainable it was better to pay a little more for it rather than close the schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>Pasty, wasty por- ridge is what you get from ordinary "loose" oats or "rolled oats."</p>
