      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>iOUR SERIAL STORY.</head>
        <p>iOUR SERIAL STORY.</p>
        <p>[ILL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>you are this man's wire now, an,t mus. alma. the best of it."</p>
        <p>Stella .pushed back the heavy hair on her brow. "I will do my best, but oh! how hideous marriage is when there is no love! If only I had had the courage to break it off at_the eleventh hour—if "I understand, dear," /..ady Mary said soothingly. "I wish I had known earlier that this marriage was so repulsive to you. But it is too late now, dearest—too late to take the best—you must content yourself with facing the worst, and perhaps it won't be so hard," she added hastily. "After all, the man does love you!" "That's the worst of it !" ' Stella said drearily. "But Me love is a flame that scorches instead of a deep glow that warms. Tell me, dearest," she went on abruptly, "did you ever hear from Jaspar Tennant?" Lady Mary flushed and looked awaf. "Yes," she said slowly, "but why talk r.bout him now? lle is nothing to you." "Nothing now, perhaps," Stella replied bitterly, 'but he once was. I loved him and he loved me, and we had almost arranged to take matters into our own hands aud run away, just going through the formality of asking Clifford to releasc me, but he never wrote,"• she went on, a spot of colour show- ing in her cheeks, "he went away and left me to,,my..fate." _ _</p>
        <p>"Child !" Lady Mary said solemnly, "don't think he forgot—don't misjudge him. What happened is now irrevocable, you are another man's wife, and have passed out of Jaspar's life."</p>
        <p>"So long as he is dead to me," Stella said in a low firm voice, "I can endure my life better, but if ever we came face to face again the old agony would come back to me."</p>
        <p>"Then God grant he may never re-enter your life," Lady Mary said solemnly. And as Stella turned away to arrange the motor veil round her hat, Lady Mary murmured sadly: "If she only knew! If she only knew !"</p>
        <p>As she stepped into the car that was to take them to the country place lent them by Lady Mary for the honeymoon, Stella's maid handed her the dog that Tennant had given to her on the last day they had met, and the little creature nestled in her arms with bark of delight to be with its mistress. "Why are you taking that dog?" Clifford said sharply. "Because I am very fond of it," she replied quietly. "She will be no trouble; Fill loves motoring, don't you, Fifi ?" As the car started off a slim frail figure darted out before them, and waved a tele- gram. With a muttered curse Clifford Hawke obeyed Stella's firm command to stop the car, and then said angrily: "What do you want ! What are you stopping the car for?" "It's Lettie Bond," Stella said, beckoning the girl to her side. "What is it, Lottie dear? Have you anything for me?" "Yes, Miss Trains--I mean Mrs. Hawke," the girl panted, sending a frightened glance at Clifford Hawke'e cruel face. "I've news from Mr. Tennant."</p>
        <p>"News?" Stella cried, and reached out her hand for the telegram. "He is alive,miss,"Lottie went on quickly, "but has been ten days a prisoner in a house in the East End; he sent a telegram for me; this is it ; and I was to tell you--" But before she could finish her speech Clifford Hawke had touched the steering wheel and the car sprang forward, leaving Lottie on the pavement. Stella put her hand on her husband's arm. Her face was very pale through her veil and her eyes shone like stars. "Why do you do that?" she said quickly. "Turn back, or stop the car, and let Inc get out. I want to hear what Lottie has to say." "Don't be ridiculous!" he snarled; "we can't stop the traffic for the sake of a hysterical fool who raves about that artist chap. Besides," he added, "you are my wife now, and have no right to want to know any- thing about any other man." Stella fell back in her seat and closed her eyes to hide the sudden tears that had leapt to them. He was right and she was wrong. She should not have wanted to hear about Jaspar Tennant, but she could not help that wild, unreasoning joy that had filled her heart when Lottie said he had sent her a mes- sage. He had not forgotten her. Ile had been forcibly detained, and been ill, while she had played him false by marrying Clifford Hawke. As these thoughts rushed through her mind she held the dog he had given her closer in her arms, as though the contact of the animal he had petted brought some comfort to her. When they arrived at Beechwood Towers she went upstairs to her own suite of rooms to rest before dinner. She wanted to think, to be able to school herself before meeting her husband again. Her maid entered the room presently with a telegram. "This has just come, ma'am," she said. "Mr. Hawke is in the grounds somewhere." 'The telegram was addressed to "Hawke," and opening it Stella read: "Mr. Tennant's message was that you should go to Lady Mary and ask her to tell you the story of his illness. She knows every- thing. Ile will come to you when you send</p>
        <p>That wag all. Stella crushed the flimsy scrap of paper in her hand. How sweet of Lottie to have sent by telegram the message that Clifford Hawke had interrupted. Her mind flew again to the message. What could it bet What could Lady Mary know? She had seen her that morning, and she had said nothing except that she had heard from Jaapar ! What was the mystery? Why was she to ask Lady Mary? She went downstairs and into the old- fashioned oak hall. She wanted to ask her husband if he would motor her back the fol- lowing day. She must see Lady Mary as soon as possible. There was another telegram lying on the hall table. It would probably contaiu congratulations on their marriage. She picked it up and opened it. The next moment she had uttered a cry of amazement, arsd the telegram fluttered from her fingers. Clifford Hawke, coming towards her at that moment, hastened to her side, but she waved him off imperiously. "Don't touch me—l cannot bear it!" "What folly is this?" he said sharply, then, as he saw the scrap of pink paper on the floor he changed colour, and stooping, picked it up. As he read it he muttered a curse beneath his breath, then turned to her curtly. "Do you take notice of this sort of thing?" he said contemptuously. "I am certainly amazed to hear that you had a wife in Australia," she said, "still snore to hear that you have a child living !" "Oh, pray ask any questions you like," he sneered. "I will answer as I would in the witness-box."</p>
        <p>"I will ask you one question," she said slowly, "And on your answer will depend the success of our future lives together. Why did you keep that first marriage a secret from met"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>BID FOR A BRIDE</head>
        <p>A BID FOR A BRIDE</p>
        <p>BLANCHE EARDLEY, Author of 'Kitty Bell—Actress," "The Lady</p>
        <p>Killer," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XIV.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>LATE MESSAGE.</head>
        <p>A LATE MESSAGE.</p>
        <p>The morning of the eleventh of September dawned, a fine beautiful day with just II "snap " in the air that made the brilliant sunshine all the more beautiful by contrast. Yet to Stella it was a day of grey skies and bleak aspect, for it was her wedding morning, not the glorious morning of that secret wed- ding at which her heart had leapt so short a time ago, but the fatal one ot show and empty pomp which was to make her the wife of Clifford Hawke. Since the day that Jasper Tennant had told her of his love, and she had. promised to wait for his telegram or a letter from Lady Mary, she had lived in an agony of suspense and won- der that had gradually givon way to a feeling of hot burning anger against herself for hav- ing listened for a moment to the lore-making of another man. He had sent no word, no sign even, and her father had not mentioned his name to her, nor had there been any ac- count of an accident in the pape7B. She could only gneiss that from his silence he had thought better of it, and had withdrawn his claim to her love. In the meantime aifford Hawke had told her that he had seen. Ja.spar in Ostend, and that he had appeared, to be doiag himself very well, and from then 'Stella had kept that brief interview to herself, and had let the arrange- ments for her werkfing to Clifford Hawke go On until the day itsvlf arrived. But now a fresh Terror seized her as she watched her maid dressing her hair for the ceremony that (mai to be the crowning joy in a girl's life, thi_t last jewel in the circlet of her womanhood. Ought she to have let this marriage go so far? she wondered anxi- ously. She did not love Clifford Hawke; she loved only one man, and he had, after wring- ing her confession of love from her, let her fall back into the arms of another man. Her maid glanced at the pale, beautiful face in the mirror with curious eyes. She had dressed many IL bride, but none that had looked like this cold, proud bride who was to marry a man whose devotion was the talk of the servants' hall. _ _ _</p>
        <p>As she went about her task with light fin- gers there was a tap at Utz door, and Isobel Frant came into the roozn..She was in a long loose morning wrap of green silk that suited her peculiar type of beauty and made her look more snake-like than ever. "I had to come in to say 'How do you do?' to the bride," she said lightly. " You have an ideal day for it, Stella; doesn't the old proverb say, Happy is the bride that the sun shines on'?" "Does it?" Stella replied dreamily. "I wonder if it is _true?"</p>
        <p>Isobel laughed. "Anyway, it ought to be true so far as you are concerned, my dear girl. It's not many people who marry adoring lovers like Clifford Hawke. I wonder if he will be an adoring husband?" • A slight shiver shook Stella's body. Al- ready she was feeling frightened at the idea of being Clifford Hawke's wife, and Isobel's sneering words pierced her like a knife. "Has Lady Mary come?" she said coldly. "She promised to be here early to help me." Isobel yawned. "I don't know. Lady Mary Stainer and I have clothing in common, and her presence in the house is never conveyed to me. I am only a poor relation, you know," she added laughingly, "of no account to anyone." As she listened to the bitter words Stella's heart melted into sudden pity. She had never understood her cold, hard cousin, but now something of the desolation in Isobel's voice found an echo in her own heart. As Isobel hated her dependence so she hated the marriage before her; her outlook was far more desolate than her cousin's. She reached out her hand impulsively. She felt she wanted the sympathy of a girl of her own age on this occasion. The maid had ler:; the room and they were alone. "Isobel," she said quickly, "I know you hate having no place of your own, but by and bye a man you love will come into your life, and then you will know what real happiness is, dear, and," she went on, not noticing the pallor of her cousin's face, "when we return from abroad you will come and stay with us, won't you?" "Stay with you! I come and live in your house and be a looker-on at the comedy of love as played by Clifford Hawke !" Isobel breathed sharply, while the pallor of her face gave way to two burning spots on each cheek. "I will never enter the house where either of you are, but I shall watch you, and be in- terested in seeing you as Clifford Hawke's wife. If ever I hated you," she went on breathlessly, "I should have my revenge to- day in seeing you marry him!" "Are you mad to speak to me like that!" Stella said after a pause. "I know no reason why you should hate me so bitterly; I have never done you any harm, and you speak as though I ha! Why do you resent my marry- ing Clifford Hawke?" A curious smile curved Isobel's lips. She had recovered her momentary loss of self- control, and only a patch of red remained of the rage that had gripped her a moment ago. "Ask Clifford Hawke himself," she said slowly; "he knows why I hate him—if he has not forgotten." Then before Stella could say another word she had turned her back and left the room. "Poor Isobel!" Stella murmured, "she is angry about something, but I can't understand what, unless " and a wave of sudden colour swept her pale face from brow to chin, "unless she is in love with him!" Then she clasped her hands tightly together. "If only she were marrying him to-day and I were free!" She hardly knew how the rest of the day passed. The house vvas teeming with excite- ment, and everything was a dream in which she moved as a shadowy dream figure. The church was full of guests, and the scent of the flowers made her head ache, while in all the crowd only one sympathetic face shone form. It was Lady Mary Stainer's, and as •their eyes met after the ceremony Stella saw that Lady Mary's were full of tears that moved her strangely. She was Clifford Hawke's bride now; nothing could untie that knot except death, and the girl shivered as she looked across the table at her bridegroom. In the laughter and gaiety that was going on no one noticed that she was very quiet, or that her face grew whiter and whiter as time went on. Isobel Frant stared curiously at her once or twice, and then turned to Clifford Hawke with a smile. "Your bride 'seems overpowered by the honour you have conferred upon her, Mr. Hawke!" He bit his lip savagely. Since that day when he had seen Isobel with a man thd living image of Paul Steinway he had been on tenter- hooks of suspense, wondering what she knew, and what she meant to do. Now the sight of Stella's deathly pale face was an irritant that spurred him to bitterness. She was his bride —yet looked as though she were going to her execution. He was being made a laughing stock—the object of amused contempt for marrying a girl who did not want him. He touched Stella's arm. "Can't you manage to look a little more cheerful if only for the sake of appearances?" he said swiftly. She flushed at his tone, but answered him quietly: "I am sorry I am so dull, but I have a dreadful headache; I aid not sleep last night." "I am sorry," he said awkwardly; "why not slip away and change your gown? It will soon be time to start for Beeehwood Towers."</p>
        <p>_ Lady Mary followed her to her room, and when they were alone she shut the door, and, taking Stella into her arms said tenderly: . "Don't loch Uk.o that., darling: Remember</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March, 16 1912.</p>
        <p>hotel 'and' faind wiiewaiting for Inc, which I suppose was a forgery. It said that a little artist's model, whom I have been helping, was in great trouble, and needed me at once. I went to the address given and found too late that it was a fraud."</p>
        <p>_ "Could you locate the house again?" the detective asked. "I don't know. It was like hundreds of houses in the same road, but there was one, thing I noticed, and that was that there seemed to be a peculiar smell of opium about the place. I believe I was drugged with it.' "Very likely. There are heaps of those dens about. Would you recognise the place again, or anyone you saw?" Jaspar shook his head. "No, I'm afraid not. You see I was in the dark all the time. It seems to me that I shall never know who my unknown host was." 'lt's early days yet,'- the detective said briskly; "but you take my word for it that this is only part of the other little job. It's a wonder you were not killed. They were too clever to do that. You say you came round in the cellar of an empty house in Greenwich?"</p>
        <p>"Yes, but I must have been taken there ; I know I went down to the docks. However, its a mystery, and likely to remain one." "Do you know anyone, sir, who owes you a grudge?" the detective asked presently. "No one who would do me a scurvy trick like that," Jaspar answered slowly; "at least, I don't think SO."</p>
        <p>"Yon never can tell," was the detective's crvntic answer. "Sometimes the man TOli</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CHAPTER XV.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XV.</p>
        <p>WIFE IN NAME ONLY.</p>
        <p>"I can't understand it! I feel as though I were dreaming; it's like a story from some blood and thunder 'shocker, and. if I were not so sure of you, sir, I should say you were suffering from delusions!" "I wish I were," Jasper Tennant said slowly; "better a delusion than an illusion/ But I shall soon be all right now, Scale, and shall be able to help you better to trace the author of this blackguardly outrage on me." The detective nodded. He was sitting in the private ward of the hospital where Jaspar had been taken, and WaS eyeing the young artist's face with professional curl- osity. "Yon see," he began, "I can't help con- necting this job with the disappearance of your papers. The one followed very quick upon the other." Jaspar shook his head. He was looking very weak and ill, and his head vvaz band- aged all over. "I don't think so," 111, said slowly. "I can't see how it could be; I calling on a lady in Park-lane," he flushed ana..u.„l,--.na from . there went .to</p>
        <p>think most above a low Ci.rk Will "se'rve you one in the dark." .</p>
        <p>"Perhaps you are right," tinspar said wearily. He was tired of talking. He wanted to lie and think of Stella, and won- dered whether Lottie had given her his MCA- sage. He had not seen ,Lottie sinee, and Lady Mary was coming for the second time that week to see him.</p>
        <p>"Before I go," Mr. Seafe said, "you will be interested to hear, at any rate, that I have obtained a faint clue regarding the cuff link I picked up in the studio that day." Jaspar's eyes opened again. "What is it?" he said quickly. lam anxious to get at the bottom of that almost before this affair." -"I went to every jeweller in London, and they all told me that it was an obsolete pattern, and from the make and style prob- ably made in the colonies. 'They were made from nuggets." "Nuggets !" Jaspar said slowly. "That's very interesting, considering it's a man who lived years ago in Australia for whom we are booking." "To-morrow I go down into the country and try to trace your sister," the detective went on. "You said she had lived some little time in a village in Devonshire, didn't you?" "Yes, it was Paignton, I remember, be- cause many letters came from there out to my father in Australia," Jaspar said. "I wonder if you will find anything out?" "I have all my notes in this little book," was the quiet reply. "I shall leave no stone unturned, you may aepend upon that. And you will find I am right about this being an offshoot of the other affair."</p>
        <p>As Jaspar lay there after his visitor had gone, his mind flew to Stella. Had she thought him very brutal? he wondered. Would she write to him when she heard from Lottie and Lady Mary how ill he had been? Did she still care?"</p>
        <p>When he opened his eyes again it was to find Lady Mary by his side. She was looking at him with tender pitying eyes. "My poor boy!" she said, "I was so dis- tressed to hear this. Who could have done it? I have known no peace of mind since!" Ile smiled.</p>
        <p>"Don't worry about it, dear. There's a chap at Scotland Yard going to look into it. I want you to tell me about her—about Stella," he went on, "how is she?" She laid her hand on his. "My dearest boy," she said gently, "I am afraid you have been very foolish. I did not know that you had both gone so far." He flushed warmly. "It was all my fault," he said quickly. "I told her I loved her the day I took Fifi to her, and I said I would call and see her father and ask him to give his consent to Stella breaking off her engagement to Clifford Hawke, whom she did not love, and if he refused, I had arranged to go and tell you everything and get you to help us like the dear you are." "And then your accident happened?" she said slowly. "I was very, anxious about you, and so was Stella, though she did not men- tion your name, but I could see. Then we both heard that you had been seen in Ostend alive and well." "In Ostend!" he exclaimed incredulously, "who on earth told you that? It was a lie —a foul lie. I went straight from Stella to the place in the East End where I thought Lottie was! Tell me from whom you heard it !''</p>
        <p>-- • "I can't really remeraber," she said slowly, "but never mind now, dear. What duo. it inannr so lor as von are11.,...1ive2"</p>
