      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>"It matters a great deal," he exclaimed quickly. "I would not for worlds have Stella believe that I would go about enjoy- ing myself at Ostend while I should have written to her. I sent her a message yester- day by Lottie," he added. "I hope she got it.</p>
        <p>Lady Mary started. "Yesterday? She,— she left town yesterday," she said slowly. He smiled. "She will come back when she gets that. Promise you will let me know and bring her to me." "My dear boy !" she exclaimed, "don't ask me any more questions. I can't promise any- thing—l—" She stopped as the door of the ward opened, and as the nurse admitted another visitor Jaspar gave a quick cry of surprised delight. "Stella!" he cried, "I knew you would come, darling; I was trapped and kept a prisoner for days, and only came to myself here, where Lady Mary brought Tr° the other day. You did believe in me, didn't you?" The girl brushed past the nurse, past Lady Mary's outstretched hand, and bending over the bed took both his hands in hers.</p>
        <p>"I came as soon as I could!" she said in a low steady voice. "I could not getaway be- fore; I was in the country." "I was telling Lady Mary that I knew you would understand when you got my mes- sage," Jaspar went on. "I came here from the place where they found me in Green- wich. It's a long story, dear." "You have been hurt seriously?" Stella said breathlessly. . He rave a boyish laugh.</p>
        <p>"It was not pleasant at tne time; but I'm all right now. But I'd like to know who it was that told you I was at Ostend?" he added.</p>
        <p>Stella raised her head and gazed at Lady Mary. "I can't remember," she said slowly, "but we will talk about things later on when you are better. I am glad to have seen you again; we shall meet at Lady Mary's, and now, dear, I must say good-bye," she added, "I came to see Lady Mary, and did not know at first that you were here." A few moments later she and Lady Mary were sitting in the electric brougham on their way to the latter's house. There was a long silence between them, then Lady Mary said at last: "Stella! Am I mad or were you married yesterday to Clifford Hawke? What is the meaning of this strange appearance without your husband?" Stella drew in her breath sharply. Irer face was very pale, but her eyes were bright with unshed tears.</p>
        <p>"I married Clifford Hawke yesterday, but left him last night. I can never go back to I him." . .</p>
        <p>(To be Continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EDUCATION CHANGES.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION CHANGES.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE COMMITTEE'S TEN YEARS' RECORD.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Cheshire Education COM* mittee took place at Cterve on Monday. Dr: W Hodgson was unanimously re-elected chairman. Sir El Baker- Wilbraham was re-elected vice-chairman. The Chairman said the work of the committee bad been very arduous and required a great deal of sacrifice on the part of the members. Vast changes had taken place in the work of education in Chestme since the Act, of 1902. At that time there were 323 non-provided schools in the county, end only 28 provided schools. Thirty-two of the non-provided schools had been trans- ferred to the county and 29 non-provlded schools bad been closed. They had built or were in process of finishing 44 NF,W COUNCIL ncltoots so that the County schools had increased to 104. At the present time about two-thirds of the schools in the county were non-provided school., and one-third Council schools. If they considered the figures they would see the enormous amount of work the committee had been doing in order to carry on satisfactorily the business of education. They bad also built a large number of secondary schools. They bad taken over grammar schools where they could, or pro- vided entirely new schools. They had 19 practically new secondary schools. In addition they had taken over from a private company the ih'orleston Dairy Institute, which had trained and turned out 1,500 dairy maids, and whose influence had vastly improved Cheshire cheese and increased its wide reputation. They had also established an agricultural and horticultural college at Holmes Chapel, which was becoming more appreciated ; they had instituted agricultural courses ot education, advanced physical training, and after much labour and trouble they were bringing to completion the county I training college for teachers. These and other things represented a vast amount of th 3 work accomplished. But the Board of Education had</p>
        <p>NO MORAL RIGHT to enforce on them rapidly altering regulations without in some way conferring with them, and giving them decent time to adjust themselves to altered conditions. It was high time they had some settled state of things and that they knew where they were. (Applause.) For some sears there had been an outcry against the increasing cost of education, and they had some right to ask for a truce as to requiring larger contributions. Education would become intensely disagreeable and unpopular unless the Government took on itself a fairer share of the burden. (Applause).</p>
      </div>
