        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Irreparably Damaged.—lt has now become the window-smashing campaign of the EC suffragists will prove most disastrous to tilt which these misguided women have at heart. those members of Parliament who have declaree„ selves willing to support the suffrage movetue'' been goaded by the outrageous action of the lel Into open opposition to any measure for eti enfranchisement. It is easy to argue that thee of the cause is not altered by the fanatical actio° comparatively few hysterical women, but the e° not ruled absolutely by logic.Publio sentiment be outraged by the utterly illogical conduct of the,' destroyers, and this feeling will find expression?", the discussion and in the lobbies, when the Con' Hill is dealt with in the House of Commons 00 22. The militants argue that nearly all great e! have been won by violence, in some form or 00, the violence which has its origin in moments of ° ment is quite a different thing to cold-blooded ci lated destruction, which it is vainly suppeso terrorise a peace-loving public. Miss Pankhurst.—Miss Christabel Ps nithurst el issued a manifesto attempting to justify the 01 private property, is thirty years of age. She ie educated woman, and was at school in Switzer' the time her father died, when she was sevetlE72 year later she took up political work, and I" became a member of the Executive Committee North ,f England Society for Women's Suffregi t. was also on the Committee of the Manchester Union Council. Two years later, she, and her 1 formed the Women's Social and Political VII . .</p>
        <p>which Miss Pankburst is the organi;ing—s.ec- retarl. and Miss Annie Kenney were the first women prison for the cause. That was in 1904, has been twice in prison since thethoi in 1907, when she served a for; imprisonment, and again in 1908. Apar'd the methods, loading to such experiences, on hardly possible not to make that exceptioo. l'ankhurst is essentially womanly in all thing°,l favourite even with those who do eot share her 17° views. ri</p>
        <p>Materials for Coats and Skirts.—A new tailoreO; an indispenable addition to the spring wardreror the wise woman with a limited allowance,eb" seal material that will be useful throughout the CO' and be still availalable for wear when auturns re, round. Serge of course will always be to the fe,ot the new shot designs, also the pin stripes, `ect mther suggested than &amp;fined are novel and a Wort Whipcord is a new material with a velvetY e at that is warranted not only to look well but to very durable. An old friend—faced cloth-5b,l material for the spring suit—is shows in all and lovely shades, while taffeta is unquestions" smartest fabric for dressy toilets. , deed' 1 A Red Year.— lie or she would I.e bold to rill I at this early date presumed to speak with authol the subject of the particular colour that Wi4st ' above its fellows, this summer for Fashion's f't, fickle as an April day, must be taken into accoeeriol it is quite safe to predict that this will be C sPet every colour. The effect, however will not he „"7 but bright and gay, in unison with the youth 0, year, for good taste will preside at Fashion's fetiwist how welcome will be the brightness after 0 sombre, melancholy shades ! Prophets, all tuerest are not wanting, arid a weighty authority er,esS sart anal rashly predicts that this will be a reo, J, uses the term in its widest sense and includer,io ranging from bright scarlet, through cerise,riti I end magentas, to maroon—all rose hues fro:air' ,a to damask ; azalla pink and reds ; old red 6 tit, rose and in addition those shades of red that sre with purple or yellow. Colours for Youth and Age.—Yellow, ming „nej mauve, green and even Chinese blue, will be pt"iii —in evening toilettes. Indeed quite a vogue eg dicted far Chinese yellow. Fair girls geoeralli bee 1 this colour, in all its tints, yet it is particularlY ing to them. Needless to say, during the dslir must be sparingly used, but it goes well with le oe I and can be safely employed for all millinery Pool I even as hat brims—if relieved with some otherdrei such as azure blue or dark green. Children are 10 to-day in sombre shades, for out-door wear at setiep such for instance as steel grey, black, and the Pot 84 browns, and as they grow older, they are proul_abe paler hues, until at the age of sixteen or ther"ot they are qualified to wear whites. A chic over,e,ell' a child of six is of black satin, wit's revers 0, Joe pale green or vermillion. The elderly are by lflo forgotten by dyers this year, a rich toi,t'gref sapphire blue (that contrasts beautifully wi° tool white hair), limpet grey, and many sh ides of Br brown being most suitable.</p>
