        <p>by Misses May White and Mollie Cookson and Masters W Cookson and H Jacl s )n. Miss D Jones played in capital style for the Cannes and Mr II Dearden was a very effioient accompsnitt to Mr tlabbitt.—A vote of thanks was proposed by Mr J W Bouchier to all the performers and Miss Brown for the very excellent programn:e, and this was seconded by Mr Brook and carried with acclamation. Mr G J Brown responded and thanked the very large audience for their appreciation of the performance :and said he would also like to include the parents for their great help in p:oviding the costumes for the children.—Great credit is due to Miss Brown for the excellent manner in which all the performers had been trained, and as a result a very substantial sum will be adCed to the foes of a most deserving Association.</p>
        <p>Lymm. " TfIE FOLLIES."--A travelling company called "The Follies " paid a visit to Lymm on Wednesday night, when they performed a mest acceptable programme in the Drill Hall before a large audience. Local musical talent was put to the test in a competion for humorous singing, the prize being ultimately won by Mr McCue. A prize for the best head of lady's hair was secured by Miss Katie Griffiths.</p>
        <p>LYAIM URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION.—The following is the c.fficial :1 t of nominations.ln Boothstlill Ward, the retiring councillor, Mr John Hardy Holt, builder and contractor, will be opposed by Mr Thomas Frederick Walpole Dixon, iron and steel merchant. In Heatley Ward, the retiring councillor is Mr Thomas Henry Warburton, basket manufacturer, who will be opposed by Mr Peter Shorrocks, cashier. In Lymm Ward, the seat will be contested by Mr Richard Harry Gotohed, draper (the retiring member) and Mr Frederic Ernest Evans. chem:st. The date of the poll is Satur- day, March 30th.</p>
        <p>LECTURE AT OUGHTEINGTON.-A lecture on a tour from Manchester to Edinburgh and the Clyde past was given by Mr W 0 Limb, on Monday, under the auspices of the Oughtrington Mutual Improvement Society. After showing numerous views of the Firth, beautiful Lochs, far famed Kyles of Bute,and the lovely watering places on the shores, 'Mr W G Limb concluded his lecture by throwing on the screen pictures showing the glories and wild beauties of that splendid island jewel, Arran. At the contusion a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Limb for his able and descriptive lecture was pro- posed by Mr T Jones, seconded by Mr W Thomas and carried.</p>
        <p>MAY QUEFN FESTIVAL.-A public meeting was held in Hurst's Pavilion on Friday the Bth lust, to bear the report of the sub-committee formed at the last public meeting, as to whether it wore advisable to hold the May Queen Festival this year. A resolution was passed that the ceremony be held on Whit Thursday subject to a guarantee fund of £5O being obtained on or before Tuesday, March 26th. The following officers and com- mittee were then provisionally elected --Mr J D B Smith, chairman; Mr A H Hudson, vice-chairman ; Mr P Cooke, hon. treasurer ; Mr J Webb, jun., hon. secretary ; Mesdames Cooke, Smith, Jones, Misses M Daniels, Moores, Messrs T Nash, F W Druce, T Kay, F CocilJ, J H Warham, C Richards, II S Saville, jun., I` Saville, A C Saville, T Eaton, H Hesketh, S W Gould. W Daniels, A Forrest, S Ingle- field, jun., P Ardern, NV Seymour, and NV H Blount. Messrs A II Fray and T Kay, hon. auditors. A vote of thanks to Mr F NV Ashton for his past services as hon. secretary was carried with loud applause. In replying, Mr Ashton stated that he was sorry to sever his con- nection with the festival, but owing to his removal to Stockton Heath he was compelled to do so.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN SUNDAY SenooL.—An entertainment on behalf of the funds of the Wesleyan Sunday school, was held before a full attendance on Wednesday night. The programme consisted of musical items illustrated by living statuary. Messrs Krekchmer Bros gave some capital pianoforte duets and made efficient accompanists Mr Rudolph Krekchmer's violin solos wore heartily applauded. The pictures of " Darby and Joan," solo by Mr J H Brazendale, " Courtship " song by Mr E Waterworth, "A Kiss First," pianoforte duet by Messrs Krehcbmer Bros., and "Britannia and her subjects " were perhaps the most popular combined items. MiS9 Winstanley sang "Rock of Ages," and Miss A Brundrett " When the heart is young." Miss Moston's recital " The Rehearsal" received an encore. The Rev Colly Hutchinson whilst officiating as chairman announced that he had been suddenly called to leave Warrington district for another circuit, and expressed his regret at having to part from the friends he had only so recently made. Mr H S Brown, on behalf of the Lvmm Wesleyan society, declared his sorrow at their having to loose Mr Hutchinson, and hoped he would find success and happiness in his new circuit. LYAIM CDORAL SOCIETY.—The local Choral Society held a musical and social evening on Thursday last, the venue being the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, which some of the ladies had tastefully decorated for the occasions. The cLoristers and their friends assembled in force, and thoroughly enjoyed a programme consisting of solos by Madame Wilson, Miss Greta Welford, Mr R Hillier and Master Andrews ; humorous items by Mr Schofield ; and h musical trio consist•ng of cello, violin, and piano, provided by Messrs l? Champ, Burrell and Cooke respectively. When Mr T M Eastbam retired from the conductorship at the end of last season a fund was inaugurated for the purpose of providing him with a souvenir of his con- nection with the Society. Mr Easth,m had been the conductor of the Society from its formation up to the time of his retirement, and on Thursday evening he was presented with a set of three silver vases in recognition of his valuable services. Mr Edward ITaylor made the presentation on behalf of past and present members of the society, Mr Eastham replying in su:table terms. After partaking of the various edible 1 delicacies provided for their delectation the company indulged in games and competitions. CONGREGATIONAL TEA AND LECTURE.—On Sunday last, the Rev A Penelegan of Bucklow Hill, preached two excellent sermons in the Congregational Church.— On Monday night, the Congregationalists held their annual tea and lecture, both or which were very well attemied. The Rev II Dunnico, of Liverpool, delivered an address on " The Church and the Social Problem." Mr Donnie° referred to the debt of gratitude that we of the twentieth century owe to the missions of Rome, Greece, and even the Hebrews—the originators of monotheism. God gave distinctive missions to Luther, Cromwell, Wesleys, Whitfield, etc.' and he believed that God still speaks to the world, in fact to-day's spirit of unrest in Russia, Turkey, France, Germany, Chine, and even the present coal and other strikes are not things to be despondent about, as it ls God calling for a higher aim. The social problem is a most difficult one and the lecturer could only dwell on one phase of it, namely that of poverty. He next spoke of the increased productive cape city of our country, food, clothing, az., having during the last 100 years multiplied say about 50 times while the population has only increased four times, with the result that we have greater poverty at the present. Facing the problem, not as but re t as men and women, and le from the doctor we must get to w tpilostelotaieecuxinmstaaellasbn' o before we or rather Nature, can effect k partytanko i taking g o iim p a cure. Poverty in this country is due to any one, or a combination of (1) insufficient production, (2) waste, (3) utuqual distribution of the wealth earned. Ile then pointed out how that it could not be due to "cause 1," but " cause 2 " was a partial reason, especially in the case of drunkenness and yet if there were a total prohibition of intoxicants and if all the persons in the United Kingdom wasted nothing on luxuries, even the money thus saved would not provide the necessities of life. He said it must not be thought he was opposed to temperance, he was not, but he believed in making the surroundings better first and then preaching temperance reform afterwards. Therefore the chief cause of poverty must be due to " cause 3 " i.e. unequal distribution of the wealth earred. He showed how that a century ago men were able to provide the majority of their own necessities, i.e. they were individualistic in their industries, but now, with steam and other power we have factories and works making men dependent upon one another. Therefore as the work has been made collective or socialised, so must the wealth be. The duty of the Church was to crush out the sweating industries and other social evils; to create a condition of Socialism that shows that God is low. True patricts can make this earth a happy biding place where there will be no unappeased hunger. In years to come many of the things we know at present will have passed away and a world-wide brotherhood having the spirit of Christ will be known instead. A vote of thanks pro- posed by Mr. Smith, seconded by Mr STI Holt and supported by Mr George Willan was heartily passed, Rev H Dunnico suitably replying. The singing of the Doxology brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND ' is a good Cough Mixture. Effective and Economical. 01 Chemists only.---Advt.</p>
        <p>Grand Master Herbert Whitelegg, in responding to the toast of the "Loyal rratTord ' Lodge' said they in Lymm were glad to see so many members of other lodges in the Warrington district present especially so many who bad served the Order as P.P.G.M. As regarded the Lymm lodge, it was interesting to know that it had now bcen in existence over 80 sears, having been founded in 1833. During the present year he came of age as an Oddfellow, and he hoped that during his apprenticeship he had done good work for the members. (Applause.) When he joined the lodge in 1891 there were 103 members and the reserve fund amounted to £4lO. At the present time they had 106 members in Lymm, and the reserve fund stood at £1,452. (Applause). That was a record of which they were very proud. Another item worthy of notice was that during that period they had paid out in sick ray to members £1,803 Ifs Bd. During the evening a framed certificate was presented by Bro. Challenor and songs were rendered by Messrs. Broadhurst, Henry Thoruason, Holt, Brooks and Henry Bennett. Messrs. Stanley and Fred Webster kindly acted as accompanists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>"BEER FOR A SICK HORSE."</p>
        <p>Mary Jane Dutton, licensee of the Foresters' Arms, Canal-street, Runcorn, was summoned on Monday for opening the houie during prohibited hours. The police evidence showed that at 12.50 in the early morning of February 29th Superintendent Ennion saw the licensee's husband leave the house with a quart of beer ins bottle. When spoken to, he said it was to warm for a sick horse with which he and another man were sitting up. The owner of the horse said she knew nothing about the matter.—Mr. Browne, for the defence, inquired whether Superintendent Ennion would ask for a conviction against a licensee who was roused in the night for brandy in case of illness. The Superinten- dent gave his opinion that it would only be safe for a licemee to Eupply a doctor.—Mr Browne said the licensee's husband humanely brought a quart of beer for a sick horse, and had no intention of charging for it. Warm beer was ordered for the animal by a vetinary surgeon. Dutton brought it in an uncorked bottle which had contained linseed oil,and it was ridiculous to suppose it was intended for human consumption.—ln dismissing the case, the Bench said it certainly was one for inquiry. RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS. At Duttoreon Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding. RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Local Government Board relative to the appointment of chaplains at the Work- house and stating that the Guardians were empowered to appoint and pay religious instructors of denomina- tions other than the Church of England without any sanction on the part of the Board. The Clerk : We kcew all that before. Mr Morris ; I take it we are just where we were before. The Clerk : Certainly.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND THE STRIKE. "VERY FORTUNATE POSITION."</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Council meeting, on Tuesday Coun- cillor Smith, moving the adoption of the Gas and Water Committee's minutes, said the engineer had been com- pelled to buy coal outside the contract price to the extent of £l,OOO, so with economics they could hold out for six weeks. They were in a very fortunate posi- tion. ' _</p>
        <p>Councillor Garghan asked why the price of coke was raised to small consumers on February 26, Councillor Smith said it had been left to the discretion of the engineer, and he had not taken any undue advantage. Councillor Garghan intimated that the price had been raised from 8d to 11d. Councillor D Davies said the Manchester and Salford Corporations bad reduced their price to 6d per cwt, and moved an amendment that Widnes shoulot do likewise. Councillor Smith said th 3 Gas Committee, prior to the Council meeting, bad decided to leave the price at 8d to small consumeis only.</p>
        <p>TO MEET DISTRESS. In view of the granting of £ICO to the M‘yor on the occasion of the opening of the new wing of the Technical school, Councillor Garghan asked that £lOO be set aside as the nucleus of a fund to meet the distress in the town, and also inquired when would they put into force the Feeding of School Childen Act. Alderman G Neil said Widnes would not be in the background when it became necessary. The Mayor stated that a fund already existed, and inquiries would at once be set on foot to find urgent cases. If the situation became acute, he purposed calling the manufacturers to tai,..e money to meet the trouble'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CREDIT_ TO THE GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>A CREDIT_ TO THE GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman mentioned that there %las a young man in the neighbourhcod en holiday who came into the House when three years of age and had been brought up in the House. He was now a valet to a Liverpool corn merchant who was away in America and the young man had come over to see his friends. He had looked in at the House end was now a smart young fellow, very well dressed end very respectable. lie mentioned it for the information of the Board who would see that their work was not always in vain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>OLD-AGE PENSION AND ITS EFFECT ON PAUPERISM..</head>
        <p>OLD-AGE PENSION AND ITS EFFECT ON PAUPERISM..</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted a report with reference to the list of out-door and in-door relief, and also lunatics in the Runcorn Union. The number on the hooks in receipt of out-door relief in July, 1910 was 568, and in July, 1911, 402, a decrease of 168. In 1910 the amount expended in out-door relief was £3,132 as compared with £1,772 in 1911, a decrease in the amount of £1,360 equal to a rate of lid in the £ for the full year. The number on the books in receipt of in-door relief in July, 1910, was 214, and in 1911, 212, a decrease of 2, and the cost of maintenanne about the same. Tee number of lunatics in the Asylum in December, 1907, was 93 ; in 1908,88 ; in 1909, 87 ; in 1910, 87 ; and in 1911, 88 ; equal to one in every 512 of the population. Excluding paupers over 70 years of age, there was an increase in the number of recipients of 26 in the year, or comparing the total number there was a reduction of 193 equal to a 20 per cent reduction. Taking Ithe whole of the county the reduction worked out at 15 per cent, and for the whole of England and Wales 16 per cent, so that their district showed up rather well. Mr Morris : I understood the Clerk to say there was no such word as pauper used officially. Mr Linaker : fie did not say that. _</p>
        <p>Mr Morris : Well I underst4ood that. Mr Linaker In the returns it is the only word you can use. Mr Morris : The proper term is deserving. (Hear, hear.) I only want the matter clear because I have been criticised a bit on that point. I did not use the word but I understood the clerk to say it was not used officially. The Clerk : No I was only speaking individually but here it is used collectively. Mr Baxter did not see how it was possible to do without such a word to describe the difference between persons who had become chargeable to the Union and those aho bad striven to keep themselves up. Mr Mclntyre : It is entirely a matter of sentiment. (Hear, hear), AIR PILLING'S ILLNESS. It was maintained that Mr Pilling was unable to be I present owing to illness and on the motion of Mr. I Woodward it was agreed that a message of sympathy be sent to Mr Pilling with the Boards hope for a speedy recovery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>CHAPLAIN POI: TIIE</head>
        <p>CHAPLAIN POI: TIIE</p>
        <p>Mr Morris reported that the House Committee had met that morning to discuss the matter and after con- sideration had decided to recommend thelappointment of the Rev. L. J. T. Darvrell, of Walton Vic4rage, at a salary of £3O a year to take the services at the Union at nine o'slock each Sunda; morning. Mr Linaker asked if the committee had considered the advisability of holding services at 4.15 in the after- noon, as he understood one cf the clergymen who bad been written to bad expressed his willingness to take the appointment if the services were fixed for that hour. Mr Morris said this had been fully considered and they had taken the advise of the master who informed them that 4.15 would be a most inconvenient hour.</p>
        <p>NO FIR:II:WOOD ctiorPINT.. The Visiting Committee reported the receipt of a letter from Mr. It. G. Duff, Local Government Board Inspector, suggesting that the Guardians consider the advisability of obtaining a stock of firewood to provide work for men who might become chargeable to the Union awing to the existing coal strike. The Com- mittee decided that the conditions at the Workhouse did not allow of firewood chopping and bundling to be undertaken and no action was taken. The number of inmates last week was stated to be 188 as against 184 last year and the number of vagrants Sias against 92 last year.</p>
        <p>Messrs. William Motherwell and Co.'s extensive bolt and rivet works in Portman-street, Glasgow, were gutted by lire last night, and damage estimated at £5,C00 was done, A fortnight ago Miss Jane Ellen. Williams left ber brother's house at Nantyr, near Llangollen, to walk over the Glyn mountains to visit friends at Llangollen. She never arrived at her destination, and has not since been seen.</p>
