      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn7d3Z;ned.licer;ll7.);;;;i;3s on Monday Mr. Knowles, Widnes, applied for sanction for certain structural alterations to the Watermen's Arms, Queen- street, and said they were absolutely Lecessary iron. a sanitary point of view. The police objector', on the ground of redundancy, to the renewal of the n'oresters' Arms, Canal-street, and the Manchester House, Bridge-street, and in the event of the alterations to the Watermen's Arms, being granted to the owners, Messrs. Greenell, Whitley, and Co., were willing that the Manchester House should be given up. Mr. Knowles pointed out that they would also require a renewal of the Forester's Arms. In the last three years Messrs. Greenell, Whitley, and Co., had surrendered about sixteen licences. _</p>
        <p>The chairman said the beech appreciated the fact that Messrs. Greenell, Whitley, and Co. had always endeavoured to carry out the wishes of the magistrates. The bench granted the alterations to the Watermen's Arms, and renewed the Foresters' Arms. The Man- chester House was referred.</p>
        <p>The police also objectedito therenewal of the Queen's Head, High-street, and it was referied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>WELSH DISESTABLISHMENT.-EARLESTOWN</p>
        <p>CHURCHMEN'S PROTEST."</p>
        <p>"That this meeting is of opinion that the dismem- berment of the Church and the secularisation of funds given to and urgently needed for religious purposes will be an injustice to the Church and ',injurious to the cause of Christianity in general." This resolution was carried at a crowded meeting of Churchmen held on Monday night in Earlestown Town Hall, and was the united protest of the Churchmen of Earlestown, Newton-le-Willows and district. ' MR. J. A. SEDDON AND " F.E."</p>
        <p>"DELUDING THE ELECTORATE."</p>
        <p>"THE IRREDUCIBLE MINIMUM."</p>
        <p>• The quarterly conference of the Newton Division Labour party was held on Saturday at Blackbrook, when about 200 delegates were present. Mr W Whittleld, of Haydock, presided. In the evening a public meeting was held. Mr J A Seddon supported a resolution, which was carried unanimously, expressing sympathy with the miners, and hoping they would stand firm until victory Ni es secured. He said he came in contact with the commercial middle class, and one thing that stood out was the colossal ignorance of the so-called educated people of the claims and conditions of the miners. That class was against the miner because of the food they got from the Press, and he hardly found one paper that had not entered into league against the miners. When Mr. F. E. Smith stated at Manchester that the danger of industrial war- fare was never heard of until the Budget of 1910, he was either deluding the electorate or was guilty of a calculated, frigid lie. For the first time it was recognised that the miner was not only a factor in national working but the very foundation of our national prosperity ; yet the Government stood powerless to deal with the problem which had arisen, and which the Labour Party asked should be settled according to the modest demands of the miners—namely, by the granting of the irreducible minimum. He denied that the battle waging was that of the agitators. It was the miners' battle, and he had never heard a single collier say anything except that he had got to stick out until they had got what they wanted. That would have to be. If the governing bodies were not prepared to take the proper road to do it, they had got to get out of the road, because the problem had got to be solved in one way only, and Hilt was by granting the miners' demands. (Applause). Mr. H. Twist, Wigan miners' agent, also supported. In the early days of the dispute they were largely mis- represented by the Conservative Press, but latterly, said he the Liberal Press had also misrepresented them. The public were asked to telieve that the miners were taking ups stiff-necked attitude, and even some of the Labour members of Parliament who were not miners— one of them no other than Philip Snowden—had fallen into the mistake of believing that they were refusing to negotiate upon the schedule rates submitted for each district. That was an absolute blunder and mistake which could not be made if they took two minutes to inquire from a man who was in touch with the actual affairs. The door of negotiation had been held open time after time by the miners' hand, and yet the public were asked to believe that it was due to the miners' stupidity, selfishness and greed that the present dead- lock bad arisen, and the nation been laid in a state of siege, drifting to the verge of civil war and rushing into famine. The strike was not going to end this week. He wished it might, but he could see it lasting at the very least another month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>NEW COUNTY COURT REGISTRAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ert;et. W.Swift, ‘-'o "o -S-L-fielen's-h.as been appointed registrar of the St Helens and Widnes county courts, in succession to the late Mr W Tyrer. Mr Swift is a son of the late Mr Thomas Swift, barrister, of Liverpool. He was born in St Helens at the time his father resided at Hardshaw Hall, now the Provi- dence Hospital. Mr Swift was articled with his father and was admitted a solicitor in May, 1889. He was partrer for some time with his brother, the late Mr J 0 Swift, and latterly with Mr Thomas Garner. He is a well-known alvocate in the St Helens, Newton, Woolton, Warrington, and neighbouring courts. He sat for North Eccleston on the St Helens Town Council from 1902 to 1905, and is a co-opted member of the St Helens Education Committee. At the last election he acted as agent for his brother, Mr Rigby Swift, M.P</p>
        <p>"BENEFICIAL CIGARETTES." ALLEGED DEFRAUDING OF TOBACCONISTS. At St. Helens, on Tuesdsy, James Mackay, traveller, of Leeds, was charged on remand with obtaining £2 Its, by false pretences from John Walden, tobacconist,l3, Westfield-street, and XI Is. from Fanny Cooper, tobacconist and sweet dealer. Mr E %V Swift defeneed.</p>
        <p>Mr Haslam Fox prosecuted for the police, and said that Mackay described himself as a herbal cigarette merchant, of Briggate, Leeds, and stated that the cigar- ettes were of considerable value to athletes, cyclists. motorists, and golfers. The cigarette was made of dock and coherent leaves and a certain quantity of stramonium. Mackay alleged that they had an exten- sive sale, and that they were beneficial, whereas Dr. Buch,n, the medical officer, said that, instead of being beneficial, the smoking of them would tend to shorten a man's wind and induce fatigue after exercise. Ile told the customer that he was anxious to open a depot, and that a shopkeeper who purchased goods from him would be made sole agent for the district. His name would be advertised, and he would be allowed 10s. a mOnth. Walden gave evidence of the transaction on Novem- ber 1, when he consented to become agent and to purchase £2 14s worth of cigarettes which were after- wards sent him from Leeds. He tried the cigarettes and decided that they were not likely to sell, lie sent them back to Leeds, but got no reply to his communi- cations, and his money was not returned. It was found that the place where the accused was supposed to have his business premises was simply an office, and it was alleged that it was a bogus business. Dr Buchan gave evidence, and said the cigarettes were good for asthma but injurious to athlates and sportsmr n. The Clerk expressed the opinion that no case of false pretences had been made out. Mr. Swift also argued that it was a civil matter. _ .</p>
        <p>The bench, after considering in private, dismissad the charges.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WORKHOUSE MASTER RESIGNS.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>MR. J. F. AWBERY LEAVING WARRINGTON. A Gift to the Deserving Poor. The members of the Warrington Board of Guardians heard with deep regret on Wednesday that, acting on medical advice, Mr. J. F. Awbery, the esteemed master of the Workhouse, had decided to resign his appointment. Since early in October Mr. Awbery has been confined to his bed with a serious internal complaint, but happily he is now making good progress towards recovery. At the same time, Dr. Monsarrat, a Liverpool specialist, has certified that he is permanently incapacitated from dis- charging the duties of his office, and there was consequently no alternative but to resign. Mr. Awbery has been advised to seek a change of climate and it is his intention, as soon as his health will allow, to journey to Lucerne, Switzerland. His departure from Warrington will be regretted by a host of friends, for there is no more popular figure in local official circles, Mr. A wbery came to Warrington nearly nine years ago to become the master of the Workhouse, and his administration of the institution has been on the most sympathetic and efficient lines. His many kindnesses to the inmates have caused them to regard him as a true friend and to no-one will his resignation cause more sorrow than to the poor who have been under his care. It will be recalled that last summer Mr. Awbery created no small sensation in the town by winning the second prize in the Calcutta Derby sweep- stakes, his half share in the ticket entitling him to a fortune of 5:18,000. Congratulations were showered upon him by hosts of friends and it is characteristic of Mr. Awbery's goodness of heart that, on resigning, I e has offered to devote his superannuation allowance to the creation of a special benevolent fund for the relief of deserving cases which are not legally entitled to assistance from the rates. . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Awhery is a native of Dover and has been associated with Poor Law work all his life. At the age of twenty-three he was appointed master of the Workhouse at Uttoxeter (Staffs) and held the position until accepting his appointment at Warrington. Dr. Taylor, resident medical officer at the Work- house, has also resigned his appointment in order to accompany Mr Awhery as his medical man. Testimony to the value of his conscientious and skilful services to the Institution was freely borne at the meeting of the Loard of Guardians on Wednesday and his resignation was also regretfully accepted. Awbery's resignation was communicated to the Board in the following letter : " The verdict of Mr. Monsarrat, the Liverpool specialist, who performed my operation and who has since been in attendance upon me, renders it impera- tive that under the medical certificate herewith attached I should resign in accordance therewith my appoint- ment as Master of the Warrington Union Workhouse and avail myself of the provisions of the Poor Law Officers' Superannuation Act 1896. The physical necessity for this course of action terminates my Poor Law career and shatters also many aspirations I had hoped to remain in the service to see realised. The wrench is a great one to me because I have always regarded and valued my work as being something more than mere routine occupation. In Warrington and also in that outer world of Poor Law activity I have never hesitated to identify myself with progres- sive organisations and movements which have sought improvement and development of our Poor Law system upon a sound and not upon a fallacious basis. In doing this, I trust I have never merited the reproach that I have been unmindful or neglectful of those interests which the Guardians are elected to represent and pro- tect. I further trust that my relations with the Board's officers, both outdoor and indoor, have been of a character that has cemented friendship as well as securing smoothness of administration, and that so far as my own staff are concerned their comfort and the cooditions of their employment have always been a constant thought and care with me. As regards the poor placed under my charge, I trust here also that my well meant efforts and endeavours for their happiness and welfare have not been destitute of success, but that to some degree my sympathies, my methods and even my manner of address to them, may after all have reconciled many to a changed condition of life it had been their misfortune to encounter. In this enforced surrendering of my appointment I hope I take with me the good wishes as well as the respect and esteem of every member of your Board, the whole of the officers connected with the Union, and the goodeill of the inmates also. My unstinted thanks are due to your Board for the unlimited kindness and practical sympathy you have $o generously extended to me during my long and serious illness. and I am glad to be able to emphasise the fact that; this expression of thanks applies to the individuality of the Board as well as to its collective constitution. To Mr. Bottomley I must pay a tribute he richly deserves. When unable to direct affairs either official or p.ivate be has been a staunch and never failing friend. He has stood and acted where I possibly could not. With a renewal of my warmest Milks for many favours and kindnesses to your Board and my fellow officers, I beg to remain, Ladies and Gentlemen, Your obedient servant, JOHN FREDERICK AWBERY, (Master). The Cflatum AN (Mr. G. Mather) said that under the circumstances they could do nothing but accept the resignation, but he was sure he was only expressing the feelings of every member when be said that they did so with the deepest regret. Their sympathies went out to the master and the matron. A better master than Mr. Awbery it would be impossible to find. (Hear, bear.) If any criticism could be levelled against Mr. or Mrs. Awbery, it would be for being too kind, if such a thing were possible. They had been kindness itself to the inmates as well as to the officials who worked under them, and they had always been very dutiful to the Board. In their management of the house they had been economical and clean—everything, in fact, that could be desired—and he felt sure the Board would have great difficulty in adequately filling their places. He moved that the resignation be accepted. Mr E L BENT, in seconding, said Mr Awbery's dis- position towards everyone with whom he had cume into</p>
        <p>contact had been kindness itself and hi 3 management of the institution highly efficient. Mr A J JOLLEY said be had been very closely associ- ated with the administration of the Woikhouse for many years and he could confidently ray that none of the words yet uttered had been too high in praise and commendation of the admirable work which had been done by Mr and Mrs Awbery. They followed an extremely able master and matron—Mr and Mrs Gates, who were only at the institution for a short time—bat their work would bear favourable comparison even with that of their immediate predecessors. Mr. Awbery had a sound knowledge of the principles that underlay scientific Poor Law administration and in the practical applica- tion of that knowledge he had always shown a most kindly, humane feeling in his relationship to the poor people under his care, and tact and courtesy towards the Guardians with whom be had been in contact, and some- times—but very rarely—in conflict, and towards the staff working under him. He (Mr. Jolley) had had con- siderable experience of a large number of workhouses in the country and he could frankly say that he had never been in any Workhouse that was better adminis- tered than their own. The more he had seen of Mr. Awbery's work the more he appreciated his genuine worth in every department of administration and in every personal and official relation of his career. Mr HOUGUITON expressed the hope that the malady from which Mr Awbery was suffering would soon be cured and that he would be spared for years to enjoy the rest he had se well earned by his v ork in Warring- ton and other Unions. He (the Ep?aker) did not know whether the loss was the greatest to the Board, the officials, the inmates or the general body of ratepayers. Mr A BROOKS said that as one of the committee of two who visited Mr and Mrs Awbery at Uttoxeter before they came to Warrington he formed very high hopes indeed of what would be the result if they were appointed. Those hopes bad been fully realized. Mr FURNESS said he had nothing but feelings of admiration for the way in which Mr Awbery bad managed the institution not only as a master but as a broad-minded man who had taken a very wide concep- tion of his duties.</p>
        <p>Mrs. SIMPSON said that as one who was very intimate with Mr and Mrs Awbery and who had seen a great deal of the inner workings of the institution, she had found that they always did their utmost to give satisfaction to the Guardians and to the ratepayers and had shown every kindness to the inmates and to their fellow officials. The inmates notonly looked up to Mr fivbery San master but loved him because tley felt he bad done his best for them and had shown them every consideration.,_. .</p>
        <p>The CLERK (Mr A Bottomley) said he was very sorry indeed at the parting. He could truly say that the Board was losing the services of two excellent officials. Amicable relationships had existed between himself and all the officers of the Union including Mr and Mrs Awbery, and to everyone of them the resignation of the master and matron would mean a great wrench. Mr B SHEARER also added a few words on behalf of the relieving officers.</p>
        <p>The resign"ation was then accepted. The CLERK said the positions of master and matron were a joint appointment and when the master resigned the matron lost her appointment. The question of superannuation allowance was referred to the Finance Committee, while the House Committee was authorized to take preliminary steps for filling the vacancies. _ A GENEROUS ACT. __ .</p>
        <p>The CLERK then rea-d-tl7e-feilo;vlni letter from Mr Awbery :—" As you will be well aware, my resignation under medical certificate of the office of master entitles me to a superannuation allowance. The amount is not a large one but as I am not without private means, I wish as an exprriment to arrange with you, if you will consent to act, that this allowance be paid by you into a separate banking account that I will open at the National Provincial Bank, and that the said allowance shall be devoted to assisting deserving cases that may be recommended to you by a Guardian, a Relieving Officer or the Master of the Workhouse but which cannot be legally assisted from the rates. The Brabazon Society endeavour to do their best for the deserving in-door poor, but owing to inadequacy of funds, their sphere of usefulness is curtailed and cannot provide for all contingencies of distress whether inside or out. I personally know too well the need for such a fund. Deserving old men and women requiring pedlars' licences, the requisite starting stock, something to sustain them over the first few days until they begin to realize on their goods. Deserving cases leaving the Workhouse in earnest search of work, witn no friends, nowhere to rest at night and no means to relieve their hunger during their search. Families going out as destitute as when they came in, to start household life over again. Boys and girls going out into service—start them with a Post Office deposit of a few shillings. In outside cases payment of rent would perhaps keep together a household passing through a period of distress and obviate the whole household becoming chargeable to the rates in one form or another. Redeeming tools that were pledged in the time of poverty, a gift of coal—and in numerous other ways could such a fund be of great assistance both to Guardians and officers in arresting distress before it becomes too acute or hopeless. Tbe fund would apply to the Union area. There is no stipulation neces- ary ; you can aid to any extent, or for whatever purpose you deem expedient or desirable. Of course, I am assuming you will consent to administer this fund for me. Arrangements can be entered into as to details later on. May the operations of the fund brighten some life that, finds itself in need of brightness.—Yours sincerely, J. F. AWBREY." The CHAIRMAN said they had seen many acts of kindness on Mr. Awbery's part, but this latest offer was over and above anything they could have imagined. There had been many benefactions from people in Warrington and neighbourhood, but for Mr Awbery during his life-time to devote what belonged to him to such a noble object waa almost beyond description. They accepted the offer with the deepest thanks and he (the Chairman) would suggest that the fund be named "The Awbery Benevolent Fund." (Hear, hear). Miss BROADBENT said they all felt deeply touched by that beautiful action of the mas`er. The first time she saw Mr Awbery after his long illness he said that what he had been thinking of more than anything else whilst lying there, was how sorry he felt for the poor people who could not afford proper medical attention. DR. TAYLOR RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>The CLERIC then reed a letter from "Dr J Taylor resident medical officer, resigning his appointment and asking to be allowcd to relinguish duly one month hones. The CHAIRMAN said he would !tot be divulging any secret when he said that Mr. Awbrey had appointed Dr. Taylor his medical man and was going to take him into his private life. He moved that the resignation he accepted with regret, for a better medical cfficer they cou'd not have. (Hear, hear). Mr BRNT seconded and the motion was agreed to. Tas question of the appointment of a successor was , referred to t'ae House Committe.</p>
      </div>
