      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>[CE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</head>
        <p>[CE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Miss L. F. WRING, sends a letter (which reached us too late last evening for publication) in which she draws attention to the resolution of the Executive Corn mittee of the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies, expressing deep indignation at the wilful distruction of property by the militants. The resolu- tion also urges the claims of the Conciliation Bill.— ED. "EX."</p>
        <p>The Industrial Upheaval. Sir,—Our British Protectionists, true to their selfish opportunist principles, are crying out that they alone can supply the remedy for industrial discontent. Lest we forget that Protection is proved to be no remedy, may I beg you to draw attention to the present unrest in Protectionist cuuntries ? If our newspapers were not so full of our own troubles, the state of affairs abroad would be prominent on their principal pages. But amid so much grave news at home we have to IoA closely in the Tariff Reform press for such paragraphs as these : " A strike involving 220,000 miners is to begin in the German Westphalian' coalfields to-day. The day has also been fixed for a general stoppage of twenty-four hours by the French miners. About 175,000 American miners are threatening an almost immediate strike, and a fourth appears imminent in Austria."—" Daily Mail," March 11th. "There seemed to be this state of uncertainty everywhere. There were grave troubles in the textile industries cf the United States, and threatened trouble in the coal industry there. There had been troubles with respect to the Argentine railways, and on the Continent the fear of the great political volcano bursting into fire at any moment."—From the speech of the Deputy-Chair- man of the International Investment Trust, Ltd. ("Morning Post," February 20th). Under the heading "Strike fever in Germany," this from the Berlin correspondent of the " Daily Mail" (March sth) : "The strike fever is epidemic throughout Germany. In addition to 'a strike of German coalminers . . . . a national strike in the clothing trade is imminent. Several thousand men in the Schicbau Dockyard, Danzig, are already on strike, and 2,000 men and women employed in the paper box-making industry in Berlin threaten to come out if certain of their demands are not granted." This is a bare summary of news that is filling the newspapers of our chief Protectionist rivals. It would be possible to supply you with many pages of it. "The Times " was quite right when it said on January 22nd, "the prevalence of industrial unrest is a general phenomenon.—Yours very truly, WM. E. DOWDING,</p>
        <p>(EditorT" The Free Trader.")</p>
        <p>Letters to the . iitkt EDITOR.: • Non</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Evelyn-street School Headmastership.</head>
        <p>Evelyn-street School Headmastership.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am of opinion that most of your readers will construe Mr. Parker's reply to Mr. Upson, appearing in your issue of March 2nd, as somewhat of a paradox. Ile says " I have formed a high estimate of the War- rington teachers," also he says "Mr. Upson praises, deservedly (note the qualifying adverb) their merits and qualifications," then almost with the next stroke of his pen he says "the Warrington teachers want stirring up, they have got becalmed in a sluggish backwater' (which means, if I can read the metaphor correctly, they have got into a stereotyped grove of incompetence.) He adds that 'the teaching capacity of the Warrington staff is lowered by the departure of the most capable in search of better pay," that "in order to stop this dry rot new blood from outside must be infused " and that this dreadful state of things is brought about by means of the move up principle, which I will deal with below. I must first see whether Mr. Parker's deductions are correct. He says the loss of Warrington teachers is common," that is fre- quent; then again he says "a change of staff emanat- ing from the managers would be a nine days wonder; only in the rare (note the adjective), case of a teacher making a move off his own bat is a vacancy caused." A drain of teachers of some kind evidently takes place but whether it is a drain of the pure element or some inferior stuff I shall be able to prove later on. You see, sir, I have no sound data to work from. It cannot be a copious drain because of that " unhealthy contentment and readiness to remain in the same groove year after year that exists amongst the War- rington teachers" and so ably expounded by Mr. Parker ! Ergo, it must be a rare drain and being rare, it cannot possibly account for Mr. Parker's water-logged condition of the Warrington staff by the departure of the best, leaving only the inferior to be acted upon by the " move up ' principle Q.E.D. What then is the cause of this &amp;generate state in some of the Warrirgton schools of which Mr. Parker complains? I say emphatically that it does not exist and refutation is unnecessary, otherwise it would be found that Mr. Parker had attacked the head teachers as well as their staffs, for the headmasters are alor,e responsible for the efficient working of school routine. The would-be applicants from the large army of incompetent teachers, that are to be found all over the kingdom, many of them holding high university degrees, are constantly on the prowl for such tit-bits as the headmaster ship now in question. They rely on their high degrees to dazzle the eyes of the non-initiated on electing com- mittees, knowing that their ineptitude as teachers cannot be got at until too late. There is no supervision of head teachers;( they can only be judged by the standards of efficiency or inefficiency of the schools they direct. Why do the Education authorities in most of the large towns adopt the move up' principle? Because they have found it the most certain plan for securing capable head teachers—and not highly degreed automata; because promotion of the fit to the exclusion of the unfit, is the best stimulant for their junior staffs; because they are aware that there is a considerable number of incompetent teachers in the country. They know that they can only get at the Academic qualification of outsiders, and that all other qualifications, the most important too, must be taken very much on trust Consequehtly they elect to deal with the men they have trained and proved rather than make a haphazard selection from an unknown quantity of both good and bad teachers. This code of ethics in school manage- ment does not commend itself to a certain section of the Education Committee, they condemn it 49 " a had example," as a means of "following the multitude to an evil," " as commercially and educationally unsound and a mistake." The 'move up' principle by merit only, is in my opinion, the grandest conceivable; it enables the competent to eontro' the incompetent. Take the promotion of officers in the army as an object lesson. In the case of incompetent officers, of whom unfortun- ately there are too many, look up their antecedents and it will be found that they have gained their promotion by the very means touched un by Mr. Upson in his letters to the " Examiner"; the move</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>over the heads of better men is the principle adopted rather than the move up of the capable. Have a competent leader and the machinery of a profession or business will go like clock work. The hmdmaster is the guiding star of his staff, they note his power of imparting the knowledge by lucid explanation, by his tact,by the force with which he rivets the attention of a class from the beginning to the end of a lesson, checking any tendency to flag by a touch of humour ; they note his intuitive understanding of the small mind and how he leads it step by step until it grasps with delight the point aimed at; they adopt his methods and themselves become experts. A teacher is born not made, the latent talent simply requires develop- ment to bring it forth into splendid action. Teaching is a'practical art. No amount of erudition, no degree however brilliant, guarantees its possession. A head master is constantly on the qui tire for innate talent in teaching,he knows that if he is to maintain a standard of excellency in Ma school, he must have an efficient staff to aid him. Will then anyone dare to say that he promotes or recommends for promotion the inept? Does he not, t ather by all the force he can bring to bear, endeavour to keep on his staff those teachers who show aptitude. Mr. Parker is fearful that in the event of important Warrington posts being filled by Warrington lads (please note that the expression is not mine) to the exclusion of the better men from outside, disastrous consequences will ensue. Will Mr. Parker please note, that the Warrington teaching waif is not made up wholly of Warrington lads, as he has been pleased to term them. Many gentlemen have come in from outside areas, have been tried and found, from the fact of their still being here, to be of the right calibre. The inept have been tried, found wanting, and have gone to swell the great army of incompetent teachers from whom and others unknown Mr. Parker recommends a selection without allowing bow it is to be done. As to the family coach idea, well, it is Utopian. Why, it is asked, do the Warrington teachers fear open competition? I think it will be found that they reason in this way: We do not fear outside competition on fair and open grounds. Our academic credentials,our teaching capacity, our general aptitude and conduct, all are laid bare for criticism ; whereas in the case of outsiders, only the bright side, viz., the academic credentials, .is brought into competition. All other qualifications are hidden in mystery and uncertainty. We are told by one who knows and whose judgment cannot be gainsaid in matters of this kind that there is material in cur area of the best and of sufficient quality and quantity'; then why extend the area? Our employers have given us the best of leaders and we have adopted all their methods. Surely then we may look to those whom we have served so long and faith- fully for promotion. If our friends throw us over who will then take up our case? I contend that this is the way the Warrington teachers reason on this point.— Yours a‘:c C. COOKE. Friars' Green House,</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Mr. Parker and the Cycle Trade_</head>
        <p>Mr. Parker and the Cycle Trade_</p>
        <p>Sir,—lf I interpret Mr. Parker's kind reply to my letter on cycle exports correctly, he accepts my state- ment, but he still considers Free Trade has benefited the manufacturers by compelling them to adopt every possible means to economise and improve their methods to the finest point. This is probably correct, and Ido not wish to criticise this assumption, but I ought to inform Mr. Parker that two features have followed in the wake of their improved methods. Firstly, the wages of the men are now considerably less, and the number of men employed in the trade has been reduced by almost half. I am of course, referring to the periods discu,sed by Mr. Parker in his original letter. I should like to thank Mr. Parker for his courteous request that we should examine together the trades I consider have been ruined by Free Trade, but I cannot see what good purpose can be served. I have a feel- ing in my mind that the question of Tariff Reform or Free Trade can never be settled to the best advantage of the many, until the workingmen have an oppor- tunity of studying its effect on their own individual trade, without having their vision obscured by party politics.—Yours faithfully, W. H. BELLIAN, The Spinney, Grappenhall.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am glad your correspondent " The Whole Truth " has brought motor cars to the front. The record of this trade is interesting and goes far to con- firm the conclusions demonstrated in my letter on the cycle trade. The imports of motor cars reached their highest in 1906, they rapidly went down until 1909, during the last two years there has been a moderate increase probably due to the great increase of taxicabs, which, coupled with the demand for private cars has more than ovPrtaken the capacity of English motor car makers. The figures for 1906 and last year in imports and exports stand as follows Net Imports. Exports. 19C6 2,174,034 495,399 1911 1,109,414 1,805,025 Decrease 731,620 Increase 1,309,02(1 704,6:0</p>
        <p>British Trade Gain .£2,071,24G</p>
        <p>Taking the whole of the groups as desired by your correspondent, and including motors, cycles, and parts, is e have the following figures Net Imports Exports. .0 .0 190 G 4,166,889 2,009,617 1911 5,621,0?5 5,568,763</p>
        <p>Increase 1,454,136 Increase 3,559,146 1,454,136 --- British Trade Gain £2,105,010</p>
        <p>I suppose this is what your correspondent refers to when he speaks of our being " pulverised by foreign countries.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that continental makers, especially the French, were first to the front as maLn- facturers of motor cars. Reasons for this were altogether outside the fiscal question and the sooner Britishers give some credit to foreigners for having brains the better. Tariff Reformers appear only to credit foreigners with intelligence when they shackle themselves with tariffs. Foreigners frequently show a greater alertness in the developing of a new industry than we do. In a former letter I showed how the Americans for a time beat us in boot manufacturing by the development of more efficient machinery. Germany seized the commercial possibility in the development of the colour industry through the English discovery of aniline dyes. We lost this trade not for the lack of tariffs but want of alertness. France stole a march upon us both in the making of motors and aeroplanes. There is however a characteristic feature about the British that while at times we may be beaten for a time by the smartness of competitors, we have determination and staying power, and in the long run come out on top. ' This has already been!shown both in the boot manu- facturing and cycle trades and is in process of being shown in the motor car industry as above figures indicate. Free Trade—or free imports if the term is preferable—helps every trade and supplies the raw material at the lowest world price not alone for manu- facturing, but for consumption as well. I cannot appreciate the point of view of either your</p>
        <p>correspondent `• The Whole Truth, " or that of the President of the Dumping Exhibition in regard to clocks. They both select particular countries from which we get our largest imports of the article in ques- tion, then they deduct our exports of the same things to these pat ticular countries. Cannot the whole truth be better apprehended by taking not specially selected countries or even trades, but our own trade as a whole ? Surely it would be folly for our manufac- turers to expect to develop a large export trade to France in motors, to Spain in grapes, to Switzerland in watches, or to the Canaries in bananas. Trade naturally follows the lines of least resistance, and our best opportunity of selling motors is in those countries that have not developed the ',manufacture of motor cors to the extent we have.</p>
        <p>CHARLES E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, March 14, 1912.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March, 16, 1912.</p>
        <p>in the hands of the capitalist. The factory system was as deplorable, if not more deplorable, than the old feudal system it had replaced, and what they must secure was that the whole community should be masters in their own house.</p>
      </div>
