      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Coat Strikes</head>
        <p>Coat Strikes</p>
        <p>NEGOTIATIONS FAIL.</p>
        <p>GOVERNMENT TO INTRODUCE A BILL.</p>
        <p>The owners and miners held separate conferences at the Foreign Office yesterday, the Prime Minister, the Chancellor of the Exchequer and other members of the Government also being in attendance. When the owners left the Foreign Office shortly after one o'clock Sir Hugh Bell took a very lugubrious view of the position. "We are not getting on at all," he said, "Nothing has been done; we are just waiting."</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick Hall, M P., chief agent for the York- shire miners, said, yes.erday afternoon, "We are no nearer now than we were a fortnight ago." Distress grows hourly. Thousands will be added to the ranks of the unemployed this week. The tail- ways are making further reductions in their services, and industry is suffering an incteasingly heavy blow, Already relief funds are being opened in the great induArial areas, where many are faced with starvation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SENSATION.</head>
        <p>A SENSATION.</p>
        <p>Late last evening an intimation came through the press agencies that: Negotiations have been broken off. Government Bill will be introduced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>OUTLOOK IN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE OUTLOOK IN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The groups of unemployedjworkmen standing about the streets of Wariington are one indication of the paralysing effect which the strike has already ex er- cised on ur leading industries. About 6,000 hands have been thrown idle, and every day that passes without a settlement intensifies the blackness cf the outlook. Not only are firms rapidly getting through their accumulated reserves of fuel, but the dislocation of the railway tranqpn t supplies is making it increas- ingly difficult to receive raw materials and to dispatch finished goods. Generally speaking, the situation at the principal works has not undergone any material change since last week-end. At the works of the Pearsin and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., two forges have been opened and an effort has been made to raiminise the effects of the strike by employing batches of men in relays. Still there are about 1,300 of the employes of this firm on the unemployed list. At Messrs. Rylands Br,A. Ltd., Church-street between 600 and 700 men remain out of work, but it is not anticipated that, any more will have to be dispensed with for another week at any rate. Messrs. .Monks, Hall and Company Limited have this week closed down their rivet works, in addition to the forge, mills and part of the tube works, the total number of workpeople involved being about 000. It is the intention of the firm to</p>
        <p>OPEN A sour krrcitEN for employees who are beginning to feel the pinch of poverty, and the arrangements were being rapidly pushed forward yesterday, Sankey Wire Mills are practically at a standstill, entailing temporary les of work to 350 employees. The Firth Company have decided to close the greater portion of their works to-day, the number of hands affected being 200. A notice has been posted up at Cockhedge Factory placing their 1,800 cotton operatives on daily contract, but it has not yet become necessary to suspend work in any of the departments. Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, are still keeping practically the whole of their workpeople employed. A number of men were suspended yesterday but this was not due to strike influences but to the fact that repairs had to be carried out. Messrs. R. A. Naylor, Ltd., Howley, do • not use coal for power purposes and, up to the present the strike has not affected them very appreciably. As a precautionary measure hoWever, the hours of work have been slightly curtailed. The St. Helens Cable to„ Ltd., are working as usual but, unless the strike is settled, a partial stoppage will take place about the middle of next week. Messrs. N. Greening and Co., Ltd., Messrs Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., are not yet affected by the strike.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Electric Power.—Probable Curtailment.</head>
        <p>Electric Power.—Probable Curtailment.</p>
        <p>Inquiries at the Corporation electricity works last evening revealed the fact that the outlook is distinctly disquieting to local traders. The stock of fuel is rapidly running down and the committee is meeting practically every day to consider the position of affairs. It is probable that the supply of electricity for power purposes will have to be curtaiied early next week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Stoppage of Cars.</head>
        <p>Stoppage of Cars.</p>
        <p>A stoppage of the. tr-amway S-e-r-vTCe is imminent, unless some unlooked-for change in the outlook takes place. The committee met yesterday, and after con- sidering the state of affairs, decided that notices should be placed in the cars warning the public to be prepared for a suspension of service on all routes on Monday night. A further notice will be issued on Monday morning. A further supply of 150 tons of coal was obtained this week, or it would have become necessary for the cars to cease running to-night. The Supplies at the Workhouse. At a meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednes- day, it was stated that several firms had declined to tender to supply coal until the strike was over. The question of supplies would therefore have to he postponed. I Mr Houghton: In the meantime, what is to be done? I see there is one Workhouse without coal already. The Clerk : Yes, sir, but that is not Warrington. At the Cottage Homes we have coal to last two or three weeks, and at the Workhouse we have house coal that will last at least six weeks. We have also a promise from the contractor to supply seven trucks of slack, so we have not felt the pinch yet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Relieving Distress.—A Town's Meeting</head>
        <p>Relieving Distress.—A Town's Meeting</p>
        <p>The Mayor has convened a meeting of " all persons interested ' at the Town Hall, on Monday next, at 330 to consider if any, and what steps should be taken to relieve the distress caused by the coal strike.</p>
        <p>Guild of Help Executive and the Strike. The Executive Committee of the Guild of help met on Monday last, when the Mayor was present. Tbe local situation created by the strike was discussed, and it was generally felt that if no settlement were reached by the end of the week, acute distress would soon become manifest. It was agreed that any proposal to start a Dtstrese Fund should only come frorn the Mayor, who would call a meeting when he thought it necessary. The Guild Executive also expressed its willingness to meet in conference others engaged in charitable and philanthropic work in the t)wn in order to discuss measures for dealing with the situation. A small Emergency Committee was appointed consisting of Messrs C E Parker, J A Ransom°, 13 S Townroe and W J Rowland.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>REV PREBENDARY WAKEFORD ON THE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>REV PREBENDARY WAKEFORD ON THE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Prebendary Wakeford, of St. hiargarets Anfleld, speaking at a meeting for men at the Parish Church last night on " Power and Government "said that no doubt the troubles of the present time were in no mean part due to the stupid blindness of our rulers at the time when the development of machinery cut off the workman from his product and made him a chattel</p>
        <p>f. t</p>
        <p>r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>RIOTOUS SCENES NEAR EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>RIOTOUS SCENES NEAR EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>Three Constables Injured. Serious rioting took place near Earlestown on Wednesday, as a result of which three police constables were taken to hospital for treatment. The scene of the outbreak was the Wood Pit just outside the town, in Haydock district. The colliery is the property of Richard Evans and Co., and the immediate cause of the trouble was the removal Of coal from the stacks. For some dip past miners and others are alleged to have been carrying away quantities of coal, and the preven- tion of this by the police has led to bad feeling. This has been accentuated by the fact that train loads of coal have been removed from the pit since the strike began, an ect which is strongly resented by the local miners, whose supplies are giving out, and who are linable to obtain fuel except at exorbitant prices. The Haydock people themselves have not interfered, but Earlestown and Ashton are contributing the rowdy element. On Tuesday evening the engine conveying the waggons from Wood Pit along the colliery line which runs through the fields between Hayduck and Earlestown was surrounded, and an effort was made to upset the whole train. The local police were completely outnumbered at the lime, and reinforcements were burr:el up from Earlestown and Wigin. The matter passed off without any great damsge being done, but the police authorities, fearing that the trouble would become more acute, drafted in constables from as far distant as Lytham and St. Anne's. " Riot to begin at three prompt !" was the notice chalked on the Earlestown railway bridge on Wednes- day morning, and during the day the local militants were joined by contingents from Wigan and St. Helens. Towards dinner time a move was made up the colliery line and through the fields, and on reaching the colliery sidings the mob stopped the men who were working on the coal and slack waggons in the siding, and went along throwing things about until they reached the Princess Pit. Those in charge of the men working on the waggons had been warned by a telephone message from Wood Pit, and when the mob arrived there no work was being done They contented themselves with overrunning the place throwing colliery coalboxes or waggons about, break- ing shovels and picks, and throwiog some of them on the roof of the buildings. A move was then made back to the colliery sidings, where the police attempted to prevent the mob from going on to the colliery railway. A lively scuffle took place, but the mob was driven away from the ollicds and foundry telonging to the firm. They made their way, honever, through the fields to the Wood Pit again, By two o'clock the crowd at the Wood Pit numbered fully I,COO, and the ringlearl.rs were very threatening. A good deal of stone-throning went on for some time. The bulk of the people were on the road which leads from Earlue own to Hay dock close by the colliery, and several hundreds took up positions in the fields round about. Soon after two o'clock those in the field on the Earlestonn side succeeded in climbing the high "stuff rook," or waste heap, from which point of vantage they were able to throw their missiles at the engine- house and lamproom. Inspector Lamb and his men charged the waste heap but were met by heavy showers of bricks and large pieces of slag, Bo that they had to fall back. Daring this movement three officers were struck by heavy missiles and knocked down. Constable Rostron, of St. Armes, who was close by Inspector Lamb, had his head badly cut, and was found to be suffering from slight concussion, while Constable Mullin, of Earlestown, and Constable Thornley, of Haydock were alsa put out of action with head injuries, and h,el to be removed subsequently to hospital. Meanwhile the fight sent on, and when the police began to surround the waste heap the valiant ones fled into the road and over the hedges into the fields. The police now got to work systeu atically, and drove the crowd before them down into Crow-lane, Newton-le-Willows, while others scattered over the fields, returning to their homes at Earlestown and Ashton. It is stated by some that the Polish colliers who are employed by the firm to the number of about 300 are the ringleaders in the matter, but this does not appear correct. The rowdy element undoubtedly.. hails from Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>COt‘LOWNERS APPEASE THE MEN.</head>
        <p>COt‘LOWNERS APPEASE THE MEN.</p>
        <p>The appearance in flaydock on Thursday of a strong force of police and an agreement and explanation by the rcanaAecuent of Messrs. it Evans and Co. as to the con- ditions under which coal might be filled, had the effect of preventing a repetition of the previous day's disorder.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>STRIKE ITEMS AND INCIDENTS.</head>
        <p>STRIKE ITEMS AND INCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>FROM FAR AND NEAR.</p>
        <p>&lt;£30,000 Strike Pay in Lancashire.</p>
        <p>Arrangements for issuing strike pay-“his week-end are heiug made by the Lancashire and Cheshire Miners' Federation. Each man receives lOs• and Is for each child under 14. The Fedeiation will thus Fay out from £25,000 to £30,000 a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>Pity for Coal Contractor.</head>
        <p>Pity for Coal Contractor.</p>
        <p>Nlaidenhead Guardians are asking p3rmission of the Local Government Board to pay their coal contractor an extra price beyond that at which he tendered. He contracted to supply "good house coal "at 224 lid a ton, and there is no strike clause in his contract. Coal is now 354 a ton, and the contractor stands to lose very heavily. New Line of Business. Workmen on a building site at Gleadlcss, Sheffield, discovered coal when digging the foundations for the house. They abandoned their original work and set up as coal miners and merchants.</p>
        <p>Price of Cheese Reduced. At the Chester cheese fair on Wednesday there was a big fall in prices owing to the strike and the lessened demand. Fine Cheshire was quoted at Vs. a cwt.tas against 813. last month. Free Concerts. Free concerts are being arranged in Manchester and the surrounding districts for the benefit of the colliers on strike.</p>
        <p>Coal from the States. A Baltimore Press correspondent writing on the 2nd inst., says the Consolidated Coal Company is loading two steamships with coal for shipment to Great Britain. Each of the steamers will carry 6,000 tons. The first will clear to-day, to bo followed by the other on Tuesday. The Atlas Coal and Coke Company, of Pennsylvania, is also loading two steamers with coal. Leigh's Lucky Position. At Leigh the rnilla and various ironworks having taken precautions before the strike, will be able, it is said, to hold out for many weeks yet. Not a single mill or workshop has become idle. Even in the families of miners there is not much distress, as usually there are some members working in the mills. Colliery pro- prietors have allowed the poor people to search for coal in the dirt heaps, and one Ectiool had to close one thy as most of the children were out "picking "coal. Leisure Spent in Garden and Library. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- . Outside Cardiff ani Svanses there is not yet much evidence of distress in South Wales. The miners in all the valleys are quietly disposed and optimistic as to the result. They take full advantage of their enforced idleness to dig their gardens and allotments on the hillsides, whilst odd jobs around their homes occupy a good number. Another noticeable and satisfactory feature of the stoppage is the extensive patronage of the libraries. Miners' New Employment</p>
        <p>. _ Between thirty and forty miners who are members of the Church of England Men's Society at the colliery village of Huthwaite, near Mansfield, are employing their leisure-time levelling a site for a new vicarage and carrying out other improvements' including the planting of a large number of shrubs. The work, which is being carried out in shifts, i 3 being done gratis. Lost Railway Traffic. _ _</p>
        <p>_ A list of traffic returns shows the enormous extent of the strike's effect on the receipts of the various com- panies. All the heavy lines report figures thonsands of Pounds below those of the corresponding week of last year. Below we give a list of companies showing the decline in revenue last week compared with a year ago : Company Decline Company Decline North-Eastern £82.591 L and Y £10,999 Midland 73,000 South-Western ... 8.800 Land N-W .. 67,000 Glasgow &amp; S-W... 8,800 Gt Western North British Gt Central 34,200 North Staffs 6,690 Gt Northern Caledonian 30,700 Cambrian Even smaller companies, and London suburban lines show a fall in traffic. _</p>
        <p>These figures, showing a total of approximately half- a-million, do not, of course, represent actual loss to the companies, or even loss of national trade as great economies have been made on all the lines. A Welsh Vicar's Amazing Speech. A Rhondda vicar delivered a sensational speech on Tuesday, on the coal strike. He is the Rev Meredith Morris, M.A., Vicar of Clydach Vale, and the address was to several hundreds of colliers. He said there were , thousands upon thousands connected with different trades and professions up and down the country who did not get a living wage. Capitalists were utterly selfish yes, devilishly selfish. There was enough wealth in Cardiff to buy up Glamorgan, yet they could not keep up an Infirmary without asking assistance from the working classes. Within a stone's throw of the Infirmary there lived ten millionaires, and he (the speaker) said they were selfish devils. The Infirmary authorities were going begging around the country while these ruilliotairea were wallowing in wealth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>STILL TICKING!</head>
        <p>STILL TICKING!</p>
        <p>THE FAMOUS FOREIGN CLOCKS.</p>
        <p>LADY GREENALL BOLDLY TAKES</p>
        <p>THE FENCE.</p>
        <p>The silence is broken at last, and Lady Greenall admits in the suitable columns of our Tariff Reform contemporary, that the clodks she gave as prizes at the Dumping Exhibition were foreign clocks. It needs nerve to make an admission so damaging to one's political professions as this is, but we will say for Her Ladyship that she is not prone to jib at the fence. Once safely over, and with no more damage than a rather bad tear in the cloak of consistency, Lady Greenall proceeds to tell us what she thinks of us for putting so awkward a fence in the middle of such apparently smiling country. We are to blame, of course! Our Free Imports policy has killed the English trade in this class of clock," and "compelled" our manufacturers to buy from the foreigner. Here the lady-president of the Dumping Exhibition appears in a new and surprising light. We had not thought, hitherto, that Lady Greenall would have been "compelled" to do anything she was not inclined to, least of all to buy foreign clocks made by foreign workingmen,as prizes for British workers in a" Britain for the British " exhibition. The lady waxes quite indignant with us and exclaims that "they date to censure me for doing what their own policy has compelled us to do." But we humbly ask, why com- pelled ? The Census of Production reveals the fact that our watch and clock industry, the death of which Lady Greenall deplores so bitterly, is a tolerably lively corpse, and could have supplied Lady Greenall with as many home-made clocks as she desired. But why clocks at all, if there were any difficulty in getting clocks of home manufac- ture ? We are the largest manufacturers in the world of articles suitable for such prizes, and it is ridiculous to say that there was any compulsion to buy foreign clocks. Person- ally, we do not object to buying foreign clocks if these are better value than the home made time-keepers, knowing that foreigners take in exchange far more of our manu- (!actured goods than we take from them, but we certainly expect some consistency in the President of a Dumping Exhibition designed to discourage foreign imports. But the mystery thickens when Lady Greenall tells us that the wicked foreigner "having killed our industry now charges his own price." Tariff Reformers not only buy foreign made clocks for exhibition prizes, but they pay the foreigner a price fixed by him- self, which we are left to infer is above what it ought to be. Inasmuch as the average price of imported clocks in 1910 was only 3s. sd. each it does not appear that the foreigner has been very extortionate. If we remember aright we were counselled at the exhibition' to trample on the foreigner, but the President of the exhibition,metaphorically speaking, and on her own showing, allows herself to be "compelled " to lie down and invite the foreigner to walk over her. Such meekness is " foreign " to her ladyship's usual attitude,and we can only presume that it was due to expediency—that frequent betrayer of Tariff Reform consistency! Meanwhile, we hope the lesson will not be lost to the numerous class, of people who agitate for Tariff Reform and the protection of home industries with one breath, and with the next give generous orders for American boots, French motor cars, and Swiss clocks !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF BILLIARD PRIZES.</p>
        <p>A flying billiard handicap took place at the Liberal Club on Wednesday evening. The games were con- tested in excellent spirit, and some of them proved very exciting. The successful competitors were : 1, W Owen; 2, G Baker; 3, E V Walker; 4, W Langley. At the close Alderman Tinnion presented the prizes, and in the course of a few remarks, said the way the games had been played, contributed very largely to the pleasure of the evening. Credit was due to the committee for the way they continually catered for the social Fart of the club, and they were always pleased to welcome suggestions from the club members At the close of his remarks he presented prizes to the winner of the Christmas handicap as follows : I,R Woods; 2, C Jordan, portmanteau; 3, W Langley, set of bowls: 4, E A Sanders, gold pendant; 5, E V Walker, ladies ear rings; 6, W Mumby, silver sardine dish ; 7, PH Brown, clock ; 8, E Lloyd, pictures ; 9, Halliwell, toast rack; 10, R Wilson, match box; 11, J Collins, biscuit barrel; 12, A Robertson, gold tooth pick ; 13, E Fowler, boots; 14, ri Gorst, sugarettee ; 15, A Crowe, an order; 16, T Locker, brooch. Amongst those present were :—Councillors J T Locker, a,..d J Halliwell, Messrs Joseph Wilkinson, F'irth Lindley, E V Walker, W Pickup, W Hayes, G 11 Brown, F Plumpton, H New and E A Sanders.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>ACCIDENT TO A MAGISTRATE.</head>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO A MAGISTRATE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Baxter, J.P , of "The Rock," Hill Cliff°, a prominent agriculturist and member of the Runcorn Rural Council; met with a serious accident at the Wilderspool Crossings on Wednesday afternoon, while out driving with his son, Mr Joseph Baxter, of Preston Brook. The son was in charge of the horse and trap and on approaching the crossings the animal was startled by the sound of a passing tramcar's gong. The vehicle caught a lamp post, and both occupants were thrown violently to the pavemeht. Mr Baxter, sen , was picked up in an unconscious conditi n. Dr. Peacock was summoned, and upon examination it was found Mr Baxter had fractured his skull. After being attended to he was conveyed home. His son sustained bruises to the face 9nd head. The horse liberated itself and galloped for about 100 yards before it was brought to standstill. _ _</p>
        <p>La—st night we learned that Mr. Baxter was progress- ing as favourably as could be expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>DAY OF INTERCESSION. Church and Free Churches to Unite on Sunday.</head>
        <p>DAY OF INTERCESSION. Church and Free Churches to Unite on Sunday.</p>
        <p>To-morrow (Sunday) is to be observed, both by the Church of England and the Free Churches, as a day for humble intercession and prayer in view of the present national unrest, and especially of the far-reaching effects of the coal strike. The Archbishop of Canterbury has informed the National Free Church Council that the srchbidiops and bishops are suggesting that Sunday should be observed in this way. In addition the Archbishops of Canterbury and York have sent a notice to all the diocesan bishops in England end Wales recommending next Sunday as a special day of prayer in view of the national crisis. The Rev. F. B. Meyer, hon. secretary of the Free Church Council, has accordingly addressed a letter, I signed by himself and tha president, to Free Church ministers and leaders. In this they say : "We feel that the Free Churches would desire to be associated in this movement thus making It compre- hensive of national religious feeling. We suggest, therefore, that at all the services next Sunday very special reference be made; both in exhortation and prayer, to the dark Litadow which is resting upon the national life at the present hour. Where practicable, special meetings for united prayer might be arranged in the following week, "That the entire Christian sentiment of the country should thus express its essential unity is of priceless importance and value."</p>
        <p>Works Fatality. Stephen Barnes, aged 9, of 2, Friars Lane, died in the Infirmary at 9 o'clock last night as the result of an accident at the works of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield &amp; Sons, Ltd. He was caught M some shafting, sustain- ing injuries to his head and breaking his leg.</p>
        <p>3*</p>
        <p>MARKET PRICES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON (WEDNESDAY). FRESH BOOS, 12 for Is. LARGE SOLES, 10 FRESH BUTTER. 1/5 S3IALL SOLES, 20 CHEESE, (Cheshire) 10d and SALMON TROUT, . LEmox SOLES, 10</p>
