      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1912-03-16_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WARRIMGTON AND DISTRICT CH</head>
        <p>WARRIMGTON AND DISTRICT CH</p>
        <p>ENDEAVOUR UNION.</p>
        <p>The annual Conventicn of the Christian Union -was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Saturday last. The afternoon sobsion took the a junior rally and there was a large gathering members. Three very good and instructive were given by the members of the Brick-st Green and B:sn't Quay Societies which appreciated, and a tea was afterwards sorved....., The evening meeting was opened by Rev the retiring president, who after a brief, announced that by the unanimous wish of the Mr Arthur Mountield had been invited to _</p>
        <p>office of President for the coming ye—a-r--. -- On taking the chair Mr. Mountield gave,, address on the past and present of the Endeavour movement in England, discussing 9,e for its decline and arguing that it wag now normal place in the churches. The first speaker was Rev. J. S. Chadbirfl a very interesting addres4 on " The uses of tion " He was followed by Mr. W. H. Starr ' whose address was listened to with deep rate The Secretary's report was read by Mr. and showed that much good work had been the year. The Union is admirably organised of usefulness is anticipated.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic _ "On Tramp in Dickens' Land." Substit, not as a rule popular, but Tuesday's lecture one of the few exceptions to prove it, for Air the hon. lantcrnist of the Everton</p>
        <p>entertained his large audience vritCo-L-e of t. this session. He took the place,at very short his colleague, Mr. G. T. Drysdale, and the tl the Society are due also to that gentleman promptness and the efficiency with which he unavoidable gap. Starting on foot last London two friends who know and love and their character followed practically C, walk to Margate. They visited and • Cobham, Gad's Hill, Rochester, Canterbury, and Margate, and retui steamer to London. The completeness Mackie's story may be judged from the c' the subjects of his pictures, for they incit originals (either probable or traditional), gravestones, Agnes Wickfield's house, til.e Uriah Heap's office, Bleak House, and even near Maidstone that gave Dickens the idea c bought by Pickwick and bearing the everl inscription "Bust urn pshi am ark." A vi Half way House between Dover and Canterbut a reminder that the Prince Consort was own; put up there for the night when he firt England,showing the changes in travelling 81! The lecturer was sincerely thanked by the air resolution coming from Mr. Wilkes) seconded by Mr. Rowlands.</p>
