        <p>following makes a ideal mixture and one which materially alters the tone cf the stock: Wheat, two parts; gocd sound oats, two parts ; white Canadian peas, one part; barley, one part. Add to the above a good sprinkling of buckwheat and hemp-seed, and you have a mixed food par excellence' GREEN FOOD. I have so often reiterated the necessity of green food in some form, that readers must well know that that fowls cannot thrive without it. On a free range the item can be dispensed with, but in confined areas it must be provided, and when cabbages ccic. are unobtainable you can have no better substitute than mangolds and turnips. Cat in halves and hung in the pen, they are greedily eaten and enjoyed by the Jowls. FEEDING FOR TABLE. This is another question altogether from feeding for laying purpose, and it can better be dealt with in a conclusive article, so I must ask you to wait awhile for this. I,LEmos 1)F INCUBATION. It may be of interest and also useful if I append herewith a table, giving the periods:of incubation for some of the different varieties of feathered stock. Hens Guinea Fowls 21 „ Pheasants ... 24 Partridges Ducks Geese</p>
